\rem source Masnavi i Ma'navi, the Spiritual Couplets of Maulana Jalalu-'d-Din Muhammad i Rumi, translated and abridged by E. H. Whinfield (London: Trubner, 1887). Text from sacred-texts.com's transcription (https://sacred-texts.com/isl/masnavi/, read from the Wayback Machine), which is labelled 1898 but is the 1887 first edition's text, corrected to the 1887 print by collation with two scans of it (archive.org cu31924026910251 and in.ernet.dli.2015.31701; masnavi_fixes.tsv). By book, story and section: [book, story, n], the stories as Whinfield numbers them ("prologue" and "epilogue" where he has them), a section running from one of his headings to the next. His story titles, headings and prose summaries are paratext; his introduction, footnotes, the page references to the Persian text, and the closing Note on Apocryphal Supplements are left out.
\rem rights Public domain (published 1887)
\h Masnavi
\toc1 Masnavi
\mt1 Masnavi
\c 1
\cp 1
\p
\v 1 \vp prologue.1\vp* HEARKEN to the reed-flute, how it complains,
\q1 Lamenting its banishment from its home:—
\q1 "Ever since they tore me from my osier bed,
\q1 My plaintive notes have moved men and women to tears.
\q1 I burst my breast, striving to give vent to sighs,
\q1 And to express the pangs of my yearning for my home.
\q1 He who abides far away from his home
\q1 Is ever longing for the day he shall return.
\q1 My wailing is heard in every throng,
\q1 In concert with them that rejoice and them that weep.
\q1 Each interprets my notes in harmony with his own feelings,
\q1 But not one fathoms the secrets of my heart.
\q1 My secrets are not alien from my plaintive notes,
\q1 Yet they are not manifest to the sensual eye and ear.
\q1 Body is not veiled from soul, neither soul from body,
\q1 Yet no man hath ever seen a soul."
\q1 This plaint of the flute is fire, not mere air.
\q1 Let him who lacks this fire be accounted dead!
\q1 'Tis the fire of love that inspires the flute,
\q1 'Tis the ferment of love that possesses the wine.
\q1 The flute is the confidant of all unhappy lovers;
\q1 Yea, its strains lay bare my inmost secrets.
\q1 Who hath seen a poison and an antidote like the flute?
\q1 Who hath seen a sympathetic consoler like the flute?
\q1 The flute tells the tale of love's bloodstained path,
\q1 It recounts the story of Majnun's love toils.
\q1 None is privy to these feelings save one distracted,
\q1 As ear inclines to the whispers of the tongue.
\q1 Through grief my days are as labour and sorrow,
\q1 My days move on, hand in hand with anguish.
\q1 Yet, though my days vanish thus, 'tis no matter,
\q1 Do thou abide, O Incomparable Pure One!
\q1 But all who are not fishes are soon tired of water;
\q1 And they who lack daily bread find the day very long;
\q1 So the "Raw" comprehend not the state of the "Ripe;"
\q1 Therefore it behoves me to shorten my discourse.
\q1 Arise, O son! burst thy bonds and be free!
\q1 How long wilt thou be captive to silver and gold?
\q1 Though thou pour the ocean into thy pitcher,
\q1 It can hold no more than one day's store.
\q1 The pitcher of the desire of the covetous never fills,
\q1 The oyster-shell fills not with pearls till it is content;
\q1 Only he whose garment is rent by the violence of love
\q1 Is wholly pure from covetousness and sin.
\q1 Hail to thee, then, O LOVE, sweet madness!
\q1 Thou who healest all our infirmities!
\q1 Who art the physician of our pride and self-conceit!
\q1 Who art our Plato and our Galen!
\q1 Love exalts our earthly bodies to heaven,
\q1 And makes the very hills to dance with joy!
\q1 O lover, 'twas love that gave life to Mount Sinai,
\q1 When "it quaked, and Moses fell down in a swoon."
\q1 Did my Beloved only touch me with his lips,
\q1 I too, like the flute, would burst out in melody.
\q1 But he who is parted from them that speak his tongue,
\q1 Though he possess a hundred voices, is perforce dumb.
\q1 When the rose has faded and the garden is withered,
\q1 The song of the nightingale is no longer to be heard.
\q1 The BELOVED is all in all, the lover only veils Him;
\q1 The BELOVED is all that lives, the lover a dead thing.
\q1 When the lover feels no longer LOVE's quickening,
\q1 He becomes like a bird who has lost its wings. Alas!
\q1 How can I retain my senses about me,
\q1 When the BELOVED shows not the light of His countenance?
\q1 LOVE desires that this secret should be revealed,
\q1 For if a mirror reflects not, of what use is it?
\q1 Knowest thou why thy mirror reflects not?
\q1 Because the rust has not been scoured from its face.
\q1 If it were purified from all rust and defilement,
\q1 It would reflect the shining of the SUN of GOD.
\q1 O friends, ye have now heard this tale,
\q1 Which sets forth the very essence of my case.
\v 2 \vp 1.1\vp* A true lover is proved such by his pain of heart;
\q1 No sickness is there like sickness of heart.
\q1 The lover's ailment is different from all ailments;
\q1 Love is the astrolabe of God's mysteries.
\q1 A lover may hanker after this love or that love,
\q1 But at the last he is drawn to the KING of love.
\q1 However much we describe and explain love,
\q1 When we fall in love we are ashamed of our words.
\q1 Explanation by the tongue makes most things clear,
\q1 But love unexplained is clearer.
\q1 When pen hasted to write,
\q1 On reaching the subject of love it split in twain.
\q1 When the discourse touched on the matter of love,
\q1 Pen was broken and paper torn.
\q1 In explaining it Reason sticks fast, as an ass in mire;
\q1 Naught but Love itself can explain love and lovers!
\q1 None but the sun can display the sun,
\q1 If you would see it displayed, turn not away from it.
\q1 Shadows, indeed, may indicate the sun's presence,
\q1 But only the sun displays the light of life.
\q1 Shadows induce slumber, like evening talks,
\q1 But when the sun arises the "moon is split asunder."
\q1 In the world there is naught so wondrous as the sun,
\q1 But the Sun of the soul sets not and has no yesterday.
\q1 Though the material sun is unique and single,
\q1 We can conceive similar suns like to it.
\q1 But the Sun of the soul, beyond this firmament,—
\q1 No like thereof is seen in concrete or abstract.
\q1 Where is there room in conception for His essence,
\q1 So that similitudes of HIM should be conceivable?
\v 3 \vp 1.2\vp* The sun (Shams) of Tabriz is a perfect light,
\q1 A sun, yea, one of the beams of God!
\q1 When the praise was heard of the "Sun of Tabriz,"
\q1 The sun of the fourth heaven bowed its head.
\q1 Now that I have mentioned his name, it is but right
\q1 To set forth some indications of his beneficence.
\q1 That precious Soul caught my skirt,
\q1 Smelling the perfume of the garment of Yusuf;
\q1 And said, "For the sake of our ancient friendship,
\q1 Tell forth a hint of those sweet states of ecstasy,
\q1 That earth and heaven may be rejoiced,
\q1 And also Reason and Spirit, a hundredfold."
\q1 I said, "O thou who art far from ' The Friend,'
\q1 Like a sick man who has strayed from his physician,
\q1 Importune me not, for I am beside myself;
\q1 My understanding is gone, I cannot sing praises.
\q1 Whatsoever one says, whose reason is thus astray,
\q1 Let him not boast; his efforts are useless.
\q1 Whatever he says is not to the point,
\q1 And is clearly inapt and wide of the mark.
\q1 What can I say when not a nerve of mine is sensible?
\q1 Can I explain 'The Friend' to one to whom He is no Friend?
\q1 Verily my singing His praise were dispraise,
\q1 For 'twould prove me existent, and existence is error.
\q1 Can I describe my separation and my bleeding heart?
\q1 Nay, put off this matter till another season."
\q1 He said, " Feed me, for I am an hungred,
\q1 And at once, for 'the time is a sharp sword.'
\q1 O comrade, the Sufi is 'the son of time present.'
\q1 It is not the rule of his canon to say, 'To-morrow.'
\q1 Can it be that thou art not a true Sufi?
\q1 Ready money is lost by giving credit."
\q1 I said, "'Tis best to veil the secrets of 'The Friend.'
\q1 So give good heed to the morals of these stories.
\q1 That is better than that the secrets of 'The Friend'
\q1 Should be noised abroad in the talk of strangers."
\q1 He said, "Without veil or covering or deception,
\q1 Speak out, and vex me not, O man of many words!
\q1 Strip off the veil and speak out, for do not I
\q1 Enter under the same coverlet as the Beloved?"
\q1 I said, "If the Beloved were exposed to outward view,
\q1 Neither wouldst thou endure, nor embrace, nor form.
\q1 Press thy suit, yet with moderation;
\q1 A blade of grass cannot pierce a mountain.
\q1 If the sun that illumines the world
\q1 Were to draw nigher, the world would be consumed.
\q1 Close thy mouth and shut the eyes of this matter,
\q1 That the world's life be not made a bleeding heart.
\q1 No longer seek this peril, this bloodshed;
\q1 Hereafter impose silence on the 'Sun of Tabriz.'"
\q1 He said, "Thy words are endless. Now tell forth
\q1 All thy story from its beginning."
\v 4 \vp 2.1\vp* Worldly senses are the ladder of earth,
\q1 Spiritual senses are the ladder of heaven.
\q1 The health of the former is sought of the leech,
\q1 The health of the latter from "The Friend."
\q1 The health of the former arises from tending the body,
\q1 That of the latter from mortifying the flesh.
\q1 The kingly soul lays waste the body,
\q1 And after its destruction he builds it anew.
\q1 Happy the soul who for love of God
\q1 Has lavished family, wealth, and goods!—
\q1 Has destroyed its house to find the hidden treasure,
\q1 And with that treasure has rebuilt it in fairer sort;
\q1 Has dammed up the stream and cleansed the channel,
\q1 And then turned a fresh stream into. the channel;—
\q1 Has cut its flesh to extract a spear-head,
\q1 Causing a fresh skin to grow again over the wound;—
\q1 Has razed the fort to oust the infidel in possession,
\q1 And then rebuilt it with a hundred towers and bulwarks.
\q1 Who can describe the unique work of Grace?
\q1 I have been forced to illustrate it by these similes.
\q1 Sometimes it presents one appearance, sometimes another.
\q1 Yea, the affair of religion is only bewilderment.
\q1 Not such as occurs when one turns one's back on God,
\q1 But such as when one is drowned and absorbed in Him.
\q1 The latter has his face ever turned to God,
\q1 The former's face shows his undisciplined self-will.
\q1 Watch the face of each one, regard it well,
\q1 It may be by serving thou wilt recognise Truth's face.
\q1 As there are many demons with men's faces,
\q1 It is wrong to join hand with every one.
\q1 When the fowler sounds his decoy whistle,
\q1 That the birds may be beguiled by that snare,
\q1 The birds hear that call simulating a bird's call,
\q1 And, descending from the air, find net and knife.
\q1 So vile hypocrites steal the language of Darveshes,
\q1 In order to beguile the simple with their trickery.
\q1 The works of the righteous are light and heat,
\q1 The works of the evil treachery and shamelessness.
\q1 They make stuffed lions to scare the simple,
\q1 They give the title of Muhammad to false Musailima.
\q1 But Musailima retained the name of "Liar,"
\q1 And Muhammad that of "Sublimest of beings."
\q1 That wine of God (the righteous) yields a perfume of musk;
\q1 Other wine (the evil) is reserved for penalties and pains.
\v 5 \vp 3.1\vp* Myriads of Christians flocked round him,
\q1 One after another they assembled in his street.
\q1 Then he would preach to them of mysteries,—
\q1 Mysteries of the Gospel, of stoles, of prayers.
\q1 He would preach to them with eloquent words
\q1 Concerning the words and acts of the Messiah.
\q1 Outwardly he was a preacher of religious duties,
\q1 But within a decoy call and a fowler's snare.
\q1 Therefore the followers of the Prophet ('Isa)
\q1 Were beguiled by the fraud of that demon soul.
\q1 He mingled in his discourses many secret doctrines
\q1 Concerning devotion and sincerity of soul.
\q1 He taught them to make a fair show of devotion,
\q1 But to say of secret sins, "What do they matter?"
\q1 Hair by hair and jot by jot they learned of him
\q1 Fraud of soul, as roses might learn of garlic.
\q1 Hair-splitters and all their disciples
\q1 Are darkened by similar preaching and discourse.
\q1 The Christians gave their hearts to him entirely,
\q1 For the blind faith of the vulgar has no discernment.
\q1 In their inmost breasts they planted love of him,
\q1 And fancied him to be the Vicar of Christ;—
\q1 Yea, him, that one-eyed and cursed Dajjal!
\q1 Save us. O God! who art our only defender!
\q1 O God, there are hundreds of snares and baits,
\q1 And we are even as greedy and foolish birds;
\q1 Every moment our feet are caught in a fresh snare;
\q1 Yea, each one of us, though he be a falcon or Simurgh!
\q1 Thou dost release us every moment, and straightway
\q1 We again fly into the snare, O Almighty One!
\v 6 \vp 3.2\vp* Every night Thou freest our spirits from the body
\q1 And its snare, making them pure as rased tablets.
\q1 Every night spirits are released from this cage,
\q1 And set free, neither lording it nor lorded over.
\q1 At night prisoners are unaware of their prison,
\q1 At night kings are unaware of their majesty.
\q1 Then there is no thought or care for loss or gain,
\q1 No regard to such an one or such an one.
\q1 The state of the "Knower" is such as this, even when awake.
\q1 God says, "Thou wouldst deem him awake though asleep,
\q1 Sleeping to the affairs of the world, day and night,
\q1 Like a pen in the directing hand of the writer.
\q1 He who sees not the hand which effects the writing
\q1 Fancies the effect proceeds from the motion of the pen.
\q1 If the "Knower" revealed the particulars of this state,
\q1 'Twould rob the vulgar of their sensual sleep.
\q1 His soul wanders in the desert that has no similitude;
\q1 Like his body, his spirit is enjoying perfect rest;—
\q1 Freed from desire of eating and drinking,
\q1 Like a bird escaped from cage and snare.
\q1 But when he is again beguiled into the snare,
\q1 He cries for help to the Almighty.
\v 7 \vp 3.3\vp* The Khalifa said to Laila, "Art thou really she
\q1 For whom Majnun lost his head and went distracted?
\q1 Thou art not fairer than many other fair ones."
\q1 She replied, "Be silent; thou art not Majnun!"
\q1 If thou hadst Majnun's eyes,
\q1 The two worlds would be within thy view.
\q1 Thou art in thy senses, but Majnun is beside himself.
\q1 In love to be wide awake is treason.
\q1 The more a man is awake, the more he sleeps (to love);
\q1 His (critical) wakefulness is worse than slumbering.
\q1 Our wakefulness fetters our spirits,
\q1 Then our souls are a prey to divers whims,
\q1 Thoughts of loss and gain and fears of misery.
\q1 They retain not purity, nor dignity, nor lustre,
\q1 Nor aspiration to soar heavenwards.
\q1 That one is really sleeping who hankers after each whim
\q1 And holds parley with each fancy.
\v 8 \vp 3.4\vp* He drew up a separate scroll to the address of each,
\q1 The contents of each scroll of a different tenor;
\q1 The rules of each of a different purport,
\q1 This contradictory of that, from beginning to end.
\q1 In one the road of fasting and asceticism
\q1 Was made the pillar and condition of right devotion.
\q1 In one 'twas said, "Abstinence profits not;
\q1 Sincerity in this path is naught but charity."
\q1 In one 'twas said, "Thy fasting and thy charity
\q1 Are both a making thyself equal with God;
\q1 Save faith and utter resignation to God's will
\q1 In weal and woe, all virtues are fraud and snares."
\q1 In one 'twas said, "Works are the one thing needful;
\q1 The doctrine of faith without works is a delusion."
\q1 In one 'twas said, "Commands and prohibitions are
\q1 Not for observance, but to demonstrate our weakness,
\q1 That we may see our own weakness (to carry them out),
\q1 And thereby recognise and confess God's power."
\q1 In one 'twas said, "Reference to thine own weakness
\q1 Is ingratitude for God's mercies towards us.
\q1 Rather regard thy power, for thou hast power from God.
\q1 Know thy power to be God's grace, for 'tis of Him."
\q1 In one 'twas said, "Leave power and weakness alone;
\q1 Whatever withdraws thine eyes from God is an idol."
\q1 In one 'twas said, "Quench not thy earthy torch,
\q1 That it may be a light to lighten mankind.
\q1 If thou neglectest regard and care for it,
\q1 Thou wilt quench at midnight the lamp of union."
\q1 In one 'twas said, "Quench that torch without fear,
\q1 That in lieu of one thou may'st see a thousand joys,
\q1 For by quenching the light the soul is rejoiced,
\q1 And thy Laila is then as bold as her Majnun.
\q1 Whoso to display his devotion renounces the world,
\q1 The world is ever with him, before and behind."
\q1 In one 'twas said, "Whatsoever God has given thee
\q1 In His creation, that He has made sweet to thee;
\q1 Yea, pleasant to thee and allowable. Take it, then,
\q1 And cast not thyself into the pangs of abstinence."
\q1 In one 'twas said, "Give up all thou possessest,
\q1 For to be ruled by covetousness is grievous sin."
\q1 (Ah! how many diverse roads are pointed out,
\q1 And each followed by some sect for dear life!
\q1 If the right road were easily attainable,
\q1 Every Jew and Gueber would have hit on it!)
\q1 In one 'twas said, "The right road is attainable,
\q1 For the heart's life is the food of the soul.
\q1 Whatever is enjoyed by the carnal man
\q1 Yields no fruit, even as salt and waste land.
\q1 Its result is naught but remorse,
\q1 Its traffic yields only loss.
\q1 It is not profitable in the long run;
\q1 Its name is called 'bankrupt' in the upshot.
\q1 Discern, then, the bankrupt from the profitable,
\q1 Consider the eventual value of this and that."
\q1 In one 'twas said, "Choose ye a wise Director,
\q1 But foresight of results is not found in dignities."
\q1 (Each sect looked to results in a different way,
\q1 And so, perforce, became captive to errors.
\q1 Real foresight of results is not simple jugglery,
\q1 Otherwise all these differences would not have arisen.
\q1 In one 'twas said, "Thyself art thy master,
\q1 Inasmuch as thou art acquainted with the Master of all;
\q1 Be a man, and not another man's beast of burden!
\q1 Follow thine own way and lose not thy head!"
\q1 In one 'twas said, "All we see is One.
\q1 Whoever says 'tis two is suffering from double vision."
\q1 In one 'twas said, "A hundred are even as one."
\q1 But whoso thinks this is a madman.
\q1 Each scroll had its contrary piece of rhetoric,
\q1 In form and substance utterly opposed to it;
\q1 This contrary to that, from first to last,
\q1 As if each was compounded of poison and antidotes.
\v 9 \vp 4.1\vp* Air, earth, water, and fire are God's servants.
\q1 To us they seem lifeless, but to God living.
\q1 In God's presence fire ever waits to do its service,
\q1 Like a submissive lover with no will of its own.
\q1 When you strike steel on flint fire leaps forth;
\q1 But 'tis by God's command it thus steps forth.
\q1 Strike not together the flint and steel of wrong,
\q1 For the pair will generate more, like man and woman.
\q1 The flint and steel are themselves causes, yet
\q1 Look higher for the First Cause, O righteous man!
\q1 For that Cause precedes this second cause.
\q1 How can a cause exist of itself without precedent cause?
\q1 That Cause makes this cause operative,
\q1 And again helpless and inoperative.
\q1 That Cause, which is a guiding light to the prophets,
\q1 That, I say, is higher than these second causes.
\q1 Men's minds recognise these second causes,
\q1 But only prophets perceive the action of the First Cause.
\v 10 \vp 4.2\vp* Though water be enclosed in a reservoir,
\q1 Yet air will absorb it, for 'tis its supporter;
\q1 It sets it free and bears it to its source,
\q1 Little by little, so that you see not the process.
\q1 In like manner this breath of ours by degrees
\q1 Steals away our souls from the prison-house of earth.
\q1 " The good word riseth up to Him,"
\q1 Rising from us whither He knoweth.
\q1 Our breathings are lifted up in fear of God,
\q1 Offerings from us to the throne of Eternity.
\q1 Then come down to us rewards for our praises,
\q1 The double thereof, yea, mercies from the King of Glory.
\q1 Therefore are we constrained to utter these praises,
\q1 That slaves may attain the height of God's gifts.
\q1 And so this rising and descent go on evermore,
\q1 And cease not forever and aye.
\q1 To speak in plain Persian, this attraction
\q1 Comes from the same quarter whence comes this sweet savour.
\v 11 \vp 5.1\vp* The beasts said, "O enlightened sage,
\q1 Lay aside caution; it cannot help thee against destiny;
\q1 To worry with precaution is toil and moil;
\q1 Go, trust in Providence, trust is the better part.
\q1 War not with the divine decree, O hot-headed one,
\q1 Lest that decree enter into conflict with thee.
\q1 Man should be as dead before the commands of God,
\q1 Lest a blow befall him from the Lord of all creatures."
\q1 He said, "True; but though trust be our mainstay,
\q1 Yet the Prophet teaches us to have regard to means.
\q1 The Prophet cried with a loud voice,
\q1 'Trust in God, yet tie the camel's leg.' 
\q1 Hear the adage, 'The worker is the friend of God;'
\q1 Through trust in Providence neglect not to use means.
\q1 Go, O Quietists, practise trust with self-exertion,
\q1 Exert yourself to attain your objects, bit by bit.
\q1 In order to succeed, strive and exert yourselves;
\q1 If ye strive not for your objects, ye are fools."
\q1 They said, "What is gained from the poor by exertions
\q1 Is a fraudulent morsel that will bring ill luck.
\q1 Again, know that self-exertion springs from weakness;
\q1 Relying on other means is a blot upon perfect trust.
\q1 Self-exertion is not more noble than trust in God.
\q1 What is more lovely than committing oneself to God?
\q1 Many there are who flee from one danger to a worse;
\q1 Many flee from a snake and meet a dragon.
\q1 Man plans a stratagem, and thereby snares himself;
\q1 What he takes for life turns out to be destruction.
\q1 He shuts the door after his foe is in the house.
\q1 After this sort were the schemes of Pharaoh.
\q1 That jealous king slew a myriad babes,
\q1 While Moses, whom he sought, was in his house.
\q1 Our eyes are subject to many infirmities;
\q1 Go! annihilate your sight in God's sight.
\q1 For our foresight His foresight is a fair exchange;
\q1 In His sight is all that ye can desire.
\q1 So long as a babe cannot grasp or run,
\q1 It takes its father's back for its carriage.
\q1 But when it becomes independent and uses its hands,
\q1 It falls into grievous troubles and disgrace.
\q1 The souls of our first parents, even before their hands,
\q1 Flew away from fidelity after vain pleasure.
\q1 Being made captives by the command, 'Get down hence,'
\q1 They became bond-slaves of enmity, lust, and vanity.
\q1 We are the family of the Lord and His sucking babes.
\q1 The Prophet said, 'The people are God's family;'
\q1 He who sends forth the rain from heaven,
\q1 Can He not also provide us our daily bread?"
\q1 The lion said, "True; yet the Lord of creatures
\q1 Sets a ladder before our feet.
\q1 Step by step must we mount up to the roof!
\q1 The notion of fatalism is groundless in this place.
\q1 Ye have feet why then pretend ye are lame?
\q1 Ye have hands— why then conceal your claws?
\q1 When a master places a spade in the hand of a slave,
\q1 The slave knows his meaning without being told.
\q1 Like this spade, our hands are our Master's hints to us;
\q1 Yea, if ye consider, they are His directions to us.
\q1 When ye have taken to heart His hints,
\q1 Ye will shape your life in reliance on their direction;
\q1 Wherefore these hints disclose His intent,
\q1 Take the burden from you, and appoint your work.
\q1 He that bears it makes it bearable by you,
\q1 He that is able makes it within your ability.
\q1 Accept His command, and you will be able to execute it;
\q1 Seek union with Him, and you will find yourselves united.
\q1 Exertion is giving thanks for God's blessings;
\q1 Think ye that your fatalism gives such thanks?
\q1 Giving thanks for blessings increases blessings,
\q1 But fatalism snatches those blessings from your hands.
\q1 Your fatalism is to sleep on the road; sleep not
\q1 Till ye behold the gates of the King's palace.
\q1 Ah! sleep not, O unreflecting fatalists,
\q1 Till ye have reached that fruit-laden Tree of Life
\q1 Whose branches are ever shaken by the wind,
\q1 And whose fruit is showered on the sleepers' heads.
\q1 Fatalism means sleeping amidst highwaymen.
\q1 Can a cock who crows too soon expect peace?
\q1 If ye cavil at and accept not God's hints,
\q1 Though ye count yourselves men, see, ye are women.
\q1 The quantum of reason ye possessed is lost,
\q1 And the head whose reason has fled is a tail.
\q1 Inasmuch as the unthankful are despicable,
\q1 They are at last cast into the fiery pit.
\q1 If ye really have trust in God, exert yourselves,
\q1 And strive, in constant reliance on the Almighty."
\v 12 \vp 5.2\vp* He said, "O friends, God has given me inspiration.
\q1 Often times strong counsel is suggested to the weak.
\q1 The wit taught by God to the bee
\q1 Is withheld from the lion and the wild ass.
\q1 It fills its cells with liquid sweets,
\q1 For God opens the door of this knowledge to it.
\q1 The skill taught by God to the silkworm
\q1 Is a learning beyond the reach of the elephant.
\q1 The earthly Adam was taught of God names,
\q1 So that his glory reached the seventh heaven.
\q1 He laid low the name and fame of the angels,
\q1 Yet blind indeed are they whom God dooms to doubt!
\q1 The devotee of seven hundred thousand years (Satan)
\q1 Was made a muzzle for that yearling calf (Adam),
\q1 Lest he should suck milk of the knowledge of faith,
\q1 And soar on high even to the towers of heaven.
\q1 The knowledge of men of external sense is a muzzle
\q1 To stop them sucking milk of that sublime knowledge.
\q1 But God drops into the heart a single pearl-drop
\q1 Which is not bestowed on oceans or skies!"
\q1 "How long regard ye mere form, O form-worshippers?
\q1 Your souls, void of substance, rest still in forms.
\q1 If the form of man were all that made man,
\q1 Ahmad and Abu Jahl would be upon a par.
\q1 A painting on a wall resembles a man,
\q1 But see what it is lacking in that empty form.
\q1 'Tis life that is lacking to that mere semblance of man.
\q1 Go! seek for that pearl it never will find.
\q1 The heads of earth's lions were bowed down
\q1 When God gave might to the Seven Sleepers' dog.
\q1 What mattered its despised form
\q1 When its soul was drowned in the sea of light?"
\v 13 \vp 5.3\vp* On his way to the lion the hare lingered,
\q1 Devising a stratagem with himself.
\q1 He proceeded on his way after delaying long,
\q1 In order to have a secret or two for the lion.
\q1 What worlds the principle of Reason embraces!
\q1 How broad is this ocean of Reason!
\q1 Yea, the Reason of man is a boundless ocean.
\q1 O son, that ocean requires, as it were, a diver.
\q1 On this fair ocean our human forms
\q1 Float about, like bowls on the surface of water;
\q1 Yea, like cups on the surface, till they are filled;
\q1 And when filled, these cups sink into the water.
\q1 The ocean of Reason is not seen; reasoning men are seen;
\q1 But our forms (minds) are only as waves or spray thereof.
\q1 Whatever form that ocean uses as its instrument,
\q1 Therewith it casts its spray far and wide.
\q1 Till the heart sees the Giver of the secret,
\q1 Till it espies that Bowman shooting from afar,
\q1 It fancies its own steed lost, while in bewilderment
\q1 It is urging that steed hither and thither;
\q1 It fancies its own steed lost, when all the while
\q1 That swift steed is bearing it on like the wind.
\q1 In deep distress that blunder head
\q1 Runs from door to door, searching and inquiring,
\q1 "Who and where is he that hath stolen my steed?"
\q1 They say, "What is this thou ridest on, O master?"
\q1 He says, "True, 'tis a steed; but where is mine?"
\q1 They say, "Look to thyself, O rider; thy steed is there."
\q1 The real Soul is lost to view, and seems far off;
\q1 Thou art like a pitcher with full belly but dry lip;
\q1 How canst thou ever see red, green, and scarlet
\q1 Unless thou seest the light first of all?
\q1 When thy sight is dazzled by colors,
\q1 These colours veil the light from thee.
\q1 But when night veils those colours from thee,
\q1 Thou seest that colours are seen only through light.
\q1 As there is no seeing outward colours without light,
\q1 So it is with the mental colours within.
\q1 Outward colours arise from the light of sun and stars,
\q1 And inward colours from the Light on high.
\q1 The light that lights the eye is also the heart's Light;
\q1 The eye's light proceeds from the Light of the heart.
\q1 But the light that lights the heart is the Light of God,
\q1 Which is distinct from the light of reason and sense.
\q1 At night there is no light, and colours are not seen;
\q1 Hence we know what light is by its opposite, darkness.
\q1 At night no colours are visible, for light is lacking.
\q1 How can colour be the attribute of dark blackness?
\q1 Looking on light is the same as looking on colours;
\q1 Opposite shows up opposite, as a Frank a Negro.
\q1 The opposite of light shows what is light,
\q1 Hence colours too are known by their opposite.
\q1 God created pain and grief for this purpose,
\q1 To wit, to manifest happiness by its opposites.
\q1 Hidden things are manifested by their opposites;
\q1 But, as God has no opposite, He remains hidden.
\q1 God's light has no opposite in the range of creation
\q1 Whereby it may be manifested to view.
\q1 Perforce "Our eyes see not Him, though He sees us."
\q1 Behold this in the case of Moses and Mount Sinai.
\q1 Discern form from substance, as lion from desert,
\q1 Or as sound and speech from the thought they convey.
\q1 The sound and speech arise from the thought;
\q1 Thou knowest not where is the Ocean of thought;
\q1 Yet when thou seest fair waves of speech,
\q1 Thou knowest there is a glorious Ocean beneath them.
\q1 When waves of thought arise from the Ocean of Wisdom,
\q1 They assume the forms of sound and speech.
\q1 These forms of speech are born and die again,
\q1 These waves cast themselves back into the Ocean.
\q1 Form is born of That which is without form,
\q1 And goes again, for, "Verily to Him do we return."
\q1 Wherefore to thee every moment come death and "return."
\q1 Mustafa saith, "The world endureth only a moment."
\q1 So, thought is an arrow shot by God into the air.
\q1 How can it stay in the air? It returns to God.
\q1 Every moment the world and we are renewed,
\q1 Yet we are ignorant of this renewing forever and aye.
\q1 Life, like a stream of water, is renewed and renewed,
\q1 Though it wears the appearance of continuity in form.
\q1 That seeming continuity arises from its swift renewal,
\q1 As when a single spark of fire is whirled round swiftly.
\q1 If a single spark be whirled round swiftly,
\q1 It seems to the eye a continuous line of fire.
\q1 This apparent extension, owing to the quick motion,
\q1 Demonstrates the rapidity with which it is moved.
\q1 If ye seek the deepest student of this mystery,
\q1 Lo! 'tis Husamu-'d-Din, the most exalted of creatures!
\v 14 \vp 6.1\vp* The ambassador said, "O Commander of the faithful,
\q1 How comes the soul down from above to earth?
\q1 How can so noble a bird be confined in a cage?"
\q1 He said, "God speaks words of power to souls,—
\q1 To things of naught, without eyes or ears,
\q1 And at these words they all spring into motion;
\q1 At His words of power these nothings arise quickly,
\q1 And strong impulse urges them into existence.
\q1 Again, He speaks other spells to these creatures,
\q1 And swiftly drives them back again into Not-being.
\q1 He speaks to the rose's ear, and causes it to bloom;
\q1 He speaks to the tulip, and makes it blossom.
\q1 He speaks a spell to body, and it becomes soul;
\q1 He speaks to the sun, and it becomes a fount of light.
\q1 Again, in its ear He whispers a word of power,
\q1 And its face is darkened as by a hundred eclipses.
\q1 What is it that God says to the ear of earth,
\q1 That it attends thereto and rests steadfast?
\q1 What is it that Speaker says to the cloud.
\q1 That it pours forth rain-water like a water-skin?
\q1 Whosoever is bewildered by wavering will,
\q1 In his ear hath God whispered His riddle,
\q1 That He may bind him on the horns of a dilemma;
\q1 For he says, 'Shall I do this or its reverse?'
\q1 Also from God comes the preference of one alternative;
\q1 'Tis from God's impulsion that man chooses one of the two.
\q1 If you desire sanity in this embarrassment,
\q1 Stuff not the ear of your mind with cotton.
\q1 Take the cotton of evil suggestions from the mind's ear,
\q1 That the heavenly voice from above may enter it,
\q1 That you may understand that riddle of His,
\q1 That you may be cognisant of that open secret.
\q1 Then the mind's ear becomes the sensorium of inspiration;
\q1 For what is this Divine voice but the inward voice?
\q1 The spirit's eye and ear possess this sense,
\q1 The eye and ear of reason and sense lack it.
\q1 The word 'compulsion' makes me impatient for love's sake;
\q1 'Tis he who loves not who is fettered by compulsion.
\q1 This is close communion with God, not compulsion,
\q1 The shining of the sun, and not a dark cloud.
\q1 Or, if it be compulsion, 'tis not common compulsion,
\q1 It is not the domination of wanton wilfulness.
\q1 O son, they understand this compulsion
\q1 For whom God opens the eyes of the inner man.
\q1 Things hidden and things future are plain to them;
\q1 To speak of the past seems to them despicable.
\q1 They possess freewill and compulsion besides,
\q1 As in oyster-shells raindrops are pearls.
\q1 Outside the shell they are raindrops, great and small;
\q1 Inside they are precious pearls, big and little.
\q1 These men also resemble the musk deer's bag;
\q1 Outside it is blood, but inside pure musk;
\q1 Yet, say not that outside 'twas mere blood,
\q1 Which on entering the bag becomes musk.
\q1 Nor say that outside the alembic 'twas mere copper,
\q1 And becomes gold inside, when mixed with elixir.
\q1 In you freewill and compulsion are vain fancies,
\q1 But in them they are the light of Almighty power.
\q1 On the table bread is a mere lifeless thing,
\q1 When taken into the body it is a life-giving spirit.
\q1 This transmutation occurs not in the table's heart,
\q1 'Tis soul effects this transmutation with water of life.
\q1 Such is the power of the soul, O man of right views!
\q1 Then what is the power of the Soul of souls? (God).
\q1 Bread is the food of the body, yet consider,
\q1 How can it be the food of the soul, O son?
\q1 Flesh-born man by force of soul
\q1 Cleaves mountains with tunnels and mines.
\q1 The might of Ferhad's soul cleft a hill;
\q1 The might of the Soul's soul cleaves the moon.
\q1 If the heart opens the mouth of mystery's store,
\q1 The soul springs up swiftly to highest heaven.
\q1 If tongue discourses of hidden mysteries,
\q1 It kindles a fire that consumes the world.
\q1 Behold, then, God's action and man's action;
\q1 Know, action does belong to us; this is evident.
\q1 If no actions proceeded from men,
\q1 How could you say, 'Why act ye thus?'
\q1 The agency of God is the cause of our action,
\q1 Our actions are the signs of God's agency;
\q1 Nevertheless our actions are freely willed by us,
\q1 Whence our recompense is either hell or 'The Friend.'"
\v 15 \vp 7.1\vp* As to a "man of heart," he takes no hurt,
\q1 Even though he should eat deadly poison.
\q1 He who gains health from practising abstinence is safe;
\q1 The poor disciple is safe in the midst of fever.
\q1 The prophet said, "O disciple, though you be bold,
\q1 Yet enter not into conflict with every foe."
\q1 Within you is a Nimrod; enter not his fire;
\q1 But if you must do so, first become an Abraham.
\q1 If you are neither swimmer nor seaman,
\q1 Cast not yourself into the sea out of self-conceit.
\q1 A swimmer brings pearls from the deep sea;
\q1 Yea, he plucks gain from the midst of perils.
\q1 If the saint handles earth, it becomes gold;
\q1 If a sinner handles gold, it turns to dust.
\q1 Whereas the saint is well-pleasing to God,
\q1 In his actions his hand is the hand of God.
\q1 But the sinner's hand is the hand of Satan and demons,
\q1 Because he is ensnared in falsity and fraud.
\q1 If folly meets him, he takes it for wisdom;
\q1 Yea, the learning gained by the wicked is folly.
\q1 Whatever a sick man eats is a source of sickness,
\q1 But if a saint imbibe infidelity it becomes faith.
\q1 Ah! footman who contendest with horsemen,
\q1 Thou wilt not succeed in carrying the day!
\v 16 \vp 7.2\vp* The whole world is jealous for this cause,
\q1 That God surpasseth the world in jealousy.
\q1 God is as a soul and the world as a body,
\q1 And bodies derive their good and evil from souls.
\q1 He to whom the sanctuary of true prayer is revealed
\q1 Deems it shameful to turn back to mere formal religion.
\q1 He who is master of the robes of a king
\q1 Brings shame on his lord by petty huckstering.
\q1 He who is admitted to the king's presence-chamber
\q1 Would show disrespect by tarrying at the doorway.
\q1 If the king grants him license to kiss his hand,
\q1 He would err were he to kiss merely the king's foot.
\q1 Though to lay head at the king's feet is due obeisance,
\q1 In the case supposed it would be wrong to kiss the feet.
\q1 The king's jealousy would be kindled against him
\q1 Who, after he had seen his face, preferred his mere perfume.
\q1 God's jealousy may be likened to a grain of wheat,
\q1 But man's jealousy is but empty chaff.
\q1 For know ye that the source of jealousy is in God,
\q1 And man's jealousy is only an offshoot from God's.
\q1 But let me now quit this subject, and make complaint
\q1 Of the severity of That Fickle Fair One.
\v 17 \vp 7.3\vp* "Wherefore dost thou abandon thy creed and faith?
\q1 What matters it if it be heathen or true?
\q1 Why hast thou forsaken thy Beloved?
\q1 What matters it if she be fair or ugly?"
\q1 Let me then, I say, make complaint
\q1 Of the severity of That Fickle Fair One.
\q1 I cry, and my cries sound sweet in His ear;
\q1 He requires from the two worlds cries and groans.
\q1 How shall I not wail under His chastening hand?
\q1 How shall I not be in the number of those bewitched by Him?
\q1 How shall I be other than night without His day?
\q1 Without the vision of His face that illumes the day?
\q1 His bitters are very sweets to my soul,
\q1 My sad heart is a lively sacrifice to my Beloved.
\q1 I am enamoured of my own grief and pain,
\q1 For it makes me well-pleasing to my peerless King.
\q1 I use the dust of my grief as salve for my eyes,
\q1 That my eyes, like seas, may teem with pearls.
\q1 The tears which are shed because of His chastening
\q1 Are very pearls, though men deem them mere tears.
\q1 'Tis "The Soul of souls" of whom I am making complaint;
\q1 Yet I do not complain;— I merely state my case.
\q1 My heart says, "He has injured me,"
\q1 But I laugh at these pretended injuries.
\q1 Do me justice, O Thou who art the glory of the just,
\q1 Who art the throne, and I the lintel of Thy door!
\q1 But, in sober truth, where are throne and doorway?
\q1 Where are "We" and "I?" There where our Beloved is!
\q1 O Thou, who art exempt from "Us" and "Me,—"
\q1 Who pervadest the spirits of all men and women;
\q1 When man and woman become one, Thou art that One!
\q1 When their union is dissolved, lo! Thou abidest!
\q1 Thou hast made these "Us" and "Me" for this purpose,
\q1 To wit, to play chess with them by Thyself.
\q1 When Thou shalt become one entity with "Us" and "You."
\q1 Then wilt Thou show true affection for these lovers.
\q1 When these "We" and "Ye" shall all become one Soul,
\q1 Then they will be lost and absorbed in the "Beloved."
\q1 These are plain truths. Come then, O Lord!
\q1 Who art exalted above description and explanation!
\q1 Is it possible for the bodily eye to behold Thee?
\q1 Can mind of man conceive Thy frowns and Thy smiles?
\q1 Are hearts, when bewitched by Thy smiles and frowns,
\q1 In a fit state to see the vision of Thyself?
\q1 When our hearts are bewitched by Thy smiles and frowns,
\q1 Can we gain life from these two alternating states?
\q1 The fertile garden of love, as it is boundless,
\q1 Contains other fruits besides joy and sorrow.
\q1 The true lover is exalted above these two states,
\q1 He is fresh and green independently of autumn or spring!
\q1 Pay tithe on Thy beauty, O Beauteous One!
\q1 Tell forth the tale of the Beloved, every whit!
\q1 For through coquetry His glances
\q1 Are still inflicting fresh wounds on my heart.
\q1 I gave Him leave to shed my blood, if He willed it;
\q1 I only said, "Is it right? " and He forsook me.
\q1 Why dost Thou flee from the cries of us on earth?
\q1 Why pourest Thou sorrow on the heart of the sorrowful?
\q1 O Thou who, as each new morn dawns from the east,
\q1 Art seen uprising anew, like a bright fountain!
\q1 What excuse makest Thou for Thy witcheries?
\q1 O Thou whose lips are sweeter than sugar,
\q1 Thou that ever renewest the life of this old world,
\q1 Hear the cry of this lifeless body and heart!
\q1 But, for God's sake, leave off telling of the Rose;
\q1 Tell of the Bulbul who is severed from his Rose.
\q1 My ardour arises not from joy or grief,
\q1 My sense mates not with illusion and fancy.
\q1 My condition is different, for it is strange.
\q1 Deny it not! God is all-powerful.
\q1 Argue not from the condition of common men,
\q1 Stumble not at severity and at mercy.
\q1 For mercy and severity, joy and sorrow, are transient,
\q1 And transient things die; "God is heir of all."
\q1 "Tis dawn! O Protector and Asylum of the dawn!
\q1 Make excuse for me to my lord Husamu-'d-Din!
\q1 Thou makest excuses for (Universal Reason and Soul;
\q1 Soul of souls and Gem of life art Thou!
\q1 The light of my dawn is a beam from Thy light,
\q1 Shining in the morning draught of Thy protection!
\q1 Since Thy gift keeps me, as it were, intoxicated,
\q1 What is this spiritual wine that causes me this joy?
\q1 Natural wine lacks the ferment in my breast,
\q1 The spheres lag behind me in revolutions!
\q1 Wine is intoxicated with me, not I with it!
\q1 The world takes its being from me, not I from it!
\q1 I am like bees, and earthly bodies like wax,
\q1 I build up these bodies as with my own wax!
\v 18 \vp 8.1\vp* Mustafa became beside himself at that sweet call,
\q1 His prayer failed on "the night of the early morning halt."
\q1 He lifted not head from that blissful sleep,"
\q1 So that his morning prayer was put off till noon.
\q1 On that, his wedding night, in presence of his bride,
\q1 His pure soul attained to kiss her hands.
\q1 Love and mistress are both veiled and hidden,
\q1 Impute it not as a fault if I call Him "Bride."
\q1 I would have kept silence from fear of my Beloved,
\q1 If He had granted me but a moment's respite.
\q1 But He said, "Speak on, 'tis no fault,
\q1 'Tis naught but the necessary result of the hidden decree,
\q1 'Tis a fault only to him who only sees faults.
\q1 How can the Pure Hidden Spirit notice faults?"
\q1 Faults seem so to ignorant creatures,
\q1 Not in the sight of the Lord of Benignity.
\q1 Blasphemy even may be wisdom in the Creator's sight,
\q1 Whereas from our point of view it is grievous sin.
\q1 If one fault occur among a hundred beauties,
\q1 'Tis as one dry stick in a garden of green herbs.
\q1 Both weigh equally in the scales
\q1 For the two resemble body and soul.
\q1 Wherefore the sages have said not idly,
\q1 " The bodies of the righteous are as pure souls."
\q1 Their words, their actions, their praises,
\q1 Are all as a pure soul without spot or blemish.
\v 19 \vp 8.2\vp* Then 'Omar said to him, "This wailing of thine
\q1 Shows thou art still in a state of ' sobriety."'
\q1 Afterwards he thus urged him to quit that state,
\q1 And called him out of his beggary to absorption in God:
\q1 "Sobriety savours of memory of the past;
\q1 Past and future are what veil God from our sight.
\q1 Burn up both of them with fire! How long
\q1 Wilt thou be partitioned by these segments as a reed?
\q1 So long as a reed has partitions 'tis not privy to secrets,
\q1 Nor is it vocal in response to lip and breathing.
\q1 While circumambulating the house thou art a stranger;
\q1 When thou enterest in thou art at home.
\q1 Thou whose knowledge is ignorance of the Giver of knowledge,
\q1 Thy wailing contrition is worse than thy sin.
\q1 The road of the 'annihilated' is another road;
\q1 Sobriety is wrong, and a straying from that other road.
\q1 O thou who seekest to be contrite for the past,
\q1 How wilt thou be contrite for this contrition?
\q1 At one time thou adorest the music of the lute,
\q1 At another embracest wailing and weeping."
\q1 While the "Discerner" reflected these mysteries,
\q1 The heart of the harper was emancipated.
\q1 Like a soul he was freed from weeping and rejoicing,
\q1 His old life died, and he was regenerated.
\q1 Amazement fell upon him at that moment,
\q1 For he was exalted above earth and heaven,
\q1 An uplifting of the heart surpassing all uplifting;
\q1 I cannot describe it; if you can, say on!
\q1 Ecstasy and words beyond all ecstatic words;—
\q1 Immersion in the glory of the Lord of glory!
\q1 Immersion wherefrom was no extrication,
\q1 As it were identification with the Very Ocean!
\q1 Partial Reason is as naught to Universal Reason,
\q1 If one impulse dependent on another impulse be naught;
\q1 But when that impulse moves this impulse,
\q1 The waves of that sea rise to this point;
\v 20 \vp 9.1\vp* In this manner she pleaded with gentle coaxing,
\q1 The while her tears fell upon her cheeks.
\q1 How could his firmness and endurance abide
\q1 When even without tears she could charm his heart?
\q1 That rain brought forth a flash of lightning
\q1 Which kindled a spark in the heart of that poor man.
\q1 Since the man was the slave of her fair face,
\q1 How was it when she stooped to slavish entreaties?
\q1 When she whose airs set thy heart a-quaking,—
\q1 When she weeps, how feelest thou then?
\q1 When she whose coquetry makes thy heart bleed
\q1 Condescends to entreaties, how is it then?
\q1 She who subdues us with her pride and severity,
\q1 What plea is left us when she begins to plead?
\q1 When she who traded in naught but bloodshed
\q1 Submits at last, ah! what a profit she makes!
\q1 God has adorned them "fair in the sight of men;"
\q1 From her whom God has adorned how can man escape?
\q1 Since He created him "to dwell together with her,"
\q1 How can Adam sever himself from his Eve?
\q1 Though he be Rustum, son of Zal, and braver than Hamza,
\q1 Yet he is submissive to the behests of his dame.
\q1 He by whose preaching the world was entranced
\q1 Was he who spake the two words, "O Humaira!"
\q1 Though water prevails over fire in might,
\q1 Yet it boils by fire when in a cauldron.
\q1 When the cauldron intervenes between these two,
\q1 Air (desire) makes as naught the action of the water.
\q1 Apparently thou art the ruler of thy wife, like water;
\q1 In reality thou art ruled by and suppliant to her.
\q1 Such is the peculiarity of man,
\q1 He cannot withstand animal desire; that is his failing.
\q1 The Prophet said that women hold dominion
\q1 Over sages and over men of heart,
\q1 But that fools, again, hold the upper hand over women,
\q1 Because fools are violent and exceedingly froward.
\q1 They have no tenderness or gentleness or amity,
\q1 Because the animal nature sways their temperament.
\q1 Love and tenderness are qualities of humanity,
\q1 Passion and lust are qualities of animality.
\q1 Woman is a ray of God, not a mere mistress,
\q1 The Creator's self, as it were, not a mere creature!
\v 21 \vp 9.2\vp* Verily, both Moses and Pharaoh walked in the right way,
\q1 Though seemingly the one did so, and the other not.
\q1 By day Moses wept before God,
\q1 At midnight Pharaoh lifted up his cry,
\q1 Saying, "What a yoke is this upon my neck, O God!
\q1 Were it not for this yoke who would boast, 'I am?'
\q1 Because Thou hast made Moses' face bright as the moon,
\q1 And hast made the moon of my face black in the face.
\q1 Can my star ever shine brighter than the moon?
\q1 If it be eclipsed, what remedy have I?
\q1 Though princes and kings beat drums,
\q1 And men beat cymbals because of my eclipse,
\q1 They beat their brass dishes and raise a clamour,
\q1 And make my moon ashamed thereby,
\q1 I, who am Pharaoh, woe is me! The people's clamour
\q1 Confounds my boast, 'I am Lord Supreme!'
\q1 Moses and I are Thy nurslings both alike,
\q1 Yet Thy axe cuts down the branches in Thy woods.
\q1 Some of these branches Thou plantest in the ground,
\q1 Others Thou castest away as useless.
\q1 Can branch strive against axe? Not so.
\q1 Can branch elude the power of the axe? Nay,
\q1 O Lord of the power that dwells in Thy axe,
\q1 In mercy make these crooked things straight!"
\v 22 \vp 9.3\vp* The dissension of this husband and wife is a parable;
\q1 They are types of thy animal and rational souls.
\q1 This husband and wife are the reason and the flesh,
\q1 A couple joined together for good and for evil.
\q1 And in this earthly house this linked pair
\q1 Day and night are ever at variance and strife.
\q1 The wife is ever seeking dainties for domestic needs,
\q1 Namely, bread and meat and her own dignity and position.
\q1 Like the wife, the animal soul seeks comfort,
\q1 Sometimes carnal, sometimes ambitious;
\q1 Reason has no care for these matters,
\q1 In its mind is naught but regard to Allah.
\q1 Though the secret moral hereof is a bait and snare,
\q1 Hear its outward form to the end.
\q1 If spiritual manifestations had been sufficient,
\q1 The creation of the world had been needless and vain.
\q1 If spiritual thought were equivalent to love of God,
\q1 Outward forms of temples and prayers would not exist.
\q1 Presents which friends make one to another
\q1 Are naught but signs and indications,
\q1 To give outward testimony and witness
\q1 Of the love concealed within the heart.
\q1 Because outward attentions are evidence
\q1 Of secret love, O beloved!
\q1 The witness may be true or false,—
\q1 Now drunk with real wine, now with sour whey;
\q1 He who drinks fermented whey displays drunkenness,
\q1 Makes a noise, and reels to and fro.
\q1 That hypocrite in prayers and fasts
\q1 Displays exceeding diligence,
\q1 That men may think him drunk with love of God;
\q1 But if you look into the truth, he is drowned in hypocrisy.
\q1 In fine, outward actions are guides
\q1 To show the way to what is concealed within.
\q1 Sometimes the guide is true, sometimes false,
\q1 Sometimes a help, and at other times a hindrance.
\q1 O Lord, grant, in answer to my prayers, discernment,
\q1 That I may know such false signs from the true!
\q1 Know you how discernment accrues to the sense?
\q1 'Tis when sense "sees by the light of Allah."
\q1 If effects are obscure, still causes testify;
\q1 Kindred, for instance, shows that there is love.
\q1 But he to whom God's light is the guide
\q1 Is no longer a slave to effects and causes.
\q1 When the light of Allah illumes his senses,
\q1 A man is no longer a slave to effects.
\q1 When love of God kindles a flame in the inward man,
\q1 He burns, and is freed from effects.
\q1 He has no need of signs to assure him of love,
\q1 For love casts its own light up to heaven.
\q1 Other details are wanting to complete this subject,
\q1 But take this much, and all hail to you!
\q1 Though reality is exposed to view in this form,
\q1 Form is at once nigh to and far from reality.
\q1 For instance, these two resemble water and a tree;
\q1 When you look to their essence they are far apart;
\q1 Yet see how quickly a seed becomes a high tree
\q1 Out of water, along with earth and sunshine!
\q1 If you turn your eyes to their real essence,
\q1 These two are far, far apart from each other!
\q1 But let us quit this talk of essences and properties,
\q1 And return to the story of those two wealth-seekers.
\v 23 \vp 9.4\vp* He said, "By Allah, who knoweth hidden secrets,
\q1 Who created pure Adam out of dust;—
\q1 In the form, three cubits high, which he gave him,
\q1 He displayed the contents of all spirits, all decrees!—
\q1 Communicated to him the indelible tablet of existence,
\q1 That he might know all that is written on those tablets,
\q1 All that should be first and last to endless eternity
\q1 He taught him, with the knowledge of his own 'names,'
\q1 So that the angels were beside themselves at his instruction,
\q1 And gained more sanctity from his sanctification.
\q1 The expansion of their minds, which Adam brought about,
\q1 Was a thing unequalled by the expansion of the heavens.
\q1 For the wide expanse of that pure mind
\q1 The wide space of the seven heavens was not enough."
\q1 The Prophet said that God has declared,
\q1 "I am not contained in aught above or below,
\q1 I am not contained in earth or sky, or even
\q1 In highest heaven. Know this for a surety. O beloved!
\q1 Yet am I contained in the believer's heart!
\q1 If ye seek me, search in such hearts!"
\q1 He said also, "Enter the hearts of my servants
\q1 To gain the paradise of beholding Me, O fearer of God."
\q1 Highest heaven, with all its light and wide expanse,
\q1 When it beheld Adam, was shaken from its place!
\q1 Highest heaven is greatness itself revealed;
\q1 But what is form when reality draws nigh?
\q1 Every angel declared, "In times of yore
\q1 We bore friendship to the plains of earth;
\q1 We were wont to sow the seed of service on the earth,
\q1 Wherefore we bore a wondrous attachment to it.
\q1 What was this attachment to that house of earth
\q1 When our own natures are heavenly?
\q1 What was the friendship of lights like us to darkness?
\q1 How can light dwell together with darkness?
\q1 O Adam! that friendship arose from the scent of thee,
\q1 Because the earth is the warp and weft of thy body.
\q1 Thy earthly body was taken from there,
\q1 Thy pure spirit of light was shed down from here!
\q1 But our souls were enlightened by thy spirit
\q1 Long, long before earth had diverted it to itself.
\q1 We used to be on earth, ignorant of the earth,—
\q1 Ignorant of the treasure buried within it.
\q1 When we were commanded to depart from that place,
\q1 We felt sorrow at turning our steps away from it.
\q1 So that we raised many questions, saying,
\q1 ' O Lord! who will come to take our place?
\q1 Wilt Thou barter the glory of our praises and homage
\q1 For the vain babble (of men)?'
\q1 The commands of God then diffused joy upon us; He said,
\q1 'What are ye saying at such length?
\q1 What ye give tongue to so foolishly
\q1 Is as the words of spoiled children to their father.
\q1 I knew of myself what ye thought,
\q1 But I desired that ye should speak it;
\q1 As this boasting of yours is very improper,
\q1 So shall my mercy be shown to prevail over my wrath:
\q1 O angels, in order to show forth that prevailing,
\q1 I inspired that pretension to cavil and doubt;
\q1 If you say your say, and I forbear to punish you,
\q1 The gainsayers of my mercy must hold their peace.
\q1 My mercy equals that of a hundred fathers and mothers;
\q1 Every soul that is born is amazed thereat.
\q1 Their mercy is as the foam of the sea of my mercy;
\q1 It is mere foam of waves, but the sea abides ever!
\q1 What more shall I say? In that earthly shell
\q1 There is naught but foam of foam of foam of foam!'"
\q1 God is that foam; God is also that pure sea,
\q1 For His words are neither a temptation nor a vain boast.
\v 24 \vp 9.5\vp* The story is now concluded, with its ups and downs,
\q1 Like lovers' musings, without beginning or ending.
\q1 It has no beginning, even as eternity,
\q1 Nor ending, for 'tis akin to world without end.
\q1 Or like water, each drop whereof is at once
\q1 Beginning and end, and also has no beginning or end.
\q1 But God forbid! This story is not a vain fable,
\q1 'Tis the ready money of your state and mine, be sure!
\q1 Before every Sufi who is enlightened
\q1 Whatever is past is never mentioned.
\q1 When his whole thoughts are absorbed in present ecstasy,
\q1 No thought of consequences enters his mind. 
\q1 Arab, water-pot, and angels are all ourselves!
\q1 "Whatsoever turneth from God is turned from Him."
\q1 Know the husband is reason, the wife lust and greed;
\q1 She is vested with darkness and a gainsayer of reason.
\q1 Learn now whence springs the root of this circumstance,
\q1 From this, that the Whole has parts of divers kinds.
\q1 These parts of the Whole are not parts in relation to it,—
\q1 Not in the way that rose's scent is a part of the rose.
\q1 The beauty of the green shoot is part of the rose's beauty,
\q1 But the turtle-dove's cooing is a part of that Bulbul's music.
\q1 But if I engage in doubts and answers,
\q1 How can I give water to thirsty souls?
\q1 Yet, if you are perplexed by Whole and finite parts,
\q1 Have patience, for ( patience is the key of joy."
\q1 Be abstinent,— abstinent from vague thoughts,
\q1 Since there are lions in that desert (of thoughts).
\q1 Abstinence is the prince of medicines,
\q1 As scratching only aggravates a scab.
\q1 Abstinence is certainly the root of medicine;
\q1 Practise abstinence, see how it invigorates thy soul!
\q1 Accept this counsel and give ear thereto,
\q1 That it may be to thee as an earring of gold!
\q1 Nay, not a mere earring, but that thou mayest be a mine of gold,
\q1 Or that thou mayest surpass moon and Pleiades.
\q1 First, know creation is in various forms;
\q1 Souls are as various as the letters from Alif to Ya.
\q1 In this variety of letters there seems disorder,
\q1 Though in fact they agree in an integral unity.
\q1 In one aspect they are opposed, in another united;
\q1 In one aspect capricious, in another serious.
\q1 The day of judgment is the day of the great review;
\q1 Whoso is fair and enlightened longs for that review;
\q1 Whoso, like a Hindoo, is black (with sin),
\q1 The day of review will sound the knell of his disgrace.
\q1 Since he has not a face like a sun,
\q1 He desires only night like to a veil!
\q1 If his thorn puts not forth a single rosebud,
\q1 The spring in disclosing him is his foe.
\q1 But he who is from head to foot a perfect rose or lily,
\q1 To him spring brings rejoicing.
\q1 The useless thorn desires the autumn,
\q1 That autumn may associate itself with the garden;
\q1 And hide the rose's beauty and the thorn's shame,
\q1 That men may not see the bloom of the one and the other's shame,
\q1 That common stone and pure ruby may appear all as one.
\q1 True, the Gardener knows the difference even in autumn,
\q1 But the sight of One is better than the world's sight.
\q1 That One Person is Himself the world, as He is the sun,
\q1 And every star in heaven is a part of the sun.
\q1 That One Person is Himself the world, and the rest
\q1 Are all His dependents and parasites, O man!
\q1 He is the perfect world, yet He is single;
\q1 He holds in hand the writing of the whole of existence.
\q1 Wherefore all forms and colours of beauty cry out,
\q1 " Good news! good news! Lo! the spring is at hand!"
\q1 If the blossoms did not shine as bright helmets,
\q1 How could the fruits display their globes?
\q1 When the blossoms are shed the fruits come to a head,
\q1 When the body is destroyed the soul lifts up its head.
\q1 The fruit is the substance, the blossom only its form,
\q1 Blossom the good news, and fruit the promised boon.
\q1 When the blossoms fall the fruit appears,
\q1 When the former vanish the fruit is tasted.
\q1 Till bread is broken, how can it serve as food?
\q1 Till the grapes are crushed, how can they yield wine?
\q1 Till citrons be pounded up with drugs,
\q1 How can they afford healing to the sick?
\v 25 \vp 10.1\vp* The Prophet said to 'Ali, "O 'Ali,
\q1 Thou art the Lion of God, a hero most valiant;
\q1 Yet confide not in thy lion-like valour,
\q1 But seek refuge under the palm-trees of the 'Truth.'
\q1 Whoso takes obedience as his exemplar
\q1 Shares its proximity to the ineffable Presence.
\q1 Do thou seek to draw near to Reason; let not thy heart
\q1 Rely, like others, on thy own virtue and piety.
\q1 Come under the shadow of the Man of Reason, 
\q1 Thou canst not find it in the road of the traditionists.
\q1 That man enjoys close proximity to Allah;
\q1 Turn not away from obedience to him in any wise;
\q1 For he makes the thorn a bed of roses,
\q1 And gives sight to the eyes of the blind.
\q1 His shadow on earth is as that of Mount Qaf,
\q1 His spirit is as a Simurgh soaring on high.
\q1 He lends aid to the slaves of the friends of God,
\q1 And advances to high place them who seek him.
\q1 Were I to tell his praises till the last day,
\q1 My words would not be too many nor admit of curtailment,
\q1 He is the sun of the spirit, not that of the sky,
\q1 For from his light men and angels draw life.
\q1 That sun is hidden in the form of a man,
\q1 Understand me! Allah knows the truth.
\q1 O 'Ali, out of all forms of religious service
\q1 Choose thou the shadow of that dear friend of God!
\q1 Every man takes refuge in some form of service,
\q1 And chooses for himself some asylum;
\q1 Do thou seek refuge in the shadow of the wise man,
\q1 That thou mayest escape thy fierce secret foes.
\q1 Of all forms of service this is fittest for thee;
\q1 Thou shalt surpass all who were before thee.
\q1 Having chosen thy Director, be submissive to him,
\q1 Even as Moses submitted to the commands of Khizr.
\q1 Have patience with Khizr's actions, O sincere one!
\q1 Lest he say, 'There is a partition between us.'
\q1 Though he stave in thy boat, yet hold thy peace;
\q1 Though he slay a young man, heave not a sigh.
\q1 God declares his hand to be even as God's hand,
\q1 For He saith, ( The hand of God is over their hands.'
\q1 The hand of God impels him and gives him life;
\q1 Nay, not life only, but an eternal soul.
\q1 A friend is needed; travel not the road alone,
\q1 Take not thy own way through this desert!
\q1 Whoso travels this road alone
\q1 Only does so by aid of the might of holy men.
\q1 The hand of the Director is not weaker than theirs;
\q1 His hand is none other than the grasp of Allah!
\q1 If absent saints can confer such protection,
\q1 Doubtless present saints are more powerful than absent.
\q1 If such food be bestowed on the absent,
\q1 What dainties may not the guest who is present expect?
\q1 The courtier who attends in the presence of the king
\q1 Is served better than the stranger outside the gate.
\q1 The difference between them is beyond calculation;
\q1 One sees the light, the other only the veil.
\q1 Strive to obtain entrance within,
\q1 If thou wouldst not remain as a ring outside the door.
\q1 Having chosen thy Director, be not weak of heart,
\q1 Nor yet sluggish and lax as water and mud;
\q1 But if thou takest umbrage at every rub,
\q1 How wilt thou become a polished mirror?"
\v 26 \vp 11.1\vp* Once a man came and knocked at the door of his friend.
\q1 His friend said, "Who art thou. O faithful one?"
\q1 He said, "'Tis I." He answered, "There is no admittance.
\q1 There is no room for the 'raw' at my well-cooked feast.
\q1 Naught but fire of separation and absence
\q1 Can cook the raw one and free him from hypocrisy!
\q1 Since thy 'self' has not yet left thee,
\q1 Thou must be burned in fiery flames."
\q1 The poor man went away, and for one whole year
\q1 Journeyed burning with grief for his friend's absence.
\q1 His heart burned till it was cooked; then he went again
\q1 And drew near to the house of his friend.
\q1 He knocked at the door in fear and trepidation
\q1 Lest some careless word might fall from his lips.
\q1 His friend shouted, "Who is that at the door?"
\q1 He answered, "'Tis Thou who art at the door. O Beloved!"
\q1 The friend said, "Since 'tis I, let me come in,
\q1 There is not room for two 'I's' in one house."
\v 27 \vp 12.1\vp* He drew forth a mirror from his side
\q1 A mirror is what Beauty busies itself with.
\q1 Since Not-being is the mirror of Being,
\q1 If you are wise, choose Not-being (self-abnegation).
\q1 Being may be displayed in that Not-being,
\q1 Wealthy men show their liberality on the poor.
\q1 He who is an hungered is the clear mirror of bread,
\q1 The tinder is the mirror of the flint and steel.
\q1 Not-being and Defect, wherever they occur,
\q1 Are the mirrors of the Beauty of all beings.
\q1 Because Not-being is a clear filtered essence,
\q1 Wherein all these beings are infused.
\q1 When a garment is made by a good tailor,
\q1 'Tis an evidence of the tailor's art.
\q1 Logs of wood would not be duly shaped
\q1 Did not the carpenter plan outline and detail.
\q1 The leech skilled in setting bones goes
\q1 Where lies the patient with a broken leg.
\q1 If there were no sick and infirm,
\q1 How could the excellence of the leech's art be seen?
\q1 If vile base copper were not mingled,
\q1 How could the alchemist show his skill?
\q1 Defects are the mirrors of the attributes of Beauty,
\q1 The base is the mirror of the High and Glorious One,
\q1 Because one contrary shows forth its contrary,
\q1 As honey's sweetness is shown by vinegar's sourness.
\q1 Whoso recognises and confesses his own defects
\q1 Is hastening in the way that leads to perfection!
\q1 But he advances not towards the Almighty
\q1 Who fancies himself to be perfect.
\q1 No sickness worse than fancying thyself perfect
\q1 Can infect thy soul, O arrogant misguided one!
\q1 Shed many tears of blood from eyes and heart,
\q1 That this self-satisfaction may be driven out.
\q1 The fault of Iblis lay in saying, "I am better than he,"
\q1 And this same weakness lurks in the soul of all creatures.
\v 28 \vp 13.1\vp* On the last day, "when Earth shall quake with quaking,"
\q1 This earth shall give witness of her condition.
\q1 For she "shall tell out her tidings openly,"
\q1 Yea, earth and her rocks shall tell them forth!
\q1 The philosopher reasons from base analogies
\q1 (True reason comes not out of a dark corner);
\q1 The philosopher (I say) denies this in his pride of intellect.
\q1 Say to him, "Go, dash thy head against a wall!"
\q1 The speech of water, of earth, of mire,
\q1 Is audible by the ears of men of heart!
\q1 The philosopher, who denies Divine Providence,
\q1 Is a stranger to the perceptions of saints.
\q1 He says that the flashes of men's morbid imaginations
\q1 Instil many vain fancies into men's minds.
\q1 But, on the contrary, 'tis his perverseness and want of faith
\q1 Which implant in himself this vain fancy of negation.
\q1 The philosopher denies the existence of the Devil;
\q1 At the same time he is the Devil's laughing-stock.
\q1 If thou hast not seen the Devil, look at thyself,
\q1 Without demon's aid how came that blue turban on thy brow?
\q1 Whosoever has a doubt or disquietude in his heart
\q1 Is a secret denier and philosopher.
\q1 Now and then he displays firm belief,
\q1 But that slight dash of philosophy blackens his face.
\q1 Beware, O believers! That lurks in you too;
\q1 You may develop innumerable states of mind.
\q1 All the seventy and two heresies lurk in you;
\q1 Have a care lest one day they prevail over you!
\q1 He in whose breast the leaf of true faith is grown
\q1 Must tremble as a leaf from fear of such a catastrophe.
\q1 Thou makest a mock of Iblis and the Devil,
\q1 Because thou art a fine man in thy own sight;
\q1 But when thy soul shall tell thy wretched faults,
\q1 What lamentation thou wilt cause to the faithful!
\q1 The sellers of base gold sit smiling in their shops,
\q1 Because the touchstone is not as yet in their sight.
\q1 O Veiler of sins! strip not the veil from us;
\q1 Lend us aid on the day of trial!
\v 29 \vp 14.1\vp* The knowledge of men of heart bears them up,
\q1 The knowledge of men of the body weighs them down.
\q1 When 'tis knowledge of the heart, it is a friend;
\q1 When knowledge of the body, it is a burden.
\q1 God saith, "As an ass bearing a load of books,"
\q1 The knowledge which is not of Him is a burden.
\q1 Knowledge which comes not immediately from Him
\q1 Endures no longer than the rouge of the tirewoman.
\q1 Nevertheless, if you bear this burden in a right spirit
\q1 'Twill be removed, and you will obtain joy.
\q1 See you bear not that burden out of vainglory,
\q1 Then you will behold a store of true knowledge within.
\q1 When you mount the steed of this true knowledge,
\q1 Straightway the burden will fall from your back.
\q1 If you drink not His cup, how will you escape lusts?
\q1 You, who seek no more of Him than to name His name?
\q1 What do His name and fame suggest? The idea of Him.
\q1 And the idea of Him guides you to union with Him.
\q1 Know you a guide without something to which it guides?
\q1 Were there no roads there would be no ghouls.
\q1 Know you a name without a thing answering to it?
\q1 Have you ever plucked a rose (Gul) from Gaf and Lam?
\q1 You name His name; go, seek the reality named by it!
\q1 Look for the moon in heaven, not in the water!
\q1 If you desire to rise above mere names and letters,
\q1 Make yourself free from self at one stroke!
\q1 Like a sword be without trace of soft iron;
\q1 Like a steel mirror, scour off all rust with contrition;
\q1 Make yourself pure from all attributes of self,
\q1 That you may see your own pure bright essence!
\q1 Yea, see in your heart the knowledge of the Prophet,
\q1 Without book, without tutor, without preceptor.
\q1 The Prophet saith, "He is one of my people,
\q1 Whoso is of like temper and spirit with me.
\q1 His soul beholds me by the selfsame light
\q1 Whereby I myself behold him,—
\q1 Without traditions and scriptures and histories,
\q1 In the fount of the water of life."
\q1 Learn the mystery, "I was last night a Kurd,
\q1 And this morning am become an Arab."
\q1 This mystery of "last night" and "this morning"
\q1 Leads you into the road that brings you to God.
\q1 But if you want an instance of this secret knowledge,
\q1 Hear the story of the Greeks and the Chinese.
\v 30 \vp 15.1\vp* At dawn the Prophet said to Zaid,
\q1 "How is it with thee this morning, O pure disciple?"
\q1 He replied, "Thy faithful slave am I." Again he said,
\q1 "If the garden of faith has bloomed, show a token of it."
\q1 He answered, "I was athirst many days,
\q1 By night I slept not for the burning pangs of love;
\q1 So that I passed by days and nights,
\q1 As the point of a spear glances off a shield.
\q1 For in that state all faith is one,
\q1 A hundred thousand years and a moment are all one;
\q1 World without beginning and world without end are one;
\q1 Reason finds no entrance when mind is thus lost."
\v 31 \vp 15.2\vp* The Prophet said, "My companions are as the stars,
\q1 Lights to them that walk aright, missiles against Satan.
\q1 If every man had strength of eyesight
\q1 To look straight at the light of the sun in heaven,
\q1 What need were there of stars, O humble one,
\q1 To one who was guided by the light of the sun?
\q1 Neither moon nor planets would be needed
\q1 By one who saw directly the Sun of 'The Truth.'
\q1 The Moon declares, as also the clouds and shadows,
\q1 ' I am a man, yet it hath been revealed to me.'
\q1 Like you, I was naturally dark,
\q1 'Twas the Sun's revelation that gave me such light.
\q1 I still am dark compared to the Sun,
\q1 Though I am light compared to the dark souls of men.
\q1 Therefore is my light weak, that you may bear it,
\q1 For you are not strong enough to bear the dazzling Sun.
\q1 I have, as it were, mixed honey with vinegar,
\q1 To succour the sickness of your hearts.
\q1 When you are cured of your sickness, O invalid,
\q1 Then leave out the vinegar and eat pure honey.
\q1 When the heart is garnished and swept clear of lust,
\q1 Therein 'The God of Mercy sitteth on His throne.'
\q1 Then God rules the heart immediately,
\q1 When it has gained this immediate connection with Him.
\q1 This subject is endless; but where is Zaid,
\q1 That I may tell him again not to seek notoriety?
\q1 'Tis not wise to publish these mysteries,
\q1 Since the last day is approaching to reveal all things."
\q1 Now you will not find Zaid, for he is fled,
\q1 He sprang from the place where the shoes were left,
\q1 Scattering the shoes in his hurry.
\q1 If you had been Zaid, you too would have been lost,
\q1 As a star is lost when the sun shines on it;
\q1 For then you see no trace or sign of it,
\q1 No place or track of it in the milky way.
\q1 Our senses and our endless discourses
\q1 Are annihilated in the light of the knowledge of our King.
\q1 Our senses and our reason within us
\q1 Are as waves on waves "assembled before us."
\q1 When night returns and 'tis the time of the sky's levee,
\q1 The stars that were hidden come forth to their work.
\q1 The people of the world lie unconscious,
\q1 With veils drawn over their faces, and asleep;
\q1 But when the morn shall burst forth and the sun arise
\q1 Every creature will raise its head from its couch;
\q1 To the unconscious God will restore consciousness;
\q1 They will stand in rings as slaves with rings in ears;
\q1 Dancing and clapping hands with songs of praise,
\q1 Singing with joy, "Our Lord hath restored us to life!"
\q1 Shedding their old skins and bones,
\q1 As horsemen stirring up a cloud of dust.
\q1 All pressing on from Not-being to Being,
\q1 On the last day, as well the thankful as the unthankful.
\v 32 \vp 16.1\vp* "The Prophet whispered in the ear of my servant
\q1 That one day he would sever my head from my neck.
\q1 The Prophet also warned by inspiration me, his friend,
\q1 That the hand of my servant would destroy me.
\q1 My servant cried, "O kill me first,
\q1 That I may not become guilty of so grievous a sin!"
\q1 I replied, "Since my death is to come from thee,
\q1 How can I balk the fateful decree?"
\q1 He fell at my feet and cried, "O gracious lord,
\q1 For God's sake cleave now my body in twain,
\q1 That such an evil deed may not be wrought by me,
\q1 And my soul burn with anguish for its beloved."
\q1 I replied, "What God's pen has written, it has written;
\q1 In presence of its writings knowledge is confounded;
\q1 There is no anger in my soul against thee,
\q1 Because I attribute not this deed to thee;
\q1 Thou art God's instrument, God's hand is the agent.
\q1 How can I chide or fret at God's instrument?"
\q1 He said, "If this be so, why is there retaliation?"
\q1 I answered, "'Tis from God, and 'tis God's secret;
\q1 If He shows displeasure at His own acts,
\q1 From His displeasure He evolves a Paradise;
\q1 He feels displeasure at His own acts,
\q1 Because He is a God of vengeance as of mercy.
\q1 In this city of events He is the Lord,
\q1 In this realm He is the King who plans all events.
\q1 If He crushes His own instruments,
\q1 He makes those crushed ones fair in His sight.
\q1 Know the great mystery of 'whatever verses we cancel,
\q1 Or cause you to forget, we substitute better for them.'
\q1 Whatever law God cancels, He makes as a weed,
\q1 And in its stead He brings forth a rose.
\q1 So night cancels the business of the daytime,
\q1 When the reason that lights our minds becomes inanimate.
\q1 Again, night is cancelled by the light of day,
\q1 And inanimate reason is rekindled to life by its rays.
\q1 Though darkness produces this sleep and quiet,
\q1 Is not the 'water of life' in the darkness?
\q1 Are not spirits refreshed in that very darkness?
\q1 Is not that silence the season of heavenly voices?
\q1 For from contraries contraries are brought forth,
\q1 Out of darkness was created light.
\q1 The Prophet's wars brought about the present peace,
\q1 The peace of these latter days resulted from those wars.
\q1 That conqueror of hearts cut off a thousand heads,
\q1 That the heads of his people might rest in peace."
\v 33 \vp 16.2\vp* To whomsoever God's order comes,
\q1 He must smite with his sword even his own child.
\q1 Fear then, and revile not the wicked,
\q1 For the wicked are impotent under God's commands.
\q1 In presence of God's commands bow down the neck of pride.
\q1 Scoff not nor chide even them that go astray!
\q1 One day Adam cast a look of contempt and scorn
\q1 Upon Iblis, thinking what a wretch he was.
\q1 He felt self-important and proud of himself,
\q1 And he smiled at the actions of cursed Iblis.
\q1 God Almighty cried out to him, "O pure one,
\q1 Thou art wholly ignorant of hidden mysteries.
\q1 If I were to blab the faults of the unfortunate,
\q1 I should root up the mountains from their bases,
\q1 And lay bare the secrets of a hundred Adams,
\q1 And convert a hundred fresh Iblises into Musulmans."
\q1 Adam answered, "I repent me of my scornful looks;
\q1 Such arrogant thoughts shall not be mine again.
\q1 O Lord, pardon this rashness in Thy slave;
\q1 I repent; chastise me not for these words!"
\q1 O Aider of aid-seekers, guide us,
\q1 For there is no security in knowledge or wealth;
\q1 "Lead not our hearts astray after Thou hast guided us,"
\q1 And avert the evil that the "Pen" has written.
\q1 Turn aside from our souls the evil written in our fates,
\q1 Repel us not from the tables of purity!
\q1 O God, Thy grace is the proper object of our desire;
\q1 To couple others with Thee is not proper.
\q1 Nothing is bitterer than severance from Thee,
\q1 Without Thy shelter there is naught but perplexity.
\q1 Our worldly goods rob us of our heavenly goods,
\q1 Our body rends the garment of our soul.
\q1 Our hands, as it were, prey on our feet;
\q1 Without reliance on Thee how can we live?
\q1 And if the soul escapes these great perils,
\q1 It is made captive as a victim of misfortunes and fears,
\q1 Inasmuch as when the soul lacks union with the Beloved,
\q1 It abides forever blind and darkened by itself.
\q1 If Thou showest not the way, our life is lost;
\q1 A life living without Thee esteem as dead!
\q1 If Thou findest fault with Thy slaves,
\q1 Verily it is right in Thee, O Blessed One!
\q1 If Thou shouldst call sun and moon obscure,
\q1 If Thou shouldst call the straight cypress crooked,
\q1 If Thou shouldst declare the highest heaven base,
\q1 Or rich mines and oceans paupers,—
\q1 All this is the truth in relation to Thy perfection!
\q1 Thine is the dominion and the glory and the wealth!
\q1 For Thou art exempt from defect and not-being,
\q1 Thou givest existence to things non-existent, and again
\q1 Thou makest them non-existent.
\v 34 \vp epilogue.1\vp* Alas! the forbidden fruits were eaten,
\q1 And thereby the warm life of reason was congealed.
\q1 A grain of wheat eclipsed the sun Of Adam, 
\q1 Like as the Dragon's tail dulls the brightness of the moon.
\q1 Behold how delicate is the heart, that a morsel of dust
\q1 Clouded its moon with foul obscurity!
\q1 When bread is "substance," to eat it nourishes us;
\q1 When 'tis empty "form," it profits nothing.
\q1 Like as the green thorn which is cropped by the camel,
\q1 And then yields him pleasure and nutriment;
\q1 When its greenness has gone and it becomes dry,—
\q1 If the camel crops that same thorn in the desert,
\q1 It wounds his palate and mouth without pity,
\q1 As if conserve of roses should turn to sharp swords.
\q1 When bread is "substance," it is as a green thorn;
\q1 When 'tis "form," 'tis as the dry and coarse thorn.
\q1 And thou eatest it in the same way as of yore
\q1 Thou wert wont to eat it, O helpless being,—
\q1 Eatest this dry thing in the same manner,
\q1 After the real "substance" is mingled with dust;
\q1 It has become mingled with dust, dry in pith and rind.
\q1 O camel, now beware of that herb!
\q1 The Word is become foul with mingled earth;
\q1 The water is become muddy; close the mouth of the well,
\q1 Till God makes it again pure and sweet;
\q1 Yea, till He purifies what He has made foul.
\q1 Patience will accomplish thy desire, not haste.
\q1 Be patient, God knows what is best.
\c 2
\cp 2
\p
\v 1 \vp prologue.1\vp* THE composition of this Masnavi has been delayed for a season.
\q1 Time is needed for blood to become milk.
\q1 Till thy fortune comes forth as a new-born babe,
\q1 Blood becomes not milk, sweet and pleasant to the mind.
\q1 When that light of God, Husamu-'d-Din
\q1 Turned his course down from the summit of heaven,—
\q1 While he had ascended to sublimest verities,
\q1 In the absence of his spring the buds blossomed not,—
\q1 But when out of that sea he came to shore,
\q1 The lute of the poesy of the Masnavi sounded again.
\q1 This Masnavi, which is the polisher of spirits,—
\q1 Its recommencement occurred on the day of "Opening."
\q1 The commencement date of this precious work
\q1 Was the year six hundred and sixty-two of the Flight.
\q1 The Bulbul started on this date and became a hawk;
\q1 Yea, a hawk to hunt out these mysteries.
\q1 May the wrist of the King be the resting-place of this hawk,
\q1 And may this door be open to the people for ever!
\v 2 \vp 1.1\vp* What is it hinders me from expounding my doctrines
\q1 But this, that my hearers' hearts incline elsewhere.
\q1 Their thoughts are intent on that Sufi guest;
\q1 They are immersed in his affairs neck deep.
\q1 So I am compelled to turn from my discourse
\q1 To that story, and to set forth his condition.
\q1 But, O friend, think not this Sufi a mere outward form,
\q1 As children see in a vine nothing but raisins.
\q1 O son, our bodies are as dried grapes and raisins;
\q1 If you are a man, cast away these things.
\q1 If you pass on to the pure mysteries of God,
\q1 You will be exalted above the nine heavenly spheres.
\q1 Now hear the outward form of my story,
\q1 But yet separate the grain from the chaff.
\v 3 \vp 1.2\vp* God sent the prophets for this purpose,
\q1 Namely, to sever infidelity from faith.
\q1 God sent the prophets to mankind
\q1 That they might gather the pure grain on their tray.
\q1 Infidel and faithful, Mosalman and Jew,
\q1 Before the prophets came, seemed all as one.
\q1 Before they came we were all alike,
\q1 No one knew whether he was right or wrong.
\q1 Genuine coin and base coin were current alike;
\q1 The world was a night, and we travellers in the dark,
\q1 Till the sun of the prophets arose, and cried,
\q1 "Begone. O slumber; welcome, O pure light!"
\q1 Now the eye sees how to distinguish colours,
\q1 It sees the difference between rubies and pebbles.
\q1 The eye distinguishes jewels from dust,
\q1 Hence it is dust makes the eyes smart.
\q1 Makers of base coin hate the daylight,
\q1 Coins of pure gold love the daylight,
\q1 Because daylight is the mirror that reflects them,
\q1 So that they see their own perfect beauty.
\v 4 \vp 1.3\vp* God has named the resurrection "that day;"
\q1 Day shows off the beauty of red and yellow.
\q1 Wherefore "Day" in 'truth is the mystery of the saints;
\q1 One day of their moons is as whole years.
\q1 Know, "Day " is the reflection of the mystery of the saints,
\q1 Eye-closing night that of their hidden secrets.
\q1 Therefore hath God revealed the chapter "Daylight,"
\q1 Which daylight is the light of the heart of Mustafa.
\q1 On the other view, that daylight means "The Friend,"
\q1 It is also a reflection of the same prophet.
\q1 For, as it is wrong to swear by a transitory being,
\q1 How can we suppose a transitory being spoken of by God?
\q1 The Friend of God said, "I love not them that set?"
\q1 How, then, could Allah have meant a transitory being?
\q1 Again, the words "by the night" mean Muhammad's veiling,
\q1 Namely, the fair earthly body that he bore;
\q1 When his sun proceeded from heaven on high
\q1 Into that body's night, it said, "He hath not forsaken thee;"
\q1 Union with God arose out of the depth of that disgrace;
\q1 That boon was the word, "He hath not been displeased."
\v 5 \vp 1.4\vp* Every expression is the sign of a state of mind;
\q1 That state is a hand, the expression an instrument.
\q1 A goldsmith's instruments in the hand of a cobbler
\q1 Are as grains of wheat sown on sand.
\q1 The tools of a cobbler in the hand of a cultivator
\q1 Are as grass before a dog or bones before an ass.
\q1 The words, "I am the Truth" were light in Mansur's mouth,
\q1 In the mouth of Pharaoh "I am Lord Supreme" was blasphemy.
\q1 The staff in the hand of Moses was a witness,
\q1 In the hands of the magicians it was naught.
\q1 For this cause 'Isa taught not to that foolish man
\q1 The words of power whereby he raised the dead.
\q1 For he who is ignorant misuses the instrument;
\q1 If you strike flint on mud you will get no fire.
\q1 Hand and instrument resemble flint and steel;
\q1 You must have a pair; a pair is needed to generate.
\q1 He who has no peer or member is the "One,"
\q1 An uneven number, One without dispute!
\q1 Whoso says "one" and "two," and so on,
\q1 Confesses thereby the existence of the "One."
\q1 When the illusion of seeing double is swept away,
\q1 They who say "one" and "two" are even as they who say "One."
\q1 If you take "One" as your ball in his tennis-field,
\q1 It is made to revolve by the strokes of his bat.
\q1 Yea, the ball that is even and without fault
\q1 Is made to revolve by the strokes of the King's hand.
\q1 O man of double vision, hearken with attention,
\q1 Seek a cure for your defective sight by listening.
\q1 Many are the holy words that find no entrance
\q1 Into blind hearts, but they enter hearts full of light.
\q1 But the deceits of Satan enter crooked hearts,
\q1 Even as crooked shoes fit crooked feet.
\q1 Though you repeat pious expressions again and again,
\q1 If you are a fool, they affect you not at all;—
\q1 Nay, not though you set them down in writing,
\q1 And though you proclaim them vauntingly;
\q1 Wisdom averts its face from you, O man of sin,
\q1 Wisdom breaks away from you and takes to flight!
\v 6 \vp 1.5\vp* "O wretch, why did you not come and say to me,
\q1 'Such and such a disastrous affair has occurred?'"
\q1 The servant replied, "By Allah, I came again and again,
\q1 That I might acquaint you with the matter.
\q1 You were always saying, 'The ass is gone, my lad!'
\q1 Along with the others in high excitement;
\q1 So I went away, thinking you knew all about it,
\q1 And were pleased at the transaction, being a wise man."
\q1 The Sufi said, "They were all singing the same words,
\q1 So I felt impelled to sing them as well.
\q1 Blind imitation of them has undone me.
\q1 Cursed be that blind imitation!"
\q1 The effect of blindly imitating unprofitable conduct
\q1 Is that men cast away honour for a morsel of bread.
\q1 The ecstasy of that company cast a reflection,
\q1 Whereby that Sufi's heart became ecstatic like them.
\q1 You need many reflections from your associates
\q1 In order to draw water from the peerless Ocean.
\q1 The first reflection cast is mere blind imitation;
\q1 After it has been often repeated you may test its truth.
\q1 Till it is thus verified, take it not from your friends;
\q1 The drop, not yet become pearl, sever not from its shell.
\v 7 \vp 1.6\vp* Would you have eyes and ears of reason clear,
\q1 Tear off the obstructing veil of greed!
\q1 The blind imitation of that Sufi proceeded from greed;
\q1 Greed closed his mind to the pure light.
\q1 Yea, 'twas greed that led astray that Sufi,
\q1 And brought him to loss of property and ruin.
\q1 Greed of victuals, greed of that ecstatic singing
\q1 Hindered his wits from grasping the truth.
\q1 If greed stained the face of a mirror,
\q1 That mirror would be as deceitful as we men are.
\q1 If a pair of scales were greedy of riches,
\q1 Would they tell truly the weight of anything?
\q1 The Prophet saith, "O people, through singleness of mind,
\q1 I ask of you no recompense for my prophesying;
\q1 I am a guide; God buyeth my guidance for you,
\q1 God giveth you my guidance in both worlds.
\q1 True, a guide deserves his wages;
\q1 Wages are due to him for directing you aright.
\q1 But what are my wages? The vision of The Friend.
\q1 Abu Bakr indeed offered me forty thousand pieces of gold,
\q1 But his forty thousand pieces were no wages for me.
\q1 How could I take brass beads for pearls of Aden?"
\q1 I will tell you a tale; hearken attentively,
\q1 That you may know how greed closes up the ears.
\q1 Every man subject to greed is a miser.
\q1 Can eyes of hearts clouded with greed see clearly?
\q1 The illusion of rank and riches blinds his sight,
\q1 Like hair dropping down before his eyes.
\v 8 \vp 2.1\vp* The pauper said, "Your beneficence is my sustenance;
\q1 To me, as to aliens, your prison is a paradise.
\q1 If you banish me from your prison in reprobation,
\q1 I must needs die of poverty and affliction."
\q1 Just so Iblis said to Allah, "O have compassion;
\q1 Lord! respite me till the day of resurrection.
\q1 For in this prison of the world I am at ease,
\q1 That I may slay the children of my enemies.
\q1 From every one who has true faith for food,
\q1 And as bread for his provisions by the way,
\q1 I take it away by fraud or deceit,
\q1 So that they raise bitter cries of regret.
\q1 Sometimes I menace them with poverty,
\q1 Sometimes I blind their eyes with tresses and moles."
\q1 In this prison the food of true faith is scarce,
\q1 And by the tricks of this dog what there is is lost.
\q1 In spite of prayers and fasts and endless pains,
\q1 Our food is altogether devoured by him.
\q1 Let us seek refuge with Allah from Satan.
\q1 Alas! we are perishing by his insolence.
\q1 The dog is one, yet he enters a thousand forms;
\q1 Whatever he enters straight becomes himself.
\q1 Whatever makes you shiver, know he is in it,—
\q1 The Devil is hidden beneath its outward form.
\q1 When he finds no form at hand, he enters your thoughts,
\q1 To cause them to draw you into sin.
\q1 From your thoughts proceeds destruction,
\q1 When from time to time evil thoughts occur to you.
\q1 Sometimes thoughts of pleasure, sometimes of business,
\q1 Sometimes thoughts of science, sometimes of house and home.
\q1 Sometimes thoughts of gain and traffic,
\q1 Sometimes thoughts of merchandise and wealth.
\q1 Sometimes thoughts of money and wives and children,
\q1 Sometimes thoughts of wisdom or of sadness.
\q1 Sometimes thoughts of household goods and fine linen,
\q1 Sometimes thoughts of carpets, sometimes of sweepers.
\q1 Sometimes thoughts of mills, gardens, and villas,
\q1 Sometimes of clouds and mists and jokes and jests.
\q1 Sometimes thoughts of peace and war,
\q1 Sometimes thoughts of honour and disgrace.
\q1 Ah! cast out of your head these vain imaginations,
\q1 Ah! sweep out of your heart these evil suggestions.
\q1 Cry, "There is no power nor strength but in God!"
\q1 To avert the Evil One from the world and your own soul.
\v 9 \vp 2.2\vp* Whatsoever is perceived by sense He annuls,
\q1 But He stablishes that which is hidden from the senses.
\q1 The lover's love is visible, his Beloved hidden.
\q1 The Friend is absent, the distraction he causes present.
\q1 Renounce these affections for outward forms,
\q1 Love depends not on outward form or face.
\q1 Whatever is beloved is not a mere empty form,
\q1 Whether your beloved be of the earth or of heaven.
\q1 Whatever be the form you have fallen in love with,—
\q1 Why do you forsake it the moment life leaves it?
\q1 The form is still there; whence, then, this disgust at it?
\q1 Ah! lover, consider well what is really your beloved.
\q1 If a thing perceived by outward senses is the beloved,
\q1 Then all who retain their senses must still love it;
\q1 And since love increases constancy,
\q1 How can constancy fail while form abides?
\q1 But the truth is, the sun's beams strike the wall,
\q1 And the wall only reflects that borrowed light.
\q1 Why give your heart to mere stones, O simpleton?
\q1 Go! seek the source of light which shineth alway!
\q1 Distinguish well true dawn from false dawn,
\q1 Distinguish the colour of the wine from that of the cup;
\q1 So that, instead of many eyes of caprice,
\q1 One eye may be opened through patience and constancy.
\q1 Then you will behold true colours instead of false,
\q1 And precious jewels in lieu of stones.
\q1 But what is a jewel? Nay, you will be an ocean of pearls;
\q1 Yea, a sun that measures the heavens!
\q1 The real Workman is hidden in His workshop,
\q1 Go you into that workshop and see Him face to face.
\q1 Inasmuch as over that Workman His work spreads a curtain,
\q1 You cannot see Him outside His work.
\q1 Since His workshop is the abode of the Wise One,
\q1 Whoso seeks Him without is ignorant of Him.
\q1 Come, then, into His workshop, which is Not-being,
\q1 That you may see the Creator and creation at once.
\q1 Whoso has seen how bright is the workshop
\q1 Sees how obscure is the outside of that shop.
\q1 Rebellious Pharaoh set his face towards Being (egoism),
\q1 And was perforce blind to that workshop.
\q1 Perforce he looked for the Divine decree to change,
\q1 And hoped to turn his destiny from his door.
\q1 While destiny at the impotence of that crafty one
\q1 All the while was secretly mocking.
\q1 He slew a hundred thousand guiltless babes
\q1 That the ordinance and decree of Allah might be thwarted.
\q1 That the prophet Moses might not be born alive,
\q1 He committed a thousand murders in the land.
\q1 He did all this, yet Moses was born,
\q1 And was protected against his wrath.
\q1 Had he but seen the Eternal workshop,
\q1 He had refrained hand and foot from these vain devices.
\q1 Within his house was Moses safe and sound,
\q1 While he was killing the babes outside to no purpose.
\q1 Just so the slave of lusts who pampers his body
\q1 Fancies that some other man bears him ill-will;
\q1 Saying this one is my enemy, and this one my foe,
\q1 While it is his own body which is his enemy and foe,
\q1 He is like Pharaoh, and his body is like Moses,
\q1 He runs abroad crying, "Where is my foe?"
\q1 While lust is in his house, which is his body,
\q1 He bites his finger in spite against strangers.
\v 10 \vp 3.1\vp* With that "brightness of lightning" He kindled their souls
\q1 So that Adam acquired knowledge from that light.
\q1 That which shone from Adam was gathered by Seth,
\q1 Wherefore Adam made him his viceroy when he saw it.
\q1 When Noah received the gift of that lustre,
\q1 He became a soul bearing pearls in the tempest of the flood.
\q1 By that light the soul of Abraham was led,
\q1 Without fear he entered Nimrod's fiery furnace.
\q1 When Ishmael sought out that light,
\q1 He meekly laid his head beneath his father's bright knife.
\q1 The soul of David was warmed by its heat,
\q1 Iron became pliable by the force of his weaving.
\q1 When Solomon was nurtured by its fruition,
\q1 The devils became the submissive slaves of his will.
\q1 When Jacob bowed his head to the Divine decree,
\q1 He recovered his sight at the scent of his son.
\q1 When moonlike Joseph saw that brilliant sun,
\q1 He became so expert as he was in interpreting dreams.
\q1 When the staff drew might from the hand of Moses,
\q1 It devoured the realm of Pharaoh at a mouthful.
\q1 When the soul of Jirjis became privy to its light,
\q1 He sacrificed his life seven times, and regained it.
\q1 When Zakhariah boasted of his love for it,
\q1 He ransomed his life in the hollow of the tree.
\q1 When Jonah swallowed a draught from that cup,
\q1 He found repose in the belly of the fish.
\q1 When John the Baptist became filled with its unction,
\q1 He laid his head in the golden charger in ardour for it.
\q1 When Jethro became aware of this exaltation,
\q1 He risked his life to find it.
\q1 Patient Job gave thanks for seven years,
\q1 For in his calamities he saw signs of its approach.
\q1 When Khizr and Elias boasted of gaining it,
\q1 They found the water of life and were no more seen.
\q1 When Jesus, Son of Mary, found that ladder of ascent,
\q1 He ascended to the height of the fourth heaven.
\q1 When Muhammad gained that blessed possession,
\q1 In a moment he cleft asunder the disk of the moon.
\q1 When Abu Bakr became the exemplar of that grace,
\q1 He was companion of that Lord, and a 'faithful witness."
\q1 When 'Omar was enraptured with that beauty,
\q1 Like a mind he discerned true and false.
\q1 When Osman viewed those brilliant sights,
\q1 He diffused light and became "Lord of the two lights."
\q1 When Martaza ('Ali) shined with its reflection,
\q1 He became the "Lion of God" in the soul's domain.
\q1 When his two sons were illumined by this light,
\q1 They became the "Pearly earrings of highest heaven;"
\q1 One of them losing his life by poison,
\q1 The other losing his head as he went about his march.
\q1 When Junaid was succoured by the forces of that light,
\q1 His ecstatic states exceeded counting.
\q1 Bayazid saw his way to increased fruition thereof,
\q1 And gained from God the name "Polestar of Gnostics."
\q1 What time King Mansur became victorious,
\q1 He left his throne and hastened to the stake.
\q1 When Karkhi of Karkh became its keeper,
\q1 He became lord of love and of the breath of Jesus.
\q1 Ibrahim son of Adham rode his horse to that point,
\q1 And became king of kings of equity.
\q1 And that Shakik starting from that junction
\q1 Became a sun of wit and acute of genius.
\q1 Fazil from a highway robber became a sage of the way,
\q1 When he was regarded with esteem by the King.
\q1 To Bishr Hafi the doctrine was announced,
\q1 And he set his face towards the desert of inquiry.
\q1 When Zu-l-Nun became distraught with care for it,
\q1 Egypt (Milk) as sugar became the house of his soul.
\q1 When Sari lost his head in seeking the way thereto,
\q1 His rank was exalted above the seats of the mighty.
\q1 A hundred thousand great (spiritual) kings
\q1 Exalted by this divine light approach the world.
\q1 Their names remain hidden through God's jealousy;
\q1 Every beggar tells not their names.
\v 11 \vp 4.1\vp* That my outward form may not mislead you,
\q1 Digest my sweet advice before copying me.
\q1 Many are they who have been captured by form,
\q1 Who aimed at form, and found Allah.
\q1 After all, soul is linked to body,
\q1 Though it in nowise resembles the body.
\q1 The power of the light of the eye is mated with fat,
\q1 The light of the heart is hidden in a drop of blood.
\q1 Joy harbours in the kidneys and pain in the liver,
\q1 The lamp of reason in the brains of the head;
\q1 Smell in the nostrils and speech in the tongue,
\q1 Concupiscence in the flesh and courage in the heart.
\q1 These connections are not without a why and a how,
\q1 But reason is at a loss to understand the how.
\q1 Universal Soul had connection with Partial Soul,
\q1 Which thence conceived a pearl and retained it in its bosom.
\q1 From that connection, like Mary,
\q1 Soul became pregnant of a fair Messiah;—
\q1 Not that Messiah who walked upon earth and water,
\q1 But that Messiah who is higher than space.
\q1 Next, as Soul became pregnant by the Soul of souls,
\q1 So by the former Soul did the world become pregnant;
\q1 Then the World brought forth another world,
\q1 And of this last are brought forth other worlds.
\q1 Should I reckon them in my speech till the last day
\q1 I should fail to tell the total of these resurrections.
\v 12 \vp 5.1\vp* 'Tis God's light that illumines the senses' light,
\q1 That is the meaning of "Light upon light."
\q1 The senses' light draws us earthwards,
\q1 God's light carries us heavenwards.
\q1 As objects of sense are of base condition,
\q1 God's light is an ocean, and the senses' light a dewdrop.
\q1 But that light which is "upon this light" is not seen,
\q1 Save through signs and holy discourses.
\q1 Since the senses' light is gross and dense,
\q1 It lies hidden in the black pupil of the eye.
\q1 When you cannot see the senses' light with the eye,
\q1 How can you see with the eye the Light of the mind?
\q1 As the senses' light is hidden in these gross veils,
\q1 Must not that Light which is pure be also hidden?
\q1 Like the senses, this world is ruled by a hidden Power.
\q1 It confesses its impotence before that hidden Power,
\q1 Which sometimes exalts it and sometimes lays it low,
\q1 Sometimes makes it dry and sometimes moist.
\q1 The hand is hidden, yet we see the pen writing;
\q1 The horse is galloping, yet the rider is hid from view.
\q1 The arrow speeds forth, yet the bow is not seen;
\q1 Souls are seen, the Soul of souls (God) is hidden.
\q1 Break not the arrow, for it is the arrow of the King;
\q1 Yea, it is an arrow from the bow of Wisdom.
\q1 "Ye shot not when ye shot," was said by God;
\q1 God's action has predominance over all actions.
\q1 Break your own passion, break not that arrow,
\q1 The eye of passion takes milk to be blood.
\q1 Kiss that arrow and bear it to the King,
\q1 Yea, though it be stained with your own blood.
\q1 Whatsoever is seen is weak and base and impotent;
\q1 What is hidden is equally fierce and headstrong.
\q1 We are the captured game; who is the snare?
\q1 We are the balls; where is the bat?
\q1 He tears and mends; who is this tailor?
\q1 He fans and kindles the flame; who is this kindler?
\q1 At one time He makes the faithful one an infidel,
\q1 At another He makes the atheist a devotee!
\v 13 \vp 6.1\vp* Through love bitter things seem sweet,
\q1 Through love bits of copper are made gold.
\q1 Through love dregs taste like pure wine,
\q1 Through love pains are as healing balms.
\q1 Through love thorns become roses,
\q1 And through love vinegar becomes sweet wine.
\q1 Through love the stake becomes a throne,
\q1 Through love reverse of fortune seems good fortune.
\q1 Through love a prison seems a rose bower,
\q1 Without love a grate full of ashes seems a garden.
\q1 Through love burning fire is pleasing light,
\q1 Through love the Devil becomes a Houri.
\q1 Through love hard stones become soft as butter,
\q1 Without love soft wax becomes hard iron.
\q1 Through love grief is as joy,
\q1 Through love Ghouls turn into angels.
\q1 Through love stings are as honey,
\q1 Through love lions are harmless as mice.
\q1 Through love sickness is health,
\q1 Through love wrath is as mercy.
\q1 Through love the dead rise to life,
\q1 Through love the king becomes a slave.
\q1 Even when an evil befalls you, have due regard;
\q1 Regard well him who does you this ill turn.
\q1 The sight which regards the ebb and flow of good and ill
\q1 Opens a passage for you from misfortune to happiness.
\q1 Thence you see the one state moves you into the other,
\q1 One opposite state generating its opposite in exchange.
\q1 So long as you experience not fears after joys,
\q1 How can you look for pleasures after disgusts?
\q1 While ye fear the doom of the angel on the left hand,
\q1 Men hope for the bliss of the angel on the right.
\q1 May you gain two wings! A fowl with only one wing
\q1 Is impotent to fly, O well-intentioned one!
\q1 Now either permit me to hold my peace altogether,
\q1 Or give me leave to explain the whole matter.
\q1 And if you mislike this and forbid that,
\q1 Who can tell what your desire is?
\q1 You must have the soul of Abraham in order with light
\q1 To see the mansions of Paradise in the fire.
\q1 Step by step he ascended above sun and moon,
\q1 And so lagged not below, as a ring that fastens a door.
\q1 Since the "Friend of God" ascended above the heavens,
\q1 And said, "I love not Gods that set;"
\q1 So this world of the body is a breeder of misconceptions
\q1 In all who have not fled from lust.
\v 14 \vp 7.1\vp* A voice came from God to Moses,
\q1 "Why hast thou sent my servant away?
\q1 Thou hast come to draw men to union with me,
\q1 Not to drive them far away from me.
\q1 So far as possible, engage not in dissevering;
\q1 'The thing most repugnant to me is divorce.'
\q1 To each person have I allotted peculiar forms,
\q1 To each have I given particular usages.
\q1 What is praiseworthy in thee is blameable in him,
\q1 What is poison for thee is honey for him.
\q1 What is good in him is bad in thee,
\q1 What is fair in him is repulsive in thee.
\q1 I am exempt from all purity and impurity,
\q1 I need not the laziness or alacrity of my people.
\q1 I created not men to gain a profit from them,
\q1 But to shower my beneficence upon them.
\q1 In the men of Hind the usages of Hind are praiseworthy,
\q1 In the men of Sind those of Sind.
\q1 I am not purified by their praises,
\q1 'Tis they who become pure and shining thereby.
\q1 I regard not the outside and the words,
\q1 I regard the inside and the state of heart.
\q1 I look at the heart if it be humble,
\q1 Though the words may be the reverse of humble.
\q1 Because the heart is substance, and words accidents,
\q1 Accidents are only a means, substance is the final cause.
\q1 How long wilt thou dwell on words and superficialities?
\q1 A burning heart is what I want; consort with burning!
\q1 Kindle in thy heart the flame of love,
\q1 And burn up utterly thoughts and fine expressions.
\q1 O Moses! the lovers of fair rites are one class,
\q1 They whose hearts and souls burn with love are another.
\q1 Lovers must burn every moment,
\q1 As tax and tithe are levied on a ruined village.
\q1 If they speak amiss, call them not sinners;
\q1 If a martyr be stained with blood, wash it not away.
\q1 Blood is better than water for martyrs,
\q1 This fault is better than a thousand correct forms.
\q1 No need to turn to the Ka'ba when one is in it,
\q1 And divers have no need of shoes.
\q1 One does not take a drunken man as a guide on the way,
\q1 Nor speak of darns to torn garments.
\q1 The sect of lovers is distinct from all others,
\q1 Lovers have a religion and a faith of their own.
\q1 Though the ruby has no stamp, what matters it?
\q1 Love is fearless in the midst of the sea of fear.
\q1 Beware, if thou offerest praises or thanksgivings,
\q1 And know them to be even as the babble of that shepherd;
\q1 Though thy praises be better compared with his,
\q1 Yet in regard to God they are full of defects.
\q1 How long wilt thou say, 'They obscure the truth,
\q1 For it is not such as they fancy'?
\q1 Thy own prayers are accepted only through mercy,
\q1 They are suffered as the prayers of an impure woman.
\q1 If her prayers are made impure by the flow of blood,
\q1 Thine are stained with metaphors and similitudes.
\q1 Blood is impure, yet its stain is removed by water;
\q1 But that impurity of ignorance is more lasting,
\q1 Seeing that without the blessed water of God
\q1 It is not banished from the man who is subject to it.
\q1 O that thou wouldst turn thy face to thy own prayers,
\q1 And become cognisant of the meaning of thy ejaculations,
\q1 And say, 'Ah! my prayers are as defective as my being;
\q1 O requite me good for evil!'"
\v 15 \vp 7.2\vp* Moses said, "O beneficent Creator,
\q1 With whom a moment's remembrance is as long ages,
\q1 I see Thy plan distorted in this world of earth and water;
\q1 My heart, like the angel's, feels a difficulty thereat.
\q1 With what object hast thou framed this plan,
\q1 And sowed therein the seeds of evil?
\q1 Why hast Thou kindled the fire of violence and wrong?
\q1 Why burnt up mosques and them who worship therein?
\q1 Paradise is attached to requirements unpleasant to us,
\q1 Hell is attached to things flattering our lusts.
\q1 The branch full of sap is the main fuel of thy fire.
\q1 'They that are burnt with fire are near to Kausar.'
\q1 Whoso is in prison and acquainted with troubles,
\q1 That is in requital for his gluttony and lusts.
\q1 Whoso is in a palace and enjoying wealth,
\q1 That is in reward for toils and troubles.
\q1 Whoso is seen enjoying uncounted gold and silver,
\q1 Know that he strove patiently to acquire it.
\q1 He, whose soul is exempt from natural conditions,
\q1 And who possesses the power of overriding causes,
\q1 Can see without causes, like eyes that pierce night;
\q1 But thou, who art dependent on sense attend to causes.
\q1 Having left Jesus, thou cherishest an ass (lust),
\q1 And art perforce excluded, like an ass;
\q1 The portion of Jesus is knowledge and wisdom,
\q1 Not so the portion of an ass, O asinine one!
\q1 Thou pitiest thine ass when it complains;
\q1 So art thou ignorant, thy ass makes thee asinine.
\q1 Keep thy pity for Jesus, not for the ass,
\q1 Make not thy lust to vanquish thy reason.
\q1 Leave thy natural lusts to whine and howl,
\q1 Tear thee from them, escape that snare of the soul!
\v 16 \vp 8.1\vp* Doing kindness is the game and quarry of good men,
\q1 A good man seeks in the world only pains to cure.
\q1 Wherever there is a pain there goes the remedy,
\q1 Wherever there is poverty there goes relief.
\q1 Seek not water, only show you are thirsty,
\q1 That water may spring up all around you.
\q1 That you may hear the words, "The Lord gives them to drink,"
\q1 Be athirst! Allah knows what is best for you.
\q1 Seek you the water of mercy? Be downcast,
\q1 And straightway drink the wine of mercy to intoxication.
\q1 Mercy is called down by mercy to the last.
\q1 Withhold not, then, mercy from any one, O son!
\q1 If of yourself you cannot journey to the Ka'ba,
\q1 Represent your helplessness to the Reliever.
\q1 Cries and groans are a powerful means,
\q1 And the All-Merciful is a mighty nurse.
\q1 The nurse and the mother keep excusing themselves,
\q1 Till their child begins to cry.
\q1 In you too has God created infant needs;
\q1 When they cry out, their milk is brought to them;
\q1 God said, "Call on God;" continue crying,
\q1 So that the milk of His love may boil up.
\v 17 \vp 8.2\vp* Moses said to one of those full of vain imaginations,
\q1 "O malevolent one, through error and heresy
\q1 You entertain a hundred doubts as to my prophethood,
\q1 Notwithstanding these proofs, and my holy character.
\q1 You have seen thousands of miracles done by me,
\q1 Yet they only multiply your doubts and cavils.
\q1 Through doubts and evil thoughts you are in a strait,
\q1 You speak despitefully of my prophethood.
\q1 I brought the host out of the Red Sea before all men,
\q1 That ye might escape the oppression of the Egyptians.
\q1 For forty years meat and drink came from heaven,
\q1 And water sprang from the rock at my prayer.
\q1 My staff became a mighty serpent in my hand,
\q1 Water became blood for my ill-conditioned enemy.
\q1 The staff became a snake, and my hand bright as the sun;
\q1 From the reflection of that light the sun became a star.
\q1 Have not these incidents, and hundreds more like them,
\q1 Banished these doubts from you, O cold-hearted one?
\q1 The calf lowed through magic,
\q1 And you bowed down to it, saying, 'Thou art my God.'
\q1 The golden calf lowed; but what did it say,
\q1 That the fools should feel all this devotion to it?
\q1 You have seen many more wondrous works done by me,
\q1 But where is the base man who accepts the truth?
\q1 What is it that charms vain men but vanity?
\q1 What else pleases the foolish but folly?
\q1 Because each kind is charmed by its own kind,
\q1 Does a cow ever seek the lion?
\q1 Did the wolf show love to Joseph,
\q1 Or only fraud upon fraud with a view to devour him?
\q1 True, if it lose his wolf-like nature it becomes a friend;
\q1 Even as the dog of the cave became a son of man.
\q1 When good Abu Bakr saw Muhammad,
\q1 He recognised his truth, saying, 'This one is true;'
\q1 When Abu Bakr caught the perfume of Muhammad,
\q1 He said, 'This is no false one.'
\q1 But Abu Jahl, who was not one of the sympathisers,
\q1 Saw the moon split asunder, yet believed not.
\q1 If from a sympathiser, to whom it is well known,
\q1 I withhold the truth, still 'tis not hidden from him;
\q1 But he who is ignorant and without sympathy,
\q1 However much I show him the truth, he sees it not.
\q1 The mirror of the heart must needs be polished
\q1 Before you can distinguish fair and foul therein."
\v 18 \vp 10.1\vp* The Sage said, "Whither are you going, O Bayazid?
\q1 Where will you bring your caravan to a halt?"
\q1 Bayazid replied, "At dawn I start for the Ka'ba."
\q1 Quoth the Sage, "What provision for the way have you?"
\q1 He answered, "I have two hundred silver dirhams;
\q1 See them tied up tightly in the corner of my cloak."
\q1 The Sage said, "Circumambulate me seven times;
\q1 Count this better than circumambulating the Ka'ba;
\q1 And as for the dirhams, give them to me, O liberal one,
\q1 And know you have finished your course and obtained your wish,
\q1 You have made the pilgrimage and gained the life to come,
\q1 You have become pure, and that in a moment of time.
\q1 Of a truth that is God which your soul sees in me,
\q1 For God has chosen me to be His house.
\q1 Though the Ka'ba is the house of His grace and favours,
\q1 Yet my body too is the house of His secret.
\q1 Since He made that house He has never entered it,
\q1 But none but That Living One enters this house;
\q1 When you have seen me you have seen God,
\q1 And have circumambulated the veritable Ka'ba.
\q1 To serve me is to worship and praise God;
\q1 Think not that God is distinct from me.
\q1 Open clear eyes and look upon me,
\q1 That you may behold the light of God in a mortal.
\q1 The Beloved once called the Ka'ba 'My house,'
\q1 But has said to me 'O my servant' seventy times;
\q1 O Bayazid, you have found the Ka'ba,
\q1 You have found a hundred precious blessings."
\q1 Bayazid gave heed to these deep sayings,
\q1 And placed them as golden earrings in his ears.
\v 19 \vp 10.2\vp* The sick man said, "Sickness has brought me this boon.
\q1 That this Prince (Muhammad) has come to me this morn,
\q1 So that health and strength may return to me
\q1 From the visit of this unparalleled King.
\q1 O blessed pain and sickness and fever!
\q1 O welcome weariness and sleeplessness by night!
\q1 Lo! God of His bounty and favour
\q1 Has sent me this pain and sickness in my old age;
\q1 He has given me pain in the back, that I may not fail
\q1 To spring up out of my sleep at midnight;
\q1 That I may not sleep all night like the cattle,
\q1 God in His mercy has sent me these pains.
\q1 At my broken state the pity of kings has boiled up,
\q1 And hell is put to silence by their threats!"
\q1 Pain is a treasure, for it contains mercies;
\q1 The kernel is soft when the rind is scraped off.
\q1 O brother, the place of darkness and cold
\q1 Is the fountain of life and the cup of ecstasy.
\q1 So also is endurance of pain and sickness and disease.
\q1 For from abasement proceeds exaltation.
\q1 The spring seasons are hidden in the autumns,
\q1 And autumns are charged with springs; flee them not.
\q1 Consort with grief and put up with sadness,
\q1 Seek long life in your own death!
\q1 Since 'tis bad, whatever lust says on this matter
\q1 Heed it not, its business is opposition.
\q1 But act contrary thereto, for the prophets
\q1 Have laid this injunction upon the world.
\q1 Though it is right to take counsel in affairs,
\q1 That you may have less to regret in the upshot;—
\q1 The prophets have laboured much
\q1 To make the world revolve on this pivot stone;
\q1 But, in order to destroy the people, lust desires
\q1 To make them go astray and lose their heads;—
\q1 The people say, 'With whom shall we take counsel?'
\q1 The prophets answer, 'With the reason of your chief.'
\q1 Again they say, 'Suppose a child or a woman enter,
\q1 Who lacks reason and clear judgment; '
\q1 They reply, 'Take counsel with them,
\q1 And act contrary to what they advise.'
\q1 Know your lust to be woman, and worse than woman;
\q1 Woman is partial evil, lust universal evil.
\q1 If you take counsel with your lust,
\q1 See you act contrary to what that base one advises.
\q1 Even though it enjoin prayers and fasting,
\q1 It is treacherously laying a snare for you.'
\q1 You must abandon and ignore your own knowledge,
\q1 And dip your hand in the dish of abnegation of knowledge.
\q1 Whatever seems profitable, flee from it,
\q1 Drink poison and spill the water of life.
\q1 Contemn whatever praises you,
\q1 Lend to paupers your wealth and profits!
\q1 Quit your sect and be a subject of aversion,
\q1 Cast away name and fame and seek disgrace!"
\v 20 \vp 10.3\vp* If you seek the explanation of God's love and favour,
\q1 In connection therewith read the chapter "Brightness."
\q1 And if you say evil also proceeds from Him,
\q1 Yet what damage is that to His perfection?
\q1 To send that evil is one of His perfections.
\q1 I will give you an illustration, O arrogant one;
\q1 The heavenly Artist paints His pictures of two sorts,
\q1 Fair pictures and pictures the reverse of fair.
\q1 Joseph he painted fair and made him beautiful;
\q1 He also painted ugly pictures of demons and 'afrits.
\q1 Both sorts of pictures are of His workmanship,
\q1 They proceed not from His imperfection, but His skill,
\q1 That the perfection of His wisdom may be shown,
\q1 And the gainsayers of His art be put to shame.
\q1 Could He not paint ugly things He would lack art,
\q1 And therefore He creates Guebers as well as Moslems.
\q1 Thus, both infidelity and faith bear witness to Him,
\q1 Both alike bow down before His almighty sway.
\q1 But know, the faithful worship Him willingly,
\q1 For they seek and aim at pleasing Him;
\q1 While Guebers worship Him unwillingly,
\q1 Their real aim and purpose being quite otherwise.
\v 21 \vp 10.4\vp* The Prophet said to that sick man,
\q1 "Pray in this wise and allay your difficulties;
\q1 'Give us good in the house of our present world,
\q1 And give us good in the house of our next world.
\q1 Make our path pleasant as a garden,
\q1 And be Thou, O Holy One, our goal!'"
\q1 The faithful will say on the last day, "O King!
\q1 Was not Hell on the route all of us travelled?
\q1 Did not faithful as well as infidels pass through it?
\q1 Yet on our way we perceived not the smoke of the fire;
\q1 Nay, it seemed Paradise and the mansion of the blessed."
\q1 Then the King will answer, "That green garden,
\q1 As it appeared to you on your passage through it,
\q1 Was indeed Hell and the place of dread torment;
\q1 Yet for you it became a garden green with trees.
\q1 Since you have laboured to make hellish lusts,
\q1 And the fire of pride that courts destruction,—
\q1 To make these, I say, pure and clean,—
\q1 And, to please God, have quenched those fires,
\q1 So that the fire of lust, that erst breathed flame,
\q1 Has become a holy garden and a guiding light,—
\q1 Since you have turned the fire of wrath to meekness,
\q1 And the darkness of ignorance to shining knowledge,
\q1 Since you have turned the fire of greed into bounty,
\q1 And the vile thorns of malice into a rose-garden;
\q1 Since you have quenched all these fires of your own
\q1 For my sake, so that those poisons are now pure sweets;—
\q1 Since you have made fiery lust as a verdant garden,
\q1 And have sowed therein the seed of fidelity,
\q1 So that nightingales of prayer and praise
\q1 Ever warble sweetly around this garden;—
\q1 Since you have responded to the call of God,
\q1 And have drawn water out of the hell of lust,—
\q1 For this cause my hell also, for your behoof,
\q1 Becomes a verdant garden and yields leaves and fruit."
\q1 What is the recompense of well-doing, O son?
\q1 It is kindness and good treatment and rich requital.
\q1 Have ye not said, "We are victims,
\q1 Mere nothings before eternal Being?
\q1 If we are drunkards or madmen,
\q1 'Tis that Cup-bearer and that Cup which make us so.
\q1 We bow down our heads before His edict and ordinance,
\q1 We stake precious life to gain His favour.
\q1 While the thought of the Beloved fills our hearts,
\q1 All our work is to do Him service and spend life for Him.
\q1 Wherever He kindles His destructive torch,
\q1 Myriads of lovers' souls are burnt therewith.
\q1 The lovers who dwell within the sanctuary
\q1 Are moths burnt with the torch of the Beloved's face."
\q1 O heart haste thither, for God will shine upon you,
\q1 And seem to you a sweet garden instead of a terror.
\q1 He will infuse into your soul a new soul,
\q1 So as to fill you, like a goblet, with wine.
\q1 Take up your abode in His soul!
\q1 Take up your abode in heaven, O bright full moon!
\q1 Like the heavenly Scribe, He will open your heart's book
\q1 That He may reveal mysteries unto you.
\q1 Abide with your Friend, since you have gone astray,
\q1 Strive to be a full moon; you are now a fragment thereof.
\q1 Wherefore this shrinking of the part from its whole?
\q1 Why this association with its foes?
\q1 Behold Genus become Species in due course,
\q1 Behold secrets become manifest through his light!
\q1 So long as woman-like you swallow blandishments,
\q1 How, O wise man, can you get relief from false flatteries?
\q1 These flatteries and fair words and deceits (of lust)
\q1 You take, and swallow, just like women.
\q1 But the reproaches and the blows of Darveshes
\q1 Are really better for you than the praises of sinners.
\q1 Take the light blows of Darveshes, not the honey of sinners,
\q1 And become, by the fortune of good, good yourself.
\q1 Because from them the robe of good fortune is gained,
\q1 In the asylum of the spirit blood becomes life.
\v 22 \vp 11.1\vp* A certain man was going into the mosque,
\q1 Just as another was coming out.
\q1 He inquired of him what had occurred to the meeting,
\q1 That the people were coming out of the mosque so soon.
\q1 The other told him that the Prophet
\q1 Had concluded the public prayers and mysteries.
\q1 "Whither go you," said he, "O foolish one,
\q1 Seeing the Prophet has already given the blessing?"
\q1 The first heaved a sigh, and its smoke ascended;
\q1 That sigh yielded a perfume of his heart's blood.
\q1 The other, who came from the mosque, said to him,
\q1 "Give me that sigh, and take my prayers instead."
\q1 The first said, "I give it, and take your prayers."
\q1 The other took that sigh with a hundred thanks.
\q1 He went his way with deep humility and contrition,
\q1 As a hawk who had ascended in the track of the falcon.
\q1 That night, as he lay asleep, he heard a voice from heaven,
\q1 "Thou hast bought the water of life and healing;
\q1 The worth of what thou hast chosen and possessed
\q1 Equals that of all the people's accepted prayers."
\v 23 \vp 11.2\vp* My people adopt my law without obeying it,
\q1 They take that coin without assaying it.
\q1 The Koran's wisdom is like the "believer's lost camel,'
\q1 Every one is certain his camel is lost.
\q1 You have lost your camel and seek it diligently;
\q1 Yet how will you find it if you know not your own?
\q1 What was lost? Was it a female camel that you lost?
\q1 It escaped from your hand, and you are in a maze.
\q1 The caravan is come to be loaded,
\q1 Your camel is vanished from the midst of it.
\q1 You run here and there, your mouth parched with heat;
\q1 The caravan moves on, and night approaches.
\q1 Your goods lie on the ground in a dangerous road,
\q1 You hurry after your camel in all directions.
\q1 You cry "O Moslems, who has seen a camel,
\q1 Which escaped from its stable this morning?
\q1 To him who shall give me news of my camel
\q1 I will give a reward of so many dirhems."
\q1 You go on seeking news of your camel from every one.
\q1 And every lewd fellow flatters you with a fresh rumour,
\q1 Saying, "I saw a camel; it went this way;
\q1 'Twas red, and it went towards this pasture."
\q1 Another says, "Its ear was cropped."
\q1 Another says, "Its cloth was embroidered."
\q1 Another that it had only one eye,
\q1 Another that it had lost its hair from mange.
\q1 To gain the reward every base fellow
\q1 Mentions a hundred marks without any foundation.
\v 24 \vp 11.3\vp* Just so every one in matters of doctrine
\q1 Gives a different description of the hidden subject.
\q1 A philosopher expounds it in one way,
\q1 And a critic at once refutes his propositions.
\q1 A third censures both of them;
\q1 A fourth spends his life in traducing the others.
\q1 Every one mentions indications of this road,
\q1 In order to create an impression that he has gone it.
\q1 This truth and that truth cannot be all true,
\q1 And yet all of them are not entirely astray in error.
\q1 Because error occurs not without some truth,
\q1 Fools buy base coins from their likeness to real coins.
\q1 If there were no genuine coins current in the world,
\q1 How could coiners succeed in passing false coins?
\q1 If there were no truth, how could falsehood exist?
\q1 Falsehood derives its plausibility from truth.
\q1 'Tis the desire of right that makes men buy wrong;
\q1 Let poison be mixed with sugar, and they eat it at once.
\q1 If wheat were not valued as sweet and good for food,
\q1 The cheat who shows wheat and sells barley would make no profit!
\q1 Say not, then, that all these creeds are false,
\q1 The false ones ensnare hearts by the scent of truth.
\q1 Say not that they are all erroneous fancies,
\q1 There is no fancy in the universe without some truth.
\q1 Truth is the "night of power " hidden amongst other nights,
\q1 In order to try the spirit of every night.
\q1 Not every night is that of power, O youth,
\q1 Nor yet is every night quite void of power.
\q1 In the crowd of rag-wearers there is but one Faqir
\q1 Search well and find out that true one.
\q1 Tell the wary and discerning believer
\q1 To distinguish the king from the beggar.
\q1 If there were no bad goods in the world,
\q1 Every fool might be a skilful merchant;
\q1 For then the hard art of judging goods would be easy.
\q1 If there were no faults, one man could judge as well as another.
\q1 Again, if all were faulty, skill would be profitless.
\q1 If all wood were common, there would be no aloes.
\q1 He who accepts everything as true is a fool,
\q1 But he who says all is false is a knave.
\v 25 \vp 13.1\vp* Fools laud and magnify the mosque,
\q1 While they strive to oppress holy men of heart.
\q1 But the former is mere form, the latter spirit and truth.
\q1 The only true mosque is that in the hearts of saints.
\q1 The mosque that is built in the hearts of the saints
\q1 Is the place of worship of all, for God dwells there.
\q1 So long as the hearts of the saints are not afflicted,
\q1 God never destroys the nation.
\q1 Our forefathers lifted their hands against the prophets;
\q1 Seeing their bodies, they took them for ordinary men.
\q1 In you also abide the morals of those men of old;
\q1 How can you avoid fearing that you will act like them?
\q1 The morals of those unthankful ones dwell in you,
\q1 Your urn will not always return unbroken from the well.
\q1 Seeing that all these bad symptoms are seen in you,
\q1 And that you are one with those men, how can you escape?
\v 26 \vp 15.1\vp* That person said in the time of Shu'aib (Jethro),
\q1 "God has seen many faults done by me;
\q1 Yea, how many sins and faults of mine has He seen,
\q1 Nevertheless of His mercy He punishes me not."
\q1 God Almighty spake in the ear of Shu'aib,
\q1 Addressing him with an inner voice in answer thereto,
\q1 "Why hast thou said I have sinned so much,
\q1 And God of His mercy has not punished my sins?"
\q1 Thou sayest the very reverse of the truth, O fool!
\q1 Wandering from the way and lost in the desert!
\q1 How many times do I smite thee, and thou knowest not?
\q1 Thou art bound in my chains from head to foot.
\q1 On thy heart is rust on rust collected,
\q1 So thou art blind to mysteries.
\q1 Thy rust, layer on layer, O black kettle!
\q1 Makes the aspect of thy inner parts foul.
\q1 If that smoke touched a new kettle,
\q1 It would show the smut, were it only as a grain of barley;
\q1 For everything is made manifest by its opposite,
\q1 In contrast with its whiteness that black shows foul.
\q1 But when the kettle is black, then afterwards
\q1 Who can see on it the impression of the smoke?
\q1 If the blacksmith be a negro,
\q1 His face agrees in colour with the smoke.
\q1 But if a man of Rum does blacksmith's work,
\q1 His face becomes grimed by the smoke fumes;
\q1 Then he quickly perceives the impression of his fault,
\q1 So that he wails and cries 'O Allah!'
\q1 When he is stubborn and follows his evil practices,
\q1 He casts dust in the eyes of his discernment.
\q1 He recks not of repentance, and, moreover, that sin
\q1 Becomes dear to his heart, so that he becomes without faith,
\q1 Old shame for sin and calling on God quit him,
\q1 Rust five layers deep settles on his mirror,
\q1 Rust spots begin to gnaw his iron,
\q1 The colour in his jewel grows less and less.
\q1 When you write on white paper,
\q1 What is written is read at a glance;
\q1 But when you write on the face of a written page,
\q1 It is not plain, reading it is deceptive;
\q1 For that black is written on the top of other black,
\q1 Both the writings are illegible and senseless.
\q1 Or if, in the third place, you write on the page,
\q1 And then blacken it like an infidel's soul,
\q1 Then what remedy but the aid of the Remedier?
\q1 Despair is copper and sight its elixir.
\q1 Lay your despair before Him,
\q1 That you may escape from pain without medicine."
\q1 When Shu'aib spoke these aphorisms to him,
\q1 From that breath of the soul roses bloomed in his heart,
\q1 His soul heard the revelations of heaven;
\q1 He said, "If He has punished me, where is the sign of it?"
\q1 Shu'aib said, "O Lord, he repels my arguments,
\q1 He seeks for a sign of that punishment."
\q1 The Veiler of sins replied, "I will tell him no secrets,
\q1 Save only one, in order to try him.
\q1 One sign that I punish him is this,
\q1 That he observes obedience and fasting and prayer,
\q1 And devotions and almsgiving, and so on,
\q1 Yet never feels the least expansion of soul.
\q1 He performs the devotions and acts enjoined by law,
\q1 Yet derives not an atom of relish from them.
\q1 Outward devotion is sweet to him, spirit is not sweet,
\q1 Nuts in plenty, but no kernel in any of them.
\q1 Relish is needed for devotions to bear fruit,
\q1 Kernels are needed that seeds may yield trees.
\q1 How can seeds without kernels become trees?
\q1 Form without soul (life) is only a dream."
\v 27 \vp 16.1\vp* He said, "Though the path of the mean is wisdom,
\q1 Yet is this same mean also relative.
\q1 The water which is insufficient for a camel
\q1 Is like an ocean to a mouse.
\q1 Whoso has four loaves as his daily allowance,
\q1 Whether he eat two or three, he observes the mean.
\q1 But if he eat all four he transgresses the mean,
\q1 A very slave to greed, and voracious as a duck.
\q1 Whoso has an appetite for ten loaves,
\q1 Know, though he eat six, he observes the mean.
\q1 If I have an appetite for fifty loaves,
\q1 While you can manage only six, we are not on a par.
\q1 You are wearied with ten prostrations in prayer,
\q1 Whilst I can endure five hundred.
\q1 Such an one goes barefoot to the Ka'ba,
\q1 Whilst another faints with going to the mosque."
\v 28 \vp 16.2\vp* "At times my state resembles a dream,
\q1 My dreaming seems to them infidelity.
\q1 Know my eyes sleep, but my heart is awake;
\q1 My body, though torpid, is instinct with energy.
\q1 The Prophet said, 'Mine eyes sleep,
\q1 But my heart is awake with the Lord of mankind.'
\q1 Your eyes are awake and your heart fast asleep,
\q1 My eyes are closed, and my heart at the 'open door.'
\q1 My heart has other five senses of its own;
\q1 These senses of my heart view the two worlds.
\q1 Let not a weakling like you censure me,
\q1 What seems night to you is broad day to me;
\q1 What seems a prison to you is a garden to me.
\q1 Busiest occupation is rest to me.
\q1 Your feet are in the mire, to me mire is rose,
\q1 What to you is funeral wailing is marriage drum to me.
\q1 While I seem on earth, abiding with you in the house,
\q1 I ascend like Saturn to the seventh heaven.
\q1 'Tis not I who companion with you, 'tis my shadow;
\q1 My exaltation transcends your thoughts,
\q1 Because I have transcended thought,
\q1 Yea, I have sped beyond reach of thought.
\q1 I am lord of thought, not overlorded by thought,
\q1 As the builder is lord of the building.
\q1 All creatures are enslaved to thought;
\q1 For this cause are they sad at heart and sorrowful.
\q1 I send myself on an embassy to thought,
\q1 And, at will, spring back again from thought.
\q1 I am as the bird of heaven and thought as the fly,
\q1 How can the fly lend a helping hand to me?
\q1 Whoso has in him a spark of the light of Omnipotence,
\q1 However much he eats, say ' Eat on;' 'tis lawful to him."
\v 29 \vp 16.3\vp* "If you are a true lover of my soul,
\q1 This truth-fraught saying of mine is no vain pretence,
\q1 'Though I talk half the night I am superior to you;'
\q1 And again, 'Fear not the night; here am I, your kinsman.'
\q1 These two assertions of mine will both seem true to you
\q1 The moment you recognise the voice of your kinsman.
\q1 Superiority and kinsmanship are both mere assertions,
\q1 Yet both are recognised for truth by men of clear wit.
\q1 The nearness of the voice proves to such an one
\q1 That the voice proceeds from a friend who is near.
\q1 The sweetness of the kinsman's voice, too, O beloved,
\q1 Proves the veracity of that kinsman.
\q1 But the uninspired fool who from ignorance
\q1 Cannot tell the voice of a stranger from a friend's,—
\q1 To him the friend's saying seems a vain pretension,
\q1 His ignorance is the material cause of his disbelief.
\q1 To the wise, whose hearts are enlightened,
\q1 The mere sound of that voice proves its truth."
\q1 "When you say to a thirsty man, 'Come quickly;
\q1 This is water in the cup, take and drink it,'
\q1 Does the thirsty man say, 'This is a vain pretension;
\q1 Go, remove yourself from me, O vain pretender,
\q1 Or proceed to give proofs and evidence
\q1 That this is generic water, and concrete water thereof'?
\q1 Or when a mother cries to her sucking babe,
\q1 'Come, O son, I am thy mother,'
\q1 Does the babe answer, 'O mother, show a proof
\q1 That I shall find comfort from taking thy milk'?
\q1 In the hearts of every sect that has a taste of the truth
\q1 The sight and the voice of prophets work miracles.
\q1 When the prophets raise their cry to the outward ear,
\q1 The souls of each sect bow in devotion within;—
\q1 Because never in this world hath the soul's ear
\q1 Heard from any man the like of that cry.
\q1 That poor man in that strange sweet voice
\q1 Recognises the voice of God, 'Verily I am nigh.'"
\v 30 \vp 17.1\vp* The Shaikh laughed, and said to him, "O friend,
\q1 This is the tree of knowledge, O knowing one;
\q1 Very high, very fine, very expansive,
\q1 The very water of life from the circumfluent ocean.
\q1 Thou hast run after form, O ill-informed one,
\q1 Wherefore thou lackest the fruit of the tree of substance.
\q1 Sometimes it is named tree, sometimes sun,
\q1 Sometimes lake, and sometimes cloud.
\q1 'Tis one, though it has thousands of manifestations;
\q1 Its least manifestation is eternal life!
\q1 Though 'tis one, it has a thousand manifestations,
\q1 The names that fit that one are countless.
\q1 That one is to thy personality a father,
\q1 In regard to another person He may be a son.
\q1 In relation to another He may be wrath and vengeance,
\q1 In relation to another, mercy and goodness.
\q1 He has thousands of names, yet is One,
\q1 Answering to all of His descriptions, yet indescribable.
\q1 Every one who seeks names, if he is a man of credulity,
\q1 Like thee, remains hopeless and frustrated of his aim.
\q1 Why cleavest thou to this mere name of tree,
\q1 So that thou art utterly balked and disappointed?
\q1 Pass over names and look to qualities,
\q1 So that qualities may lead thee to essence!
\q1 The differences of sects arise from His names;
\q1 When they pierce to His essence they find His peace!"
\v 31 \vp 18.1\vp* Although a domestic fowl may have taken thee,
\q1 Who art a duckling, under her wing and nurtured thee,
\q1 Thy mother was a duck of that ocean,
\q1 Thy nurse was earthy, and her wing dry land.
\q1 The longing for the ocean which fills thy heart,—
\q1 That natural longing of thy soul comes from thy mother.
\q1 Thy longing for dry land comes to thee from thy nurse;
\q1 Quit thy nurse, for she will lead thee astray.
\q1 Leave thy nurse on the dry land and push on,
\q1 Enter the ocean of real Being, like the ducks!
\q1 Though thy nurse may frighten thee away from water,
\q1 Do thou fear not, but haste on into the ocean!
\q1 Thou art a duck, and flourishest on land and water,
\q1 And dost not, like a domestic fowl, dig up the house.
\q1 Thou art a king of "the sons of Adam honoured by God," 
\q1 And settest foot alike on sea and land;—
\q1 For impress on thy mind, "We have carried them by sea,"
\q1 Before the words, "We have carried them by land."
\q1 The angels go not on dry land,
\q1 And the animals know nothing of the sea;
\q1 Thou in body art an animal, in thy soul an angel;
\q1 Hence thou goest both upon earth and on heaven."
\q1 Hence to outward view "He is a man like you,"
\q1 While to his sharp-seeing heart "it hath been revealed."
\q1 His earthy form has fallen on earth,
\q1 His spirit revolving above highest heaven.
\q1 O boy, we are all of us waterfowl,
\q1 The sea knows full well our language.
\q1 Solomon is, as it were, that sea, and we as the birds;
\q1 In Solomon we hold our course to eternity.
\q1 Along with Solomon plunge into the ocean,
\q1 Then, like David, the water will make us coats-of-mail.
\q1 That Solomon is present to every one,
\q1 But negligence closes their eyes and bewitches them.
\q1 Hence, through ignorance, sloth, and folly,
\q1 Though he stands hard by us, we are shut off from him.
\q1 The noise of thunder makes the head of the thirsty ache;
\q1 When he knows not that it unlocks the blessed showers,
\q1 His eyes are fixed on the running stream,
\q1 Unwitting of the sweetness of the rain from heaven.
\q1 He urges the steed of his desire towards the caused,
\q1 And perforce remains shut off from the Causer.
\q1 Whoso beholds the Causer face to face,
\q1 How can he set his heart on things caused on earth?
\c 3
\cp 3
\p
\v 1 \vp 1.1\vp* O son, the pious are God's children,
\q1 Absent or present He is informed of their state.
\q1 Deem Him not absent when they are endangered,
\q1 For He is jealous for their lives.
\q1 He saith, "These saints are my children,
\q1 Though remote and alone and away from their Lord.
\q1 For their trial they are orphans and wretched,
\q1 Yet in love I am ever holding communion with them.
\q1 Thou art backed by all my protection,
\q1 My children are, as it were, parts of me.
\q1 Verily these Darveshes of mine
\q1 Are thousands on thousands, and yet no more than One;
\q1 For if not, how did Moses with one magic staff
\q1 Turn the realm of Pharaoh upside down?
\q1 And if it were not so, how did Noah with one curse
\q1 Make East and West alike drowned in his flood?
\q1 Nor could one prayer of eloquent Lot
\q1 Have razed their strong city against their will,—
\q1 Their mighty city, like to Paradise,
\q1 Became as a Tigris of black water; go, see its vestige!
\q1 Towards Syria is this vestige and memorial,
\q1 Thou seest it in passing on the way to Jerusalem.
\q1 Thousands of God-fearing prophets
\q1 In every age hold divine chastisements in hand.
\q1 Should I tell of them my limits would be exceeded,
\q1 And not hearts only but very hills would bleed."
\v 2 \vp 1.2\vp* Thou art asleep, and the smell of that forbidden fruit
\q1 Ascends to the azure skies,
\q1 Ascends along with thy foul breath,
\q1 Till it overpowers heaven with stench;—
\q1 Stench of pride, stench of lust, stench of greed.
\q1 All these stink like onions when a man speaks.
\q1 Though thou swearest, saying, "When have I eaten?
\q1 Have I not abstained from onions and garlic?"
\q1 The very breath of that oath tells tales,
\q1 As it strikes the nostrils of them that sit with thee.
\q1 So too prayers are made invalid by such stenches,
\q1 That crooked heart is betrayed by its speech.
\q1 The answer to that prayer is, "Be ye driven into hell,"
\q1 The staff of repulsion is the reward of all deceit.
\q1 But, if thy speech be crooked and thy meaning straight,
\q1 Thy crookedness of words will be accepted of God.
\q1 That faithful Bilal, when he called to prayer,
\q1 Would devoutly cry, "Come hither, come hither!"
\q1 At last men said, "O Prophet, this call is not right,
\q1 This is wrong; now, what is thy intention?
\q1 O Prophet, and O ambassador of the Almighty,
\q1 Provide another Mu'azzin of better talent,
\q1 'Tis an error at the beginning of our divine worship
\q1 To utter the words, 'Come to the asylum!'"
\q1 The wrath of the Prophet boiled up, and he said
\q1 (Uttering one or two secrets from the fount of grace),
\q1 "O base ones, in God's sight the 'Ho!' of Bilal
\q1 Is better than a hundred 'Come hithers' and ejaculations.
\q1 Ah! excite not a tumult, lest I tell forth openly
\q1 Your secret thoughts from first to last.
\q1 If ye keep not your breath sweet in prayer,
\q1 Go, desire a prayer from the Brethren of Purity!"
\q1 For this cause spake God to Moses,
\q1 At the time he was asking aid in prayer,
\q1 "O Moses! desire protection of me
\q1 With a mouth that thou hast not sinned withal."
\q1 Moses answered, "I possess not such a mouth."
\q1 God said, "Call upon me with another mouth!
\q1 Act so that all thy mouths
\q1 By night and by day may be raising prayers.
\q1 When thou hast sinned with one mouth,
\q1 With thy other mouth cry, 'O Allah!'
\q1 Or else cleanse thy own mouth,
\q1 And make thy spirit alert and quick.
\q1 Calling on God is pure, and when purity approaches,
\q1 Impurity arises and takes its departure.
\q1 Contraries flee away from contraries;
\q1 When day dawns night takes flight.
\q1 When the pure name (of God) enters the mouth,
\q1 Neither does impurity nor that impure mouth remain!"
\v 3 \vp 1.3\vp* That person one night was crying, "O Allah!"
\q1 That his mouth might be sweetened thereby,
\q1 And Satan said to him, "Be quiet, O austere one!
\q1 How long wilt thou babble, O man of many words?
\q1 No answer comes to thee from nigh the throne,
\q1 How long wilt thou cry 'Allah' with harsh face?"
\q1 That person was sad at heart and hung his head,
\q1 And then beheld Khizr present before him in a vision,
\q1 Who said to him, "Ah! thou hast ceased to call on God,
\q1 Wherefore repentest thou of calling upon Him?"
\q1 The man said, "The answer 'Here am I' came not,
\q1 Wherefore I fear that I am repulsed from the door."
\q1 Khizr replied to him, "God has given me this command;
\q1 Go to him and say, 'O much-tried one,
\q1 Did not I engage thee to do my service?
\q1 Did not I engage thee to call upon me?
\q1 That calling 'Allah' of thine was my 'Here am I,'
\q1 And that pain and longing and ardour of thine my messenger;
\q1 Thy struggles and strivings for assistance
\q1 Were my attractions, and originated thy prayer.
\q1 Thy fear and thy love are the covert of my mercy,
\q1 Each 'O Lord!' of thine contains many 'Here am I's."
\q1 The soul of fools is alien from this calling on God,
\q1 Because it is not their wont to cry, 'O Lord!'
\q1 On their mouths and hearts are locks and bonds,
\q1 That they may not cry to God in time of distress.
\q1 God gave Pharaoh abundance of riches and wealth,
\q1 So that he boasted that he was 'Lord Supreme.'
\q1 In the whole of his life he suffered no headache,
\q1 So that he never cried to God, wretch that he was.
\q1 God granted him the absolute dominion of the world,
\q1 But withheld from him pain and sorrow and cares;
\q1 Because pain and sorrow and loads of cares
\q1 Are the lot of God's friends in the world.
\q1 Pain is better than the dominion of the world,
\q1 So that thou mayest call on God in secret.
\q1 The cries of those free from pain are dull and cold,
\q1 The cries of the sorrowful come from the burning hearts."
\v 4 \vp 2.1\vp* The house of 'Isa was the banquet of men of heart,
\q1 Ho! afflicted one, quit not this door!
\q1 From all sides the people ever thronged,
\q1 Many blind and lame, and halt and afflicted,
\q1 To the door of the house of 'Isa at dawn,
\q1 That with his breath he might heal their ailments.
\q1 As soon as he had finished his orisons,
\q1 That holy one would come forth at the third hour;
\q1 He viewed those impotent folk, troop by troop,
\q1 Sitting at his door in hope and expectation;
\q1 He spoke to them, saying, "O stricken ones!
\q1 The desires of all of you have been granted by God;
\q1 Arise, walk without pain or affliction,
\q1 Acknowledge the mercy and beneficence of God!"
\q1 Then all, as camels whose feet are shackled,
\q1 When you loose their feet in the road,
\q1 Straightway rush in joy and delight to the halting-place,
\q1 So did they run upon their feet at his command.
\q1 How many afflictions caused by thyself to thyself
\q1 Hast thou escaped through these princes of the faith?
\q1 How long that lameness of thine was thy steed!
\q1 How seldom was thy soul void of sorrow and grief!
\q1 O careless straggler, bind a rope upon thy feet,
\q1 Lest thou lose even thine own self!
\q1 But thy ingratitude and unthankfulness
\q1 Forget the honey draught thou hast sipped.
\q1 That road was perforce closed to thee
\q1 When thou didst wound the hearts of the men of heart.
\q1 Quick! clasp them and ask pardon of them;
\q1 Like the clouds, shed tears of lamentation,
\q1 So that their rose-garden may bloom for thee,
\q1 And their ripe fruits burst open of themselves.
\q1 Press around that door, be not viler than a dog,
\q1 If thou wouldest rival the Seven Sleepers' dog.
\v 5 \vp 2.2\vp* Whereas want of fidelity is shameful even in dogs,
\q1 How can it be right in men?
\q1 God Almighty Himself makes boast of fidelity,
\q1 Saying, "Who more faithful to his promise than We?"
\q1 Know, infidelity is fidelity to God's adversary,
\q1 No one has pre-eminence over the rights of God.
\q1 The claims of a mother are less than God's, for He,
\q1 That bounteous One, made her debtor for thy embryo.
\q1 He gave thee a form whilst thou wast in her womb,
\q1 In her womb He gave thee needful rest and nurture.
\q1 He viewed her as a part united to thee,
\q1 Then His wisdom separated that united part.
\q1 God devised a thousand plans and arts,
\q1 To make thy mother lavish affection upon thee.
\q1 Wherefore the claims of God predominate over the mother's,
\q1 Whoso acknowledges not God's claims is a fool.
\q1 He who made mother and breast and milk
\q1 United mother to father also,— despise Him not!
\q1 O Lord, O Ancient of days, Thy mercies,
\q1 Whether known to us or unknown, are all from Thee!
\q1 Thou hast commanded, saying, "Remember thy God,"
\q1 Because God's claims are never exhausted!
\q1 Since thou hast been led astray by faithless men,
\q1 Turn now from thy evil doubts to the opposite mind.
\q1 I am free from error and all faithlessness;
\q1 Thou must come to me and rescind evil doubts.
\q1 Cut off these evil doubts and cast them away,
\q1 For in the presence of such thou becomest double.
\q1 Therefore thou hast chosen harsh friends and companions;
\q1 If I ask where they are thou sayest they are gone.
\q1 The good friend goes up to highest heaven,
\q1 Evil friends sink beneath the bottom of the earth,
\q1 Whilst thou art left alone in the midst, forlorn,
\q1 Even as the fires left by the departed caravan."
\q1 O brave friend, grasp His skirt,
\q1 Who is removed alike from the world above and below;
\q1 Who neither, like Jesus, ascends to heaven,
\q1 Nor, like Korah, sinks into the earth;
\q1 Who will abide with thee in the house and abroad
\q1 When thou lackest house and home.
\q1 He will bring forth peace out of perturbations,
\q1 And when thou art afflicted will keep His promise.
\v 6 \vp 2.3\vp* O son, a hundred thousand tests await thee,
\q1 Whoever thou art who sayest "I am a prince of the gate,"
\q1 If the vulgar detect not such an one by tests,
\q1 Yet the skilled wayfarers seek of him a sign.
\q1 When a man makes pretension to be a tailor,
\q1 The master places before him a piece of silk,
\q1 Saying, "Cut out a large head-dress,"
\q1 And failure in the test leads him to the pillory.
\q1 If all the evil men were not tested,
\q1 Every catamite would through fraud pass for a Rustam.
\q1 Suppose he wears the semblance of one clad in mail,
\q1 Yet when wounded he is at once taken captive.
\q1 The God-intoxicated are not sobered by old age,
\q1 They remain beside themselves till the last trump.
\q1 The wine of God is true, and not false,
\q1 But thou hast drunk only sour whey.
\q1 Thou makest thyself out to be a Junaid or a Bayazid;
\q1 Go! for do I not know a hatchet from a ploughshare?
\q1 O plotter, how canst thou conceal by fraud
\q1 Baseness, sloth, covetousness, and lust?
\q1 Thou holdest thyself out as a lover of God,
\q1 But thou hast coquetted with the evil demon.
\q1 The lover and the beloved on the last day
\q1 Will be joined together and raised in sight of all.
\q1 How foolish and silly thou hast made thyself!
\q1 Thou hast drunk blood of grapes, nay, my blood!
\q1 Go! for I know thee not. Get away!
\q1 I am a lover beside himself, whose words are wild.
\q1 Thou fanciest thyself near to God,
\q1 Saying, "The maker of the dish is not far from the dish."
\q1 Knowest thou not that the nearness of saints to God
\q1 Involves the power to do mighty works and signs?
\q1 Iron was as wax in the hands of David,
\q1 Wax in thy hands is as iron.
\q1 God's nearness and His beneficence are common to all,
\q1 But only eminent saints enjoy inspired love.
\q1 Nearness is of various kinds, O son,
\q1 The sun shines alike on rocks and on gold.
\q1 Yet the sun possesses a nearness to gold,
\q1 Whereof the common willow has no cognisance.
\q1 The dry branch and the green are alike near the sun,
\q1 Does the sun veil himself from either?
\q1 Yet what is the nearness of that green branch,
\q1 Wherefrom thou eatest ripe fruits?
\q1 But as for the dry branch) from its nearness to the sun,
\q1 What does it but more quickly grow dry and sapless?
\q1 Be not intoxicated after the manner of this branch,
\q1 Which, when it becomes sober, has cause for repentance,
\q1 But, like those drunkards who, when they drink wine,
\q1 Bear ripe fruits of wisdom of penitence.
\v 7 \vp 4.1\vp* Lust is that snake; How say you it is dead?
\q1 It is only frozen by the pangs of hunger.
\q1 If it obtains the state of Pharaoh,
\q1 So as to command the (frozen) rivers to flow,
\q1 Straightway it is led to pride like Pharaoh's,
\q1 And it plunders the goods of many a Moses and Aaron.
\q1 Through pressure of want this snake is as a fly,
\q1 It becomes a gnat through wealth and rank and luxury.
\q1 Beware, keep that snake in the frost of humiliation,
\q1 Draw it not forth into the sunshine of 'Iraq!
\q1 So long as that snake is frozen, it is well;
\q1 When it finds release from frost you become its prey.
\q1 Conquer it and save yourself from being conquered,
\q1 Pity it not, it is not one who bears affection.
\q1 For that warmth of the sun kindles its lust,
\q1 And that bat of vileness flaps its wings.
\q1 Slay it in sacred war and combat,
\q1 Like a valiant man will God requite you with union.
\q1 When that man cherished that snake,
\q1 That stubborn brute was happy in the luxury of warmth;
\q1 And of necessity worked destruction, O friend;
\q1 Yea, many more mischiefs than I have told.
\q1 If you wish to keep that snake tied up
\q1 Without trouble, be faithful, be faithful!
\q1 But how can base men attain this wish?
\q1 It requires a Moses to slay serpents;
\q1 And a hundred thousand men were slain by his serpent,
\q1 In dire confusion, according to his purpose.
\v 8 \vp 5.1\vp* The eye of outward sense is as the palm of a hand,
\q1 The whole of the object is not grasped in the palm.
\q1 The sea itself is one thing, the foam another;
\q1 Neglect the foam, and regard the sea with your eyes.
\q1 Waves of foam rise from the sea night and day,
\q1 You look at the foam ripples and not the mighty sea.
\q1 We, like boats, are tossed hither and thither,
\q1 We are blind though we are on the bright ocean.
\q1 Ah! you who are asleep in the boat of the body,
\q1 You see the water; behold the Water of waters!
\q1 Under the water you see there is another Water moving it,
\q1 Within the spirit is a Spirit that calls it.
\q1 Where were Moses and Jesus when that Sun
\q1 Showered down water on the fields sown with corn?
\q1 Where were Adam and Eve what time
\q1 God Almighty fitted the string to His bow?
\q1 The one form of speech is evil and defective;
\q1 The other form, which is not defective, is perfect.
\q1 If I speak thereof your feet stumble,
\q1 Yet if I speak not of it, woe be to you!
\q1 And if I speak in terms of outward form,
\q1 You stick fast in that same form, O son.
\q1 You are footbound like the grass in the ground,
\q1 And your head is shaken by the wind uncertainly.
\q1 Your foot stands not firmly till you move it,
\q1 Nay) till you pluck it not up from the mire.
\q1 When you pluck up your foot you escape from the mire,
\q1 The way to this salvation is very difficult.
\q1 When you obtain salvation at God's hands, O wanderer,
\q1 You are free from the mire, and go your way.
\q1 When the suckling is weaned from its nurse,
\q1 It eats strong meats and leaves the nurse.
\q1 You are bound to the bosom of earth like seeds,
\q1 Strive to be weaned through nutriment of the heart.
\q1 Eat the words of wisdom, for veiled light
\q1 Is not accepted in preference to unveiled light.
\q1 When you have accepted the light, O beloved,
\q1 When you behold what is veiled without a veil,
\q1 Like a star you will walk upon the heavens;
\q1 Nay, though not in heaven, you will walk on high.
\q1 Keep silence, that you may hear Him speaking
\q1 Words unutterable by tongue in speech.
\q1 Keep silence, that you may hear from that Sun
\q1 Things inexpressible in books and discourses.
\q1 Keep silence, that the Spirit may speak to you;
\q1 Give up swimming and enter the ark of Noah;
\q1 Not like Canaan when he was swimming,
\q1 Who said, "I desire not to enter the ark of Noah passing by."
\v 9 \vp 5.2\vp* Noah cried, "Ho! child, come into the ark and rest,
\q1 That you be not drowned in the flood, O weak one."
\q1 Canaan said, "Nay! I have learned to swim,
\q1 I have lit a torch of my own apart from thy torch."
\q1 Noah replied, "Make not light of it, for 'tis the flood of destruction,
\q1 Swimming with hands and feet avails naught today.
\q1 The wind of wrath and the storm blow out torches;
\q1 Except the torch of God, all are extinguished."
\q1 He answered, "Nay! I am going to that high mountain,
\q1 For that will save me from all harm."
\q1 Noah cried, "Beware, do not so, mountains are now as grass;
\q1 Except the Friend none can save thee."
\q1 He answered, "Why should I listen to thy advice?
\q1 For thou desirest to make me one of thy flock.
\q1 Thy speech is by no means pleasing to me,
\q1 I am free from thee in this world and the next."
\q1 Thus the more good advice Noah gave him,
\q1 The more stubborn refusals he returned.
\q1 Neither was his father tired of advising Canaan,
\q1 Nor did his advice make any impression on Canaan;
\q1 While they were yet talking a violent wave
\q1 Smote Canaan's head, and he was overwhelmed.
\v 10 \vp 5.3\vp* Yesterday an inquirer questioned me,
\q1 Since he was interested in the foregoing narrative,
\q1 Saying, "The Prophet, whose words are as a seal,
\q1 Said, 'Acquiescence in infidelity is infidelity.'
\q1 And again, 'Acquiescence in God's ordinance
\q1 Is incumbent on all true believers.'
\q1 Infidelity and hypocrisy are not ordained of God;
\q1 If I acquiesce in them I am at variance with God.
\q1 And yet, if I acquiesce not, that again is wrong;
\q1 What way of escape is there from this dilemma? "
\q1 I said to him, "This infidelity is ordained, not ordinance,
\q1 Though this infidelity is the work of the ordinance.
\q1 Therefore distinguish the ordinance from the ordained,
\q1 That thy difficulty may be at once removed.
\q1 I acquiesce in infidelity so far as it is God's ordinance,
\q1 Not so far as it is our evil and foul passions.
\q1 Infidelity qua ordinance is not infidelity,
\q1 Call not God an infidel. Set not foot in this place.
\q1 Infidelity is folly, ordained infidelity wisdom,
\q1 How can mercy and vengeance be the same?
\q1 Ugliness of the picture is not ugliness of the painter,
\q1 Not so, for he erases ugly pictures.
\q1 The ability of the painter is shown in this,
\q1 That he can paint both ugly and beautiful pictures.
\q1 If I should pursue this argument properly,
\q1 So that questions and answers should be prolonged,
\q1 The unction of the mystery of love would escape me,
\q1 The picture of obedience would become another picture."
\v 11 \vp 5.4\vp* A certain man whose hair was half grey came in haste
\q1 To a barber who was a friend of his,
\q1 Saying, "Pluck out the white hairs from my beard,
\q1 For I have selected a young bride, O my son.
\q1 The barber cut off his beard and laid it before him,
\q1 Saying, "Do you part them, the task is beyond me."
\q1 Questions are white and answers black; do you choose,
\q1 For the man of faith knows not how to choose.
\q1 Thus, one smote Zaid a blow,
\q1 And Zaid attacked him for his treachery.
\q1 The striker said, "Let me first ask you a question,
\q1 Give me an answer to it and then strike me;
\q1 I struck your back and a bruise appeared,
\q1 Now I ask you a question in all kindliness,
\q1 Did this bruise proceed from my hand,
\q1 Or from the smitten part of your back, O complainer?"
\q1 Zaid replied, "Through pain I am not in a condition
\q1 To enter upon thought and consideration of this.
\q1 You, who are free from pain, think this out;
\q1 Such trifling thoughts occur not to a man in pain."
\q1 Men in pain have no time for other thoughts,
\q1 Whether you enter mosque or Christian church.
\q1 Your carelessness and injustice suggest thoughts
\q1 And unprecedented difficulties to your imagination.
\q1 The man in pain cares only for the faith,
\q1 He is aware only of man and his work.
\q1 He set's God's command upon his head and face,
\q1 And for thinking, he puts it aside.
\v 12 \vp 6.1\vp* Whoso is restricted to religious raptures is but a man;
\q1 Sometimes his rapture is excessive, sometimes deficient.
\q1 The Sufi is, as it were, the "son of the season,"
\q1 But the pure (Safi) is exalted above season and state.
\q1 Religious raptures depend on feelings and will,
\q1 But the pure one is regenerated by the breath of Jesus.
\q1 You are a lover of your own raptures, not of me;
\q1 You turn to me only in hope of experiencing raptures.
\q1 Whoso is now defective, now perfect,
\q1 Is not adored by Abraham; he is "one that sets."
\q1 Because the stars set, and are now up, now down,
\q1 He loved them not; "I love not them that set."
\q1 Whoso is now pleasing and now unpleasing
\q1 Is at one time water, at another fire.
\q1 He may be the house of the moon, but not the true moon;
\q1 Or as the picture of a mistress, but not the living one.
\q1 The mere Sufi is the " child of the season;"
\q1 He clings to seasons as to a father,
\q1 But the pure one is drowned in overwhelming love.
\q1 A child of any one is never free from season and state.
\q1 The pure one is drowned in the light that is not begotten,"
\q1 "What begets not and is not begotten" is God.
\q1 Go I seek such love as this, if you are alive;
\q1 If not, you are enslaved by varying seasons.
\q1 Gaze not on your own pictures, fair or ugly,
\q1 Gaze on your love and the object of your desire.
\q1 Gaze not at the sight of your own weakness or vileness,
\q1 Gaze at object of your desire, O exalted one.
\v 13 \vp 7.1\vp* Little is known by any one but the spiritual man,
\q1 Who has in his heart a touchstone of vital truth.
\q1 The others, hovering between two opinions,
\q1 Fly towards their nest on a single wing.
\q1 Knowledge has two wings, opinion only one wing;
\q1 Opinion is weak and lopsided in its flight.
\q1 The bird having but one wing quickly drops down,
\q1 And again flies on two steps or more.
\q1 This bird of opinion goes on rising and falling
\q1 On one wing, in hope to reach his nest.
\q1 When he escapes from opinion and knowledge is seen,
\q1 This bird gains two wings and spreads both of them.
\q1 Afterwards he "goes upright on a straight path,
\q1 Not grovelling on his face or creeping." 
\q1 He flies up on two wings even as the angel Gabriel,
\q1 Free of opinion, of duplicity, and of vain talk.
\q1 Though the whole world say to him,
\q1 "Thou art firm in the road of God's faith,"
\q1 He is not made more ardent by their saying this,
\q1 Nor is his lofty soul inclined from its course.
\q1 And though all say to him, "Thou art in the wrong way,
\q1 Thou thinkest thyself a rock who art but a blade of grass,"
\q1 He relapses not into opinion at their rebukes,
\q1 Nor is he vexed at their malevolence.
\q1 Nay, even if sea and mountains should cry out,
\q1 Saying, "Thou art mated with error,"
\q1 He would not relapse one jot into vain imaginations,
\q1 Nor would he be grieved by the reproaches of his foes.
\v 14 \vp 9.1\vp* Therefore hath God commanded, "Make an exception,
\q1 Couple the words 'If God will' with your vows.
\q1 Because the governance of actions is in my hands,
\q1 The wills of all are subject to my will.
\q1 Every moment I impart a fresh bias to the heart,
\q1 Every instant I set a fresh mark on the heart;
\q1 Each day I am engaged in a fresh work,
\q1 There is naught that swerves from my purpose."
\q1 There is a tradition, "The heart is like a feather
\q1 In the desert, which is borne captive by the winds;
\q1 The wind drives it everywhere at random,
\q1 Now to right and now to left in opposite directions."
\q1 According to another tradition, know the heart is like
\q1 To water in a kettle boiling on the fire.
\q1 So every moment a fresh purpose occurs to the heart,
\q1 Not proceeding from itself, but from its situation.
\q1 Why, then, are you confident about the heart's purposes?
\q1 Why make you vows only to be covered with shame?
\q1 All these changes proceed from the effect of God's will;
\q1 Although you see the pit, you cannot avoid it.
\q1 The strange thing is, not that winged fowl
\q1 Fall into the deadly snare without seeing it,
\q1 But that they see the snare and the limed twig,
\q1 And yet fall into it, whether they will or no;
\q1 Their eyes and ears are open and the snare is in front,
\q1 Yet they fly into the snare with their own wings!
\v 15 \vp 9.2\vp* Behold that king's son clad in rags,
\q1 With bare head and fallen into distress;
\q1 Consumed by lusts and riotous living,
\q1 Having sold all his clothes and substance;
\q1 Having lost house and home, utterly disgraced,
\q1 Fulfilling the desire of his enemies by his disgrace.
\q1 If he sees a pious man he cries, "O sir,
\q1 Aid me, for the love of God;
\q1 For I have fallen into this dire disgrace;
\q1 I have squandered goods and gold and wealth.
\q1 Aid me so that perchance I may escape hence,
\q1 And extricate myself from this deep slough."
\q1 He repeats this prayer to high and low,
\q1 " Release me, release me, release me!"
\q1 His eyes and ears are open, and he is free from bonds,
\q1 No jailer watches him, no chain binds him;
\q1 What, then, is the bond from which he asks release?
\q1 What is the prison from which he seeks an exit?
\q1 'Tis the bond of God's purpose and hidden decrees;
\q1 Ah! none but the pure in sight can see that bond;
\q1 Though not visible, that bond exists in concealment;
\q1 'Tis more stringent than prison or chains of iron,
\q1 For the mason can pull down prison walls,
\q1 And blacksmiths can break asunder iron chains;
\q1 But, strange to say, this ponderous hidden bond,—
\q1 Blacksmiths are impotent to break this asunder!
\q1 Ahmad alone could see that bond on Omm Jahil's back,
\q1 And the rope of palm fibre bound upon her neck;
\q1 Yea, he saw wood on the back of the wife of Bu Lahab,
\q1 And she, the bearer of the firewood, said it was heavy.
\q1 No eye but his saw that rope and that firewood,
\q1 For to him things unseen were visible.
\q1 The others explained it, saying
\q1 That Ahmad was beside himself, and they in their senses.
\q1 Nevertheless from the weight of the load her back bent,
\q1 And she complained of its weight before him,
\q1 Saying, "Aid me to escape from this load,
\q1 And to shake off this grievous burden."
\q1 He who sees clearly these indications,
\q1 Does he not know also the doomed from the elect?
\q1 Yea, he knows them, yet conceals it by command of God,
\q1 Since God permits him not to reveal it.
\v 16 \vp 10.1\vp* He turned to his wife and said, "O dame,
\q1 The harvest of December is not as that of July;
\q1 Though they be dead or though they be living,
\q1 Are they not equally visible to the eyes of the heart?
\q1 I behold them clearly before me,
\q1 Wherefore should I disfigure my countenance like you?
\q1 Though they have gone forth by revolution of fortune,
\q1 They are with me still, playing round me.
\q1 The cause of lamentation is separation or parting,
\q1 But I am still with my dear ones, and embrace them.
\q1 Ordinary people may see them in dreams,
\q1 But I see them clearly, though wide awake.
\q1 I conceal myself a while from this world,
\q1 I shake down the leaves of outward sense from the tree.
\q1 Know, O wife, outward sense is captive to reason,
\q1 And reason, again, is captive to spirit.
\q1 Spirit unlooses the chained hands of reason;
\q1 Yea, it opens all things that are closed.
\q1 Sensations and thoughts resemble weeds
\q1 Which occupy the surface of pure water.
\q1 The hand of reason puts these weeds aside,
\q1 And the pure water is then visible to the wise.
\q1 Weeds in plenty cover the stream like bubbles;
\q1 When they are swept aside, the water is seen;
\q1 But when God unlooses not the hands of reason,
\q1 The weeds on our water grow thick through carnal lust;
\q1 Yea, they cover up your water more and more,
\q1 While your lust is smiling and your reason weeping.
\q1 When fear of God binds the hands of lust,
\q1 Then God unlooses the two hands of reason.
\q1 Then the powerful senses are subdued by you,
\q1 When you submit to reason as your commander.
\q1 Then your sleepless sense is lulled into sleep,
\q1 That mysteries may appear to the soul.
\q1 You behold visions when broad awake,
\q1 And the gates of heaven are open before you."
\v 17 \vp 11.1\vp* He said, "This at least is notorious to all men,
\q1 That the world obeys the command of God.
\q1 Not a leaf falls from a tree
\q1 Without the decree and command of that Lord of lords;
\q1 Not a morsel goes from the mouth down the throat
\q1 Till God says to it, 'Go down.'
\q1 Desire and appetite, which are the reins of mankind,
\q1 Are themselves subservient to the rule of God.
\q1 Hear this much, that, whereas the totality of actions
\q1 Is not effected without God's direction,
\q1 When the decree of God becomes the pleasure of man,
\q1 Then man desires the fulfilment of God's decrees;
\q1 And this too spontaneously, not in hope of reward,
\q1 But because his very nature is congruous therewith.
\q1 He desires not even his own life for himself,
\q1 Nor is he relying on the hope of sweets of life to come.
\q1 Whatever path is taken by the eternal decree,
\q1 Whether it be life or death, 'tis all one to him.
\q1 He lives for the sake of God, not for wealth;
\q1 He dies for the sake of God, not in fear and grief.
\q1 His faith is based on his desire to do God's will,
\q1 Not on hope to gain paradise with its groves and founts.
\q1 His avoidance of infidelity is also for God's sake,
\q1 It proceeds not from fear of falling into the fire.
\q1 Thus this temper of his arises from his very nature,
\q1 Not from any discipline and endeavour of his own.
\q1 At times he laughs when he contemplates God's pleasure,
\q1 God's decrees are to him as sweetmeats of sugar.
\q1 I ask, does not the world march agreeably to the will
\q1 And commands of a man rejoicing in this disposition?
\q1 Why, then, should such an one make prayers and petitions,
\q1 Saying, 'O God, change such and such a decree?'
\q1 His own death and his children's deaths
\q1 For God's sake seem to him as sweets in the mouth.
\q1 In the view of that faithful one his children's deaths
\q1 Are as sweetmeats to a starving beggar.
\q1 Why, therefore, should he make prayers
\q1 Unless he pray for what is pleasing to God?
\q1 These prayers and petitions, not those of self-pity
\q1 Make that man to be endued with salvation.
\q1 He utterly burned up all his self-pity,
\q1 At the time when he lit the lamp of love to God.
\q1 His love was the hell that burned up his inclinations;
\q1 Yea, he burned up his own inclinations one by one."
\v 18 \vp 12.1\vp* That Daquqi possessed a sweet aspect,
\q1 As a lover of God and a worker of miracles.
\q1 He resembled the moon of heaven come down on earth,
\q1 He was as a light to them that walked in darkness.
\q1 He rarely tarried in one place,
\q1 And seldom stayed two days in one village.
\q1 He said, "If I tarry in one house two days,
\q1 Attachment to that house becomes a passion with me.
\q1 I guard myself from being deceived into loving a home;
\q1 Up! Soul, and travel in search of eternal wealth.
\q1 My heart's inclination is not satisfied by houses,
\q1 So that they should be places of temptation for me."
\q1 Thus by day he traveled, and by night prayed,
\q1 His eyes were always gazing on the King as a falcon's;
\q1 Cut off from mankind, though not for any fault,
\q1 Severed from men and women, though not for baseness;
\q1 Having compassion on mankind, and wholesome as water,
\q1 A kind intercessor, and one whose prayers were heard.
\q1 Benevolent to the good and the bad, and a firm ally,
\q1 Better than a mother, and kinder than a father.
\q1 The Prophet said, "To you, O blessed ones,
\q1 I am as a father, affectionate and indulgent;
\q1 For this cause, that you are all portions of me."
\q1 Wherefore should you tear away the parts from the whole?
\q1 If the part be severed from its whole it is useless;
\q1 If a limb be rent from the body it dies.
\q1 Till it is again joined to its whole,
\q1 'Tis a dead thing, and a stranger to life.
\q1 Thus Daquqi, in devotions and praises and prayers,
\q1 Was ever seeking the particular favourites of God.
\q1 Throughout his long journeys his object was this,
\q1 To interchange a word with the favourites of God.
\q1 He cried continually as he went his way,
\q1 "O Lord, let me draw near to Thy chosen ones!"
\q1 So Daquqi (the mercy of God be upon him!)
\q1 Said, "I journeyed long time to East and to West,
\q1 I journeyed years and months for love of that Moon,
\q1 Heedless of the way, absorbed in God.
\q1 With bare feet I trod upon thorns and flints,
\q1 Seeing I was bewildered, and beside myself, and senseless.
\q1 Think not my feet touched the earth,
\q1 For the lover verily travels with the heart.
\q1 What knows the heart of road and stages?
\q1 What of distant and near, while it is drunk with love?
\q1 Distance and nearness are attributes of bodies,
\q1 The journeyings of spirits are after another sort.
\q1 You journeyed from the embryo state to rationality
\q1 Without footsteps or stages or change of place,
\q1 The journey of the soul involves not time and place.
\q1 And my body learnt from the soul its mode of journeying,
\q1 Now my body has renounced the bodily mode of journeying;
\q1 It journeys secretly and without form, though under a form."
\q1 He added, "One day I was thus filled with longing
\q1 To behold in human form the splendours of 'The Friend,'
\q1 To witness the Ocean gathered up into a drop,
\q1 The Sun compressed into a single atom;
\q1 And when I drew near to the shore of the sea
\q1 The day was drawing to a close."
\v 19 \vp 12.2\vp* In the adorations and benedictions of righteous men
\q1 The praises of all the prophets are kneaded together.
\q1 All their praises are mingled into one stream,
\q1 All the vessels are emptied into one ewer.
\q1 Because He that is praised is, in fact, only One,
\q1 In this respect all religions are only one religion.
\q1 Because all praises are directed towards God's light,
\q1 Their various forms and figures are borrowed from it.
\q1 Men never address praises but to One deemed worthy,
\q1 They err only through mistaken opinions of Him.
\q1 So, when a light falls upon a wall,
\q1 That wall is a connecting-link between all its beams;
\q1 Yet when it casts that reflection back to its source,
\q1 It wrongly shows great as small, and halts in its praises.
\q1 Or if the moon be reflected in a well,
\q1 And one looks down the well, and mistakenly praises it,
\q1 In reality he is intending to praise the moon,
\q1 Although, through ignorance, he is looking down the well.
\q1 The object of his praises is the moon, not its reflection;
\q1 His infidelity arises from mistake of the circumstances.
\q1 That well-meaning man goes wrong through his mistake;
\q1 The moon is in heaven, and he fancies it in the well.
\q1 By these false idols mankind are perplexed,
\q1 And driven by vain lusts to their sorrow.
\v 20 \vp 7.2\vp* He of himself lifted the veil that hid his crime;
\q1 Had he not done so, God would have kept it hidden.
\q1 Criminals and sinners, even in the course of sinning,
\q1 Themselves rend the coverings of their crimes.
\q1 Their sins are veiled among the heart's secrets,
\q1 Yet the criminal himself exposes them to view,
\q1 Saying, "Behold me wearing a pair of horns,
\q1 A cow of hell in sight of all men."
\q1 Thus, even here, in the midst of thy sin, thy hand and foot
\q1 Bear witness of the secrets of thy heart.
\q1 Thy secret thought is as a governor who says to thee,
\q1 "Tell forth thy convictions, withhold them not;"
\q1 Especially in seasons of passion and angry talk
\q1 It betrays thy secrets one by one.
\q1 Thy secret sins and crimes govern hand and foot,
\q1 Saying, "Disclose us to men, O hand and foot!"
\q1 And since these witnesses take the bit in their mouths,
\q1 Especially in times of passion and wrath and revenge,
\q1 Therefore the same God who appointed this governor
\q1 To blazen forth thy secret sins to the world
\q1 Is also able to create many more governors
\q1 To divulge thy secret sins on the day of judgment.
\q1 O man whose only handiwork is crime and sin,
\q1 Thy secret sins are manifest; no divulging is needed.
\q1 There is no need to proclaim thy sins,
\q1 All men are cognisant of thy sin-burnt heart.
\q1 Thy soul every moment casts up sparks of fire,
\q1 Which say, "See me a man destined to the fire;
\q1 I am a part of the fire, and go to join my whole;
\q1 Not a light, so that I should join the Source of light."
\v 21 \vp 7.3\vp* Kill thine own lust and give life to the world;
\q1 It has killed its lord, reduce it to servitude.
\q1 That claimant of the cow is thy lust; Beware!
\q1 It has made itself lord and master.
\q1 That slayer of the cow is thy reason; Go!
\q1 Be not obdurate to the prayers of him that kills the cow.
\q1 Reason is a poor captive, and ever cries to God
\q1 For meat on its dish without labouring and toiling.
\q1 On what depends its getting meat without toiling?
\q1 On its killing the cow of the body, the source of evil.
\q1 Lust says, "Why hast thou killed my cow?"
\q1 It says, "Because lust's cow is the form of the body."
\q1 Reason, the Lord's child, has become a pauper,
\q1 Lust, the murderer, has become a lord and chief.
\q1 Know'st thou what is meat untoiled for?
\q1 'Tis the food of spirits and the aliment of the Prophet.
\q1 But it is attainable only by slaying the cow;
\q1 Treasure is gained by digging, O digger of treasure!
\v 22 \vp 13.1\vp* The faculty of using similitudes is peculiar to a saint
\q1 Who is signally marked by knowledge of hidden mysteries.
\q1 What know you of the mystery hid in aught, that you
\q1 In your folly should use similitudes of curl and cheek?
\q1 Moses took his staff to be a stick, though it was not;
\q1 It was a serpent, and its mystery was revealed.
\q1 If a saint such as he knew not the mystery of a stick,
\q1 What know you of the mystery of the snare and grains?
\q1 When the eye of a Moses erred as to a similitude,
\q1 How can a presumptuous mouse understand one?
\q1 Those similitudes of yours are changed into serpents
\q1 To tear you into pieces with their jaws.
\q1 Such a parable did cursed Iblis use,
\q1 So that he became cursed of God till the day of doom.
\q1 Such a parable did Korah use in his argument,
\q1 So that he was swallowed up in the earth with his wealth.
\q1 Such parables know to be as crows and owls,
\q1 Whereby a hundred households are annihilated.
\q1 When Noah was building the ark in the desert,
\q1 A hundred parable-mongers attacked him with irony,
\q1 Saying, "In the desert, where is no water or well,
\q1 He builds a boat! What ignorant folly is this!"
\v 23 \vp 13.2\vp* The men of Saba said, "O preachers, enough!
\q1 What you say is enough, if there are any wise here.
\q1 God has placed a 'lock upon our hearts,'
\q1 And no man can overcome the Creator.
\q1 That great Painter has painted us thus;
\q1 His painting cannot be altered by argument.
\q1 Keep telling forever a stone to become a ruby,
\q1 Keep telling forever the old to become young!
\q1 Tell earth to assume the quality of water,
\q1 Bid water to become honey or milk!
\q1 God is the creator of heaven and them that dwell therein;
\q1 Also of water and of earth, and them that dwell therein;
\q1 To heaven He gave its revolutions and its purity,
\q1 To the earth its dark look and appearance.
\q1 Can the heaven will to become as dregs?
\q1 Can earth will to assume the clearness of pure wine?
\q1 That Person has assigned 'to each its lot,
\q1 Can mountain by endeavour become as grass?"
\q1 The prophets answered, "Verily God has created
\q1 Some qualities in you which you cannot alter;
\q1 But He has created other accidental qualities,
\q1 Which, being objectionable, may be made good.
\q1 Bid stone become gold— that is impossible;
\q1 Bid copper become gold— that is possible.
\q1 Bid sand bloom as a rose— it cannot;
\q1 Bid dust turn to mud— that is within its capacity.
\q1 God has sent some pains for which there is no cure,
\q1 Such, for instance, as lameness, loss of nose, and blindness.
\q1 God has sent other pains for which there are cures,
\q1 To wit, crooked mouth and headache.
\q1 God has ordained these remedies of His mercy;
\q1 The use of these in pain and anguish is not in vain.
\q1 Nay, the majority of pains may be cured;
\q1 When you seek those cures earnestly you find them."
\q1 The men of Saba replied, "O men, these pains of ours
\q1 Are not of the sort 'that admit of cure.
\q1 Long time ye utter these presages and warnings,
\q1 But our bonds are made thereby heavier every moment.
\q1 If our sickness admitted of a cure,
\q1 It would certainly have been lessened by your spells.
\q1 When the body is obstructed water reaches not the liver,
\q1 Though one drinks the ocean, it passes elsewhere.
\q1 Then of course the hands and feet become dropsical,
\q1 And yet that draught does not quench his thirst."
\q1 The prophets replied, "To despair is wrong,
\q1 The mercy and grace of God are boundless.
\q1 One must not despair of the grace of such a Benefactor,
\q1 One must cling to the stirrup-straps of God.
\q1 Ah! many are the conditions which at first are hard,
\q1 But, are afterwards relieved and lose their harshness.
\q1 Oftentimes hope succeeds to hopelessness,
\q1 Many times does sunlight succeed to darkness.
\q1 We admit that ye are weighted as with stones,
\q1 _And that ye have locks upon your ears and your hearts.
\q1 No condition of ours is altogether as we wish,
\q1 Our business is to be resigned and to obey.
\q1 God has enjoined this servitude upon us;
\q1 We say not this merely on our own authority.
\q1 We enjoy life on condition of doing His will;
\q1 If He bids us, we sow our seed upon the sand.
\q1 The soul of the prophet cares for naught but God,
\q1 It has naught to do with approving or disapproving His works."
\q1 The men of Saba replied, "If ye yourselves are happy,
\q1 Ye make us miserable and annoy and disturb us.
\q1 Our souls were void of all anxieties,
\q1 And ye have plunged us into cares and anxieties.
\q1 The comfort and harmony which we enjoyed heretofore
\q1 Have been rent in pieces by your evil presages.
\q1 We used to be as parrots munching sugar,
\q1 Ye have made us as fowls brooding on death.
\q1 On every side stories inspiring anxiety,
\q1 On every side sounds exciting fears:
\q1 On every side in the world an evil presage,
\q1 On every side evil portents threatening punishment:
\q1 This is the burden of your parables and presages,
\q1 This the purport of your awe-inspiring stories."
\q1 The prophets replied, "Our evil presages
\q1 Are corroborated by the state of your souls.
\q1 Suppose you are sleeping in a place of danger,
\q1 And serpents are drawing near to bite your heads,
\q1 A kind friend will inform you of your danger,
\q1 Saying, 'Jump up, lest the serpent devour you.'
\q1 You reply, 'Why do you utter evil presages?'
\q1 He answers, 'What presage? Up, and see for yourself!
\q1 By means of this evil presage I rouse you,
\q1 And release you from danger and lead you to your home.'
\q1 Like a prophet he warns you of hidden danger,
\q1 For a prophet sees what worldlings cannot see."
\v 24 \vp 13.3\vp* If you do a kindness to a generous man, 'tis fitting,
\q1 For each kindness he will return seven hundredfold.
\q1 When you treat a base man with scorn and contumely,
\q1 He will become your slave in all sincerity.
\q1 Infidels when enjoying prosperity do wrong,
\q1 When they are in hell they cry, "O our Lord!"
\q1 For base men are purified when they suffer evil,
\q1 And when they enjoy prosperity they do evil.
\q1 Wherefore the mosque of their devotion is hell,
\q1 As the snare is the fetter of wild fowl.
\q1 The prison is the hermitage of the wicked thief,
\q1 For when he is there he is ever crying to God.
\q1 Whereas the object of man's being is to worship God,
\q1 Hell is ordained as a place of worship for the proud.
\q1 Man has the power to engage in any actions soever,
\q1 But worship of God is the main object of his existence.
\q1 Read the text, "I have not created Jinns and men but to worship me."
\q1 The only object of the world is to worship God.
\q1 Though the object of a book is to teach an art,
\q1 If you make a pillow of it, it serves that purpose too.
\q1 Yet its main object is not to serve as a pillow,
\q1 But to impart knowledge and useful instruction.
\q1 If you use a sword for a tent-peg,
\q1 You prefer the worse use of it to the better.
\q1 Though the object of all men's being is wisdom,
\q1 Yet each man has a different place of worship.
\q1 The place of worship of the noble is nobility,
\q1 The place of worship of the base is degradation.
\q1 Smite the base to make them bow the head,
\q1 Give to the noble to make them repay liberally.
\q1 Inasmuch as the base are evil and arrogant,
\q1 Hell and humbling are the "small gate" for them.
\q1 Verily God has created two places of adoration,
\q1 Hell for the base and increased bliss for the noble.
\q1 Even so Moses made a small gate in Jerusalem,
\q1 To make the Israelites bow the head in entering it.
\v 25 \vp 13.4\vp* The prophets said, "How long, in our benevolence,
\q1 Shall we give to this and that one good advice?
\q1 How long shall we hammer cold iron in vain?
\q1 How long waste breath in blowing into a lattice?
\q1 Men are moved by God's decree and fixed ordinance,
\q1 As sharp-set teeth are caused by heat of belly.
\q1 'Tis Primal Soul that dominates the Second Soul,
\q1 Fish begins to stink at the head, not the tail.
\q1 Yet be advised and keep your steed straight as an arrow,
\q1 When God says 'Proclaim' we must obey.
\q1 O men, ye know not to which party ye belong,
\q1 Exert yourselves then, till ye see which ye are.
\q1 When you place goods upon a ship,
\q1 You do it in trust that the voyage will be prosperous;
\q1 You know not which of the two events will befall you,
\q1 Whether you will be drowned or come safe to land.
\q1 If you say, 'Till I know which will be my fate
\q1 I will not set foot upon the ship;
\q1 Shall I be drowned on the voyage or a survivor?
\q1 Reveal to me in which class I shall be.
\q1 I shall not undertake the voyage on the chance—
\q1 On the bare hope of reaching land, as the rest do.'
\q1 In that case no trade at all will be undertaken by you,
\q1 As the secret of these two events is always hidden.
\q1 The lamp of the heart, that is a timid trader,
\q1 Acquires neither loss nor gain by its ventures.
\q1 Nay, it acquires loss, for it is precluded from gain;
\q1 'Tis the lamp that takes fire that acquires light.
\q1 Since all things are dependent on probability,
\q1 Religion is so first of all, for thereby you find release.
\q1 In this world no knocking at the door is possible
\q1 Save hope, and God knows what is best."
\v 26 \vp 13.5\vp* The final cause of trading is hope or probability,
\q1 When traders work themselves lean as spindles.
\q1 When the merchant goes to his shop in the morning,
\q1 He does so in hope and probability of gaining bread.
\q1 If you have no hope of getting bread, why go?
\q1 There is the fear of loss, since you are not strong.
\q1 But does not this fear of utter loss in your trade
\q1 Become weakened in the course of your exertions?
\q1 You say, "Although the fear of loss is before me,
\q1 Yet I feel greater fear in remaining idle.
\q1 I have a better hope through exerting myself;
\q1 My fear is increased by remaining idle."
\q1 Why then, O faint-hearted one, in the matter of religion
\q1 Are you paralysed by the fear of loss?
\q1 See you not how the traders in this market of ours
\q1 Make large profits, both apostles and saints?
\q1 What a mine of wealth awaits them on leaving it,
\q1 Seeing they make such profits while still here!
\q1 Fire is soft to them as cotton raiment,
\q1 The ocean bears them gently like a porter;
\q1 Iron in their hands is soft as wax,
\q1 The winds are their obedient slaves.
\v 27 \vp 14.1\vp* In this tale there is a warning for thee, O Soul,
\q1 That thou mayest acquiesce in God's ordinances,
\q1 And be wary and not doubt God's benevolence,
\q1 When sudden misfortunes befall thee.
\q1 Let others grow pale from fear of ill fortune,
\q1 Do thou smile like the rose at loss and gain;
\q1 For the rose, though its petals be torn asunder,
\q1 Still smiles on, and it is never cast down.
\q1 It says, "Why should I fall into grief in disgrace?
\q1 I gather beauty even from the thorn of disgrace."
\q1 Whatsoever is lost to thee through God's decree
\q1 Know of a surety is so much gained from misfortune.
\q1 What is Sufiism? 'Tis to find joy in the heart
\q1 Whensoever distress and care assail it.
\q1 Know troubles to be that eagle of the Prophet's
\q1 Which carried off the sandal of that holy one,
\q1 In order to save his foot from the bite of the viper
\q1 O excellent device!— to preserve him from harm.
\q1 'Tis said, "Mourn not for your slaughtered cattle
\q1 If a wolf has harried your flocks;"
\q1 For that calamity may avert a greater calamity,
\q1 And that loss may ward off a more grievous loss.
\v 28 \vp 15.1\vp* God said, "Do thou grant his earnest request,
\q1 Enlarge his faculty according to his freewill.
\q1 Freewill is as the salt to piety,
\q1 Otherwise heaven itself were matter of compulsion.
\q1 In its revolutions reward and punishment were needless,
\q1 For 'tis freewill that has merit at the great reckoning.
\q1 If the whole world were framed to praise God,
\q1 There would be no merit in praising God.
\q1 Place a sword in his hand and remove his impotence,
\q1 To see if he turns out a warrior or a robber.
\q1 Because freewill is that wherewith 'we honour Adam,'
\q1 Half the swarm become bees and half wasps.
\q1 The faithful yield honeycombs like bees,
\q1 The infidels yield store of poison like wasps.
\q1 For the faithful feed on choice herbs,
\q1 So that, like bees, their chyle yields life-giving food,
\q1 Whilst infidels feed on filth and garbage,
\q1 And generate poison according to their food."
\q1 Men inspired of God are the fountain of life;
\q1 Men of delusions are a synonym for death.
\q1 In the world the praise "Well done, faithful servant!"
\q1 Is given to freewill which is used with prudence.
\q1 If all dissolute men were shut up in prison,
\q1 They would all be temperate and devout and pious.
\q1 When power of choice is absent actions are worthless;
\q1 But beware lest death snatch away your capital!
\q1 Your power of choice is a capital yielding profit,
\q1 Remember well the day of final account!
\v 29 \vp 16.1\vp* God's mercy is known through the fruits thereof,
\q1 But who save God knows His essence?
\q1 No one knows the very essence of God's attributes
\q1 But only in their effects and by similitudes.
\q1 A child knows naught of the nature of sexual intercourse,
\q1 Except what you tell him, that it is like sweetmeats.
\q1 Yet how far does the pleasure of sexual intercourse
\q1 Really resemble that derived from sweetmeats?
\q1 Nevertheless the fiction produces a relation
\q1 Between you, with your perfect knowledge, and the child;
\q1 So that the child knows the matter by a similitude,
\q1 Though he knows not its essence or actual nature.
\q1 Hence if he says, "I know it," 'tis not far wrong
\q1 And if he says, "I know it not," 'tis not wrong.
\q1 Should one say, "Do you know Noah,
\q1 That prophet of God and luminary of the Spirit?"
\q1 If you say, "Do I not know him, for that moon
\q1 Is more famed than the sun and moon of heaven?
\q1 Little children in their schools,
\q1 And elders in their mosques,
\q1 All read his name prominently in the Koran,
\q1 And preachers tell his story from times of yore;—"
\q1 You say true, for you know him by report,
\q1 Though the real nature of Noah is not revealed to you.
\q1 On the other hand, if you say, "What know I of Noah
\q1 As his contemporaries knew him?
\q1 I am a poor ant— what can I know of the elephant?
\q1 What knows a fly of the motions of the elephant?—"
\q1 This statement also is true, O brother,
\q1 Seeing that you know not his real nature.
\q1 But this impotence to perceive real essence,
\q1 Though common to ordinary men, is not universal;
\q1 Because essence and its deepest secrets
\q1 Are open and manifest to the eyes of the perfect.
\q1 Negation and affirmation of one proposition are lawful;
\q1 When the aspects differ the relation is double.
\q1 "Thou castest not when thou castest" shows such relation,
\q1 Here negation and affirmation are both correct.
\q1 Thou castest it, since it is in thy hand,
\q1 Thou castest not, since 'tis God who affords the strength.
\q1 The might of the sons of Adam is limited,
\q1 How can a handful of sand shatter an army?
\q1 The sand was in man's hands, the casting was God's.
\q1 Owing to the two relations negation and affirmation are both true.
\q1 The infidels know the prophets,
\q1 As well as they doubtless know their own children;
\q1 Yea, the infidels know them as well as their own sons,
\q1 By a hundred tokens and a hundred evidences,
\q1 But from envy and malice conceal their knowledge,
\q1 And incline themselves to say, "We know them not."
\q1 So when God says in one place "knows them,"
\q1 In another He says, "None knows them beside me."
\q1 For in truth they are hid under God's overshadowing,
\q1 And none but God knows them by actual experience.
\q1 Therefore take this declaration with its context,
\q1 Remembering how you know and do not know Noah.
\v 30 \vp 17.1\vp* A damsel said to her lover, "O fond youth,
\q1 You have visited many cities in your travels;
\q1 Which of those cities seems most delightful to you?"
\q1 He made answer, "The city wherein my love dwells.
\q1 In whatever nook my queen alights,
\q1 Though it be as the eye of a needle, 'tis a wide plain;
\q1 Wherever her Yusuf-like face shines as a moon,
\q1 Though it be the bottom of a well, 'tis Paradise.
\q1 With thee, my love, hell itself were heaven,
\q1 With thee a prison would be a rose-garden.
\q1 With thee hell would be a mansion of delight,
\q1 Without thee lilies and roses would be as flames of fire!"
\v 31 \vp 17.2\vp* He said, "I am a drawer of water; water attracts me,
\q1 Even though I know water may be my death.
\q1 No drawer of water flees from water,
\q1 Even though it may cause him a hundred deaths.
\q1 Though it may make my hand and belly dropsical,
\q1 My love for water will never be lessened.
\q1 I should say, when they asked me about my belly,
\q1 'Would that the ocean might flow into it!'
\q1 Though the bottle of my belly were burst with water,
\q1 And though I should die, my death would be acceptable.
\q1 Wheresoever I see one seeking water, I envy him,
\q1 And cry, 'Would I were in his place!'
\q1 My hand is a tabor and my belly a drum,
\q1 Like the rose I beat the drum of love of water.
\q1 Like the earth or like a foetus I devour blood,
\q1 Since I became a lover this is my occupation.
\q1 If that 'Faithful Spirit' should shed my blood,
\q1 I would drink it up drop by drop like the earth.
\q1 At night I boil on the fire like a cooking-pot,
\q1 From morn till eve I drink blood like the sand.
\q1 It repents me that I planned a stratagem,
\q1 And that I fled from before his wrath.
\q1 Tell him to sate his wrath on my poor life,
\q1 He is the 'Feast of Sacrifice,' and I his loving cow.
\q1 The cow, whether it eats or sleeps,
\q1 Thinks of naught but sacrificing itself.
\q1 Know me to be that cow of Moses which gave its life,
\q1 Each part of me gives life to the righteous.
\q1 That cow of Moses was made a sacrifice,
\q1 And its least part became a source of life.
\q1 That murdered man leapt up from his deadness
\q1 At the words, ' Strike the corpse with part of her.'
\q1 O pious ones, slay the cow (of lust),
\q1 If ye desire true life of soul and spirit!
\q1 I died as inanimate matter and arose a plant,
\q1 I died as a plant and rose again an animal.
\q1 I died as an animal and arose a man.
\q1 Why then should I fear to become less by dying?
\q1 I shall die once again as a man
\q1 To rise an angel perfect from head to foot!
\q1 Again when I suffer dissolution as an angel,
\q1 I shall become what passes the conception of man!
\q1 Let me then become non-existent, for non-existence
\q1 Sings to me in organ tones, 'To him shall we return.'
\q1 Know death to be the gathering together of the people,
\q1 The water of life is hidden in the land of darkness.
\q1 Like a water-lily seek life there!
\q1 Yea, like that drawer of water, at the risk of life,
\q1 Water will be his death, yet he still seeks water,
\q1 And still drinks on,— and God knows what is right.
\q1 O lover, cold-hearted and void of loyalty,
\q1 Who from fear for your life shun the beloved!
\q1 O base one, behold a hundred thousand souls
\q1 Dancing towards the deadly sword of his love:
\q1 Behold water in a pitcher; pour it out;
\q1 Will that water run away from the stream?
\q1 When that water joins the water of the stream
\q1 It is lost therein, and becomes itself the stream.
\q1 Its individuality is lost, but its essence remains,
\q1 And hereby it becomes not less nor inferior.
\q1 I will hang myself upon my lord's palm-tree
\q1 In excuse for having fled away from him!"
\q1 Even as a ball rolling along on head and face,
\q1 He fell at the feet of the Prince with streaming eyes.
\q1 The people were all on the alert, expecting
\q1 That the Prince would burn him or hang him,
\q1 Saying, "Moth-like he has seen the blaze of the light,
\q1 And fool-like has plunged therein and lost his life."
\q1 But the torch of love is not like that torch,
\q1 'Tis light, light in the midst of light,
\q1 'Tis the reverse of torches of fire,
\q1 It appears to be fire, but is all sweetness.
\v 32 \vp 17.3\vp* That. Bokharian then cast himself into the flame,
\q1 But his love made the pain endurable;
\q1 And as his burning sighs ascended to heaven,
\q1 The love of the Prince was kindled towards him.
\q1 The heart of man is like the root of a tree,
\q1 Therefrom grow the leaves on firm branches.
\q1 Corresponding to that root grow up branches
\q1 As well on the tree as on souls and intellects.
\q1 The tops of the perfect trees reach the heavens,
\q1 The roots firm, and the branches in the sky.
\q1 Since then the tree of love has grown up to heaven,
\q1 How shall it not also grow in the heart of the Prince?
\q1 A wave washes away the remembrance of the sin from his heart,
\q1 For from each heart is a window to other hearts.
\q1 Since in each heart there is a window to other hearts,
\q1 They are not, separated and shut off like two bodies.
\q1 Thus, even though two lamp-dishes be not joined,
\q1 Yet their light is united in a single ray.
\q1 No lover ever seeks union with his beloved,
\q1 But his beloved is also seeking union with him.
\q1 But the lover's love makes his body lean,
\q1 While the beloved's love makes hers fair and lusty.
\q1 When in this heart the lightning spark of love arises,
\q1 Be sure this love is reciprocated in that heart.
\q1 When the love of God arises in thy heart,
\q1 Without doubt God also feels love for thee.
\q1 The noise of clapping of hands is never heard
\q1 From one of thy hands unaided by the other hand
\q1 The man athirst cries, "Where is delicious water?"
\q1 Water too cries, "Where is the water-drinker?"
\q1 This thirst in my soul is the attraction of the water;
\q1 I am the water's and the water is mine.
\q1 God's wisdom in His eternal foreknowledge and decree
\q1 Made us to be lovers one of the other.
\q1 Nay more, all the parts of the world by this decree
\q1 Are arranged in pairs, and each loves its mate.
\q1 Every part of the world desires its mate,
\q1 Just as amber attracts blades of straw.
\q1 Heaven says to earth, "All hail to thee!
\q1 We are related to one another as iron and magnet."
\q1 Heaven is man and earth woman in character;
\q1 Whatever heaven sends it, earth cherishes.
\q1 When earth lacks heat, heaven sends heat;
\q1 When it lacks moisture and dew, heaven sends them.
\q1 The earthy sign succours the terrestrial earth,
\q1 The watery sign (Aquarius) sends moisture to it;
\q1 The windy sign sends the clouds to it,
\q1 To draw off unwholesome exhalations.
\q1 The fiery sign (Leo) sends forth the heat of the sun,
\q1 Like a dish heated red-hot in front and behind.
\q1 The heaven is busily toiling through ages,
\q1 Just as men labour to provide food for women.
\q1 And the earth does the woman's work, and toils
\q1 In bearing offspring and suckling them.
\q1 Know then earth and heaven are endued with sense,
\q1 Since they act like persons endued with sense.
\q1 If these two lovers did not suck nutriment from each other,
\q1 Why should they creep together like man and wife?
\q1 Without the earth how could roses and saffron grow?
\q1 For naught can grow from the sole heat and rain of heaven.
\q1 This is the cause of the female seeking the male,
\q1 That the work of each may be accomplished.
\q1 God has instilled mutual love into man and woman,
\q1 That the world may be perpetuated by their union.
\q1 Earth says to the earth of the body, "Come away,
\q1 Quit the soul and come to me as dust.
\q1 Thou art of my genus, and wilt be better with me,
\q1 'Thou had'st better quit the soul and fly to me!"
\q1 Body replies, "True, but my feet are fast bound,
\q1 Though like thee I suffer from separation."
\q1 Water calls out to the moisture of the body,
\q1 "O moisture, return to me from your foreign abode!"
\q1 Fire also calls out to the heat of the body,
\q1 "Thou art of fire; return to thy root!"
\q1 In the body there are seventy-and-two diseases;
\q1 It is ill compacted owing to the struggle of its elements.
\q1 Disease comes to rend the body asunder,
\q1 And to drag apart its constituent elements.
\q1 The four elements are as birds tied together by the feet;
\q1 Death, sickness and disease loose their feet asunder.
\q1 The moment their feet are loosed from the others,
\q1 'The bird of each element flies off by itself.
\q1 The repulsion of each of these principles and causes
\q1 Inflicts every moment a fresh pang on our bodies.
\q1 That it may dissolve these composite bodies of ours,
\q1 The bird of each part tries to fly away to its origin;
\q1 But the wisdom of God prevents this speedy end,
\q1 And preserves their union till the appointed day.
\q1 He says, "O parts, the appointed time is not yet;
\q1 It is useless for you to take wing before that day."
\q1 But as each part desires reunion with its original,
\q1 How is it with the soul who is a stranger in exile?
\q1 It says, "O parts of my habitation here below,
\q1 My absence is sadder than yours, as I am heaven-born.
\q1 The body loves green pastures and running water,
\q1 For this cause that its origin is from them.
\q1 The love of the soul is for life and the living one,
\q1 Because its origin is the Soul not bound to place.
\q1 The love of the soul is for wisdom and knowledge,
\q1 That of the body for houses, gardens, and vineyards;
\q1 The love of the soul is for things exalted on high,
\q1 That of the body for acquisition of goods and food.
\q1 The love too of Him on high is directed to the soul:
\q1 Know this for 'He loves them that love Him.'"
\q1 The sum is this, that whoso seeks another,
\q1 The soul of that other who is sought inclines to him.
\q1 Let us quit the subject.— Love for that soul athirst
\q1 Was kindled in the breast of the Prince of Bokhara.
\q1 The smoke of that love and the grief of that burning heart
\q1 Ascended to his master and excited his compassion.
\v 33 \vp 17.4\vp* He said, "O phoenix of God and goal of the spirit
\q1 I thank thee that thou hast come back from Mount Qaf!
\q1 O Israfil of the resurrection-day of love,
\q1 O love, love, and heart's desire of love!
\q1 Let thy first boon to me be this,
\q1 To lend thine ear to my orisons.
\q1 Though thou knowest my condition clearly,
\q1 O protector of slaves, listen to my speech.
\q1 A thousand times, O prince incomparable,
\q1 Has my reason taken flight in desire to see thee,
\q1 And to hear thee and to listen to thy words,
\q1 And to behold thy life-giving smiles.
\q1 Thy inclining thine ear to my supplications
\q1 Is as a caress to my misguided soul.
\q1 The baseness of my heart's coin is known to thee,
\q1 But thou hast accepted it as genuine coin.
\q1 Thou art proud towards the arrogant and proud;
\q1 All clemencies are as naught to thy clemency.
\q1 First hear this, that while I remained in absence,
\q1 First and last alike escaped me.
\q1 Secondly, hear this. O prince beloved,
\q1 That I searched much, but found no second to thee.
\q1 Thirdly, that when I had departed outside thee,
\q1 I said it was like the Christian Trinity.
\q1 Fourthly, when my harvest was burned up,
\q1 I knew not the fourth from the fifth.
\q1 Wheresoever thou findest blood on the roads,
\q1 Trace it, and 'tis tears of blood from my eyes.
\q1 My words are thunder, and these sighs and tears
\q1 Are drawn by it as rain from the clouds.
\q1 I am distracted between speaking and weeping.
\q1 Shall I weep, or shall I speak, or what shall I do?
\q1 If I speak, my weeping ceases;
\q1 If I weep, I cease to praise and magnify thee."
\q1 He spoke thus, and then fell to weeping,
\q1 So that high and low wept with him.
\q1 So many "Ahs" and "Alases" proceeded from his heart,
\q1 That the people of Bokhara formed a circle round him.
\q1 Talking sadly, weeping sadly, smiling sadly,
\q1 Men and women, small and great, were all assembled.
\q1 The whole city wept in concert with him;
\q1 Men and women mingled together as on the last day.
\q1 Then Heaven said to Earth,
\q1 "If you never saw a resurrection-day, see it here!"
\q1 Reason was amazed, saying, "What love, what ecstasy!
\q1 Is his separation more wondrous, or his reunion?"
\v 34 \vp 18.1\vp* Our body and substance are snow, doomed to perish,
\q1 God is He who buys them, for "God hath bought them."
\q1 You prefer this perishing snow to God's price
\q1 Because you are in doubt and have not certainty;
\q1 And, strange to say, opinion abides in you, O weak one,
\q1 And never flies away to the garden of certainty.
\q1 Every opinion is aiming at certainty, O son,
\q1 And more and more moves its wings towards certainty.
\q1 When it reaches knowledge it stands erect,
\q1 And its knowledge again hastes on towards certainty,
\q1 Because in the approved road of the faith
\q1 Knowledge is lower than certainty, but above opinion.
\q1 Know knowledge aspires to certainty,
\q1 And certainty again to sight and ocular evidence.
\q1 In the chapter, "Desire of riches occupieth you,"
\q1 After "Nay," read "Would that ye knew!"
\q1 Knowledge conducts you to sight, O knower!
\q1 "If ye are certain, ye shall see hell-fire."
\q1 Sight follows on certainty with no interval,
\q1 Just as reasoned knowledge is born of opinion.
\q1 See the account of this in the chapter cited,
\q1 How knowledge of certainty becomes the eye of certainty.
\q1 As for me, I am above both opinion and certainty;
\q1 My head is not affected by your cavils.
\q1 Since my mouth has eaten of His sweetmeats,
\q1 I am become clear-sighted, and see Him face to face!
\v 35 \vp 18.2\vp* Behold these potherbs boiling in the pot,
\q1 How they jump and toss about in the heat of the fire.
\q1 Whilst they are boiling, they keep leaping up,
\q1 Even to the top of the pot, and utter cries,
\q1 Saying to the housewife, "Why do you set us on the fire?
\q1 Now you have bought us, why should you afflict us?"
\q1 The housewife pushes them down with her spoon, saying,
\q1 "Be still, and boil well, and leap not off the fire.
\q1 I do not boil you because I dislike you,
\q1 But that you may acquire a good savour and taste.
\q1 When you become food you will be mingled with life;
\q1 This trial is not imposed on you to distress you.
\q1 In the garden you drank water soft and fresh;
\q1 That water-fed one was reserved for this fire.
\q1 Mercy was first shown to it before vengeance,
\q1 That mercy might train it to be proof against trial;
\q1 Mercy was shown to it previously to vengeance,
\q1 That it might acquire its substance of being.
\q1 Because flesh and skin grow not without tender care,
\q1 How should they not grow when warmed by the Friend's love.
\q1 If vengeance follows as a necessary consequence,
\q1 That you may make an offering of that substance,
\q1 Mercy follows again to compensate for it,
\q1 That you may be purified and raised above your nature.
\q1 I am Abraham, and you his son under the knife.
\q1 Lay down your head! 'I have seen I must sacrifice you.'
\q1 Yield your head to vengeance, your heart to constancy,
\q1 That I may cut your throat like an Ismailian's.
\q1 I cut off your head, but that head is such
\q1 That it is restored to life by being cut off!"
\q1 My main object herein is to inculcate resignation,
\q1 O Musulman! it behoves you to seek resignation.
\q1 O potherbs, you boil in trials and sufferings
\q1 That neither existence nor self may remain in you.
\q1 Though you once smiled in that earthly garden,
\q1 You are really roses of the garden of life and sight.
\q1 If you are torn away from the garden of earth,
\q1 You become sweet food to revive man's life;
\q1 Yea, become his food and strength and thought!
\q1 You were only milk, you become a lion of the forest!
\q1 You issue from God's attributes at first;
\q1 Return again back to those attributes with all speed!
\q1 You come from the clouds and sunshine and sky,
\q1 Then assume moral qualities and ascend the sky.
\q1 You come in the form of rain and sunshine,
\q1 You depart endued with excellent attributes.
\q1 You begin as a part of the sun, clouds, and stars,
\q1 You rise to be breath, act, word, and thought!
\q1 The life of animals comes from the death of plants.
\q1 True is the saying, 'Kill me, O faithful ones!'
\q1 Since such exaltation awaits us after death.
\q1 True it is that 'In our death is life.'
\q1 Acts, words, and faith are the food of the King,
\q1 So that in this ascent one attains to heaven.
\q1 Thus, as potherbs become the food of men,
\q1 They rise above the grade of minerals to that of animals.
\v 36 \vp 18.3\vp* A certain goose pops his head out of his coop,
\q1 And displays himself as a critic of the Masnavi,
\q1 Saying, "This poem, the Masnavi, is childish;
\q1 'Tis but a story of the prophets, and so on.
\q1 'Tis not an account of the arguments and deep mysteries,
\q1 Whereto holy men direct their attention;—
\q1 Concerning asceticism, and so on to self-annihilation,
\q1 Step by step, up to communion with God;—
\q1 An explanation and definition of each several state,
\q1 Whereto the men of heart ascend in their flight."
\q1 Whereas the Book of God resembles the Masnavi in this,
\q1 The infidels abused it in the same manner,
\q1 Saying, 'It contains old tales and stories;
\q1 There is no deep analysis or lofty investigation therein.
\q1 Little boys can understand it;
\q1 It only contains commands and prohibitions,
\q1 Accounts of Yusuf and his curled locks,
\q1 Accounts of Jacob, of Zulaikha and her love,
\q1 Accounts of Adam, of the wheat, and of the serpent Iblis,
\q1 Accounts of Hud, of Noah, of Abraham, and the fire."
\q1 Know the words of the Koran are simple,
\q1 But within the outward sense is an inner secret one.
\q1 Beneath that secret meaning is a third,
\q1 Whereat the highest wit is dumbfoundered.
\q1 The fourth meaning has been seen by none
\q1 Save God, the Incomparable and All-sufficient.
\q1 Thus they go on, even to seven meanings, one by one,
\q1 According to the saying of the Prophet, without doubt.
\q1 Do thou, O son, confine not thy view to the outward meaning,
\q1 Even as the demons saw in Adam only clay.
\q1 The outward meaning of the Koran is like Adam's body,
\q1 For its semblance is visible, but its soul is hidden.
\q1 O reviling dog! thou makest a clamour,
\q1 Thou makest thy abuse of the Koran thy destruction.
\q1 This is not a lion, wherefrom thou canst save thy life,
\q1 Or canst secure thyself from his talons!
\q1 The Koran cries out even to the last day,
\q1 "O people, given up as a prey to ignorance,
\q1 If ye have imagined me to be only empty fables,
\q1 Ye have sown the seed of reviling and infidelity.
\q1 Ye yourselves who abuse me will see yourselves
\q1 Annihilated, and made like a tale that is told!"
\v 37 \vp 18.4\vp* A gnat came in from the garden and fields,
\q1 And called on Solomon for justice,
\q1 Saying, "O Solomon, you extend your equity
\q1 Over demons and the sons of Adam and fairies.
\q1 Fish and fowl dwell under the shelter of your justice;
\q1 Where is the oppressed one whom your mercy has not sought?
\q1 Grant me redress, for I am much afflicted,
\q1 Being cut off from my garden and meadow haunts."
\q1 Then Solomon replied, "O seeker of redress,
\q1 Tell me from whom do you desire redress?
\q1 Who is the oppressor, who, puffed up with arrogance,
\q1 Has oppressed you and smitten your face?"
\q1 The gnat replied, "He from whom I seek redress is the Wind,
\q1 'Tis he who has emitted the smoke of oppression at me;
\q1 Through his oppression I am in a grievous strait,
\q1 Through him I drink blood with parched lips!"
\q1 Solomon replied to him, "O sweet- voiced one,
\q1 You must hear the command of God with all your heart.
\q1 God has commanded me saying, 'O dispenser of justice,
\q1 Never hear one party without the other!'
\q1 Till both parties come into the presence,
\q1 The truth is never made plain to the judge."
\q1 When the Wind heard the summons, it came swiftly,
\q1 And the gnat instantly took flight.
\q1 In like manner the seekers of God's presence-seat,—
\q1 When God appears, those seekers vanish.
\q1 Though that union is life eternal,
\q1 Yet at first that life is annihilation.
\c 4
\cp 4
\p
\v 1 \vp 1.1\vp* The lover invoked blessings on that rough patrol,
\q1 Because their harshness had wrought bliss for him.
\q1 They were poison to most men, but sweets to him,
\q1 Because those harsh ones had united him with his love.
\q1 In the world there is nothing absolutely bad;
\q1 Know, moreover, evil is only relative.
\q1 In the world there is neither poison nor antidote,
\q1 Which is not a foot to one and a fetter to another;
\q1 To one the power of moving, to another a clog;
\q1 To one a poison, to another an antidote.
\q1 Serpents' poison is life to serpents,
\q1 In relation to mankind it is death.
\q1 To the creatures of the sea the sea is a garden,
\q1 To the creatures of the land it is fatal.
\q1 In the same way, O man, reckon up with intelligence
\q1 The relations of these things in endless variety.
\q1 In relation to this man Zaid is as Satan,
\q1 In relation to another he is as a Sultan.
\q1 The latter calls Zaid a sincere Mussulman,
\q1 The former calls him a Gueber deserving to be killed.
\q1 Zaid, one and the same person, is life to the one,
\q1 And to the other an annoyance and a pest.
\q1 If you desire that God may be pleasing to you,
\q1 Then look at Him with the eyes of them that love Him.
\q1 Look not at that Beauty with your own eyes,
\q1 Look at that Object of desire with His votaries' eyes;
\q1 Shut your own eyes from beholding that sweet Object,
\q1 And borrow from His admirers their eyes;
\q1 Nay, borrow from Him both eyes and sight,
\q1 And with those eyes of His look upon His face,
\q1 In order that you may not be disappointed with the sight.
\q1 God says, "Whoso is God's, God also is his."
\q1 God says, "I am his eye, his hand, his heart,"
\q1 That his good fortune may emerge from adversity.
\q1 Whatsoever is hateful to you, if it should lead you
\q1 To your beloved, at once becomes agreeable to you.
\v 2 \vp 1.2\vp* God calls himself "Seeing," to the end that
\q1 His eye may every moment scare you from sinning.
\q1 God calls himself "Hearing," to the end that
\q1 You may close your lips against foul discourse.
\q1 God calls himself "Knowing," to the end that
\q1 You may be afraid to plot evil.
\q1 These names are not mere accidental names of God,
\q1 As a negro may be called Kafu'r (white);
\q1 They are names derived from God's essential attributes,
\q1 Not mere vain titles of the First Cause.
\q1 For if so, they would be only empty pleasantries,
\q1 Like calling the deaf a hearer and the blind a seer,
\q1 Or a name like "impudent" for a modest man,
\q1 Or "beautiful" for an ugly negro,
\q1 Or such a title as "Haji" for a new-born boy,
\q1 Or that of "Ghazi" applied to a noble idler.
\q1 If such titles as these are used in praising persons
\q1 Who do not possess the qualities implied, 'tis wrong;
\q1 'Twould be jesting or mockery or madness.
\q1 "God is exalted above" what is said by evil men.
\q1 I knew you before I met you face to face,
\q1 That you had a fair face but an evil heart;
\q1 Yea, I knew you before I saw you,
\q1 That you were rooted in iniquity through guile.
\q1 When my eye is red owing to inflammation,
\q1 I know 'tis so from the pain, though I see it not.
\q1 You regarded me as a lamb without a shepherd;
\q1 You fancied that I had no guardian.
\q1 Lovers have suffered chastisement for this cause,
\q1 That they have cast ill-timed looks at fair ones.
\q1 They have supposed the fawn to have no shepherd,
\q1 They have supposed the captive to be going a begging;
\q1 Till in the twinkling of an eye an arrow pierces them,
\q1 Saying, "I am her guardian; look not at her rashly!"
\q1 What! am I less than a lamb or a fallow deer,
\q1 That I should have none to shepherd me?
\q1 Nay, I have a Guardian worthy of dominion,
\q1 Who knows every wind that blows upon me.
\q1 He is aware whether that wind is chill or mild,
\q1 He is not ignorant nor absent, O mean one.
\q1 The carnal soul is made by God blind and deaf;
\q1 I saw with the heart's eye your blindness afar off.
\q1 For this cause I never inquired about you for eight years.
\q1 Because I saw you filled with ignorance and duplicity.
\q1 Why indeed should I inquire about one in the furnace,
\q1 Who is bowed down with reproach, like yourself?
\v 3 \vp 1.3\vp* The lust of the world is like a bath stove,
\q1 Whereby the bath of piety is heated;
\q1 But the lot of the pious is purity from the stove's filth,
\q1 Because they dwell in the bath and in cleanliness.
\q1 The rich are as those that carry dung
\q1 To heat the furnace of the bath withal.
\q1 God has instilled into them cupidity,
\q1 That the bath may be warmed and pleasant.
\q1 Quit this stove and push on into the bath;
\q1 Know quitting the stove to be the bath itself.
\q1 Whoso is in the stove-room is as a servant
\q1 To him who is temperate and prudent.
\q1 Your lust is as fire in the world,
\q1 With a hundred greedy mouths wide open.
\q1 In the judgment of reason this gold is foul dung,
\q1 Although, like dung, it serves to kindle the fire.
\q1 Whoso was born in the stove-room and never saw purity,
\q1 The smell of sweet musk is disagreeable to him.
\v 4 \vp 2.1\vp* In outward form thou art the microcosm,
\q1 But in reality the macrocosm.
\q1 Seemingly the bough is the cause of the fruit,
\q1 But really the bough exists because of the fruit.
\q1 Were he not impelled by desire of fruit,
\q1 The gardener would never have planted the tree.
\q1 Therefore in reality the tree is born from the fruit,
\q1 Though seemingly the fruit is born from the tree.
\q1 For this cause Mustafa said, "Adam and all prophets
\q1 Are my followers and gather under my standard.
\q1 Though to outward view I am a son of Adam,
\q1 In reality I am his first forefather,
\q1 Because the angels worshipped him for my sake,
\q1 And 'twas in my footsteps that he ascended to heaven.
\q1 Hence in reality our first parent was my offspring,
\q1 As in reality the tree is born of its own fruit."
\q1 What is first in thought is last in act.
\q1 Thought is the special attribute of the Eternal.
\q1 This product goes forth from heaven very swiftly,
\q1 And comes to us like a caravan.
\q1 'Tis not a long road that this caravan travels;
\q1 Can the desert stop the deliverer?
\q1 The heart travels to the Ka'ba every moment,
\q1 And by divine grace the body acquires the same power.
\q1 Distance and nearness affect only the body,
\q1 What do they matter in the place where God is?
\q1 When God changes the body,
\q1 It regards not parasangs or miles.
\q1 Even on earth there is hope of approaching God.
\q1 Press on like a lover, and cease vain words, O son!
\v 5 \vp 2.2\vp* Report to Bilqis what marvels ye have seen,
\q1 And what plains of gold belong to Solomon;
\q1 How ye beheld forty mansions faced with gold,
\q1 And how ye were ashamed of your presents;
\q1 That she may know Solomon is not covetous of gold;
\q1 He has received gold from the Creator of gold.
\q1 The moment he wills it, every grain of earth's dust
\q1 Is changed into gold and precious pearls.
\q1 For this cause, O thou that lovest gold,
\q1 On the last day God will make earth all silver (white).
\q1 Quit thy wealth, even if it be the realm of Saba;
\q1 Thou wilt find many realms not of this earth.
\q1 What thou callest a throne is only a prison;
\q1 Thou thinkest thyself enthroned, but art outside the door.
\q1 Thou hast no sovereignty over thine own passions,
\q1 How canst thou sway good and evil?
\q1 Thy hair turns white without thy concurrence,
\q1 Take shame to thyself for thy evil passions.
\q1 Whoso bows his head to the King of kings
\q1 Will receive a hundred kingdoms not of this world;
\q1 But the delight of bowing down before God
\q1 Will seem sweeter to thee than countless glories."
\v 6 \vp 2.3\vp* Haste to renounce thy kingdom, like Ibrahim bin Adham,
\q1 To obtain, like him, the kingdom of eternity.
\q1 At night that king would sleep on his throne,
\q1 With his guards of state surrounding his palace,
\q1 Though he needed no guards for the purpose
\q1 Of warding off robbers and vagabonds;
\q1 For he who is a just king knows everything,
\q1 And is safe from harm and his mind is at peace.
\q1 Justice is the guardian of his steps,
\q1 Not guards with drums round his palace.
\q1 His purpose in having this band of music was this,
\q1 To recall to his longing heart that call of God.
\q1 The wailing of horn and the thunder of drum
\q1 Resemble in some sort that dread "trumpet blast."
\q1 Wherefore philosophers say that we have learned
\q1 Our melodies from those of the revolving spheres.
\q1 The song of the spheres in their revolutions
\q1 Is what men sing with lute and voice.
\q1 The faithful hold that the sweet influences of heaven
\q1 Can make even harsh voices melodious.
\q1 As we are all members of Adam,
\q1 We have heard these melodies in Paradise;
\q1 Though earth and water have cast their veil upon us,
\q1 We retain faint reminiscences of those heavenly songs.
\q1 But while we are thus shrouded by gross earthly veils,
\q1 How can the tones of the dancing spheres reach us?
\q1 Hence it is that listening to music is lovers' food,
\q1 Because it recalls to them their primal union with God.
\q1 The inward feelings of the mind acquire strength,
\q1 Nay, are shown outwardly, under influence of music.
\q1 The fire of love burns hotter under stimulus of music,
\q1 Even as occurred in the case of the nut-gatherer.
\v 7 \vp 2.4\vp* Once that noble Ibrahim, as he sat on his throne,
\q1 Heard a clamour and noise of cries on the roof,
\q1 Also heavy footsteps on the roof of his palace.
\q1 He said to himself, "Whose heavy feet are those?"
\q1 He shouted from his window, "Who goes there?
\q1 'Tis no man's step; surely 'tis a fairy."
\q1 His guards, filled with confusion, bowed their heads,
\q1 Saying, "It is we who are going the rounds in search."
\q1 He said, "What seek ye?" They said, "Our camels".
\q1 He said, "Who ever searched for camels on a housetop?"
\q1 They said, "We follow thy example,
\q1 Who seekest union with God while sitting on a throne."
\q1 This was all, and no man ever saw him again,
\q1 Just as fairies are invisible to men.
\q1 His substance was hid from men, though he was with them,
\q1 For what can men see save the outward aspect and dress?
\q1 As he was removed from the sight of friends and strangers,
\q1 His fame was noised abroad like that of the 'Anka.
\q1 For the soul of every bird that reaches Mount Qaf
\q1 Confers glory on the whole family of birds. 
\v 8 \vp 2.5\vp* I tell the tale of Saba in lovers' style.
\q1 When the breeze bore Solomon's words to that garden,
\q1 'Twas as when bodies meet souls at the resurrection,
\q1 Or as when boys return to their loved homes.
\q1 The people of love are hidden amongst the peoples,
\q1 As a liberal man encompassed by the contumely of the base.
\q1 Souls are disgraced by union with bodies,
\q1 Bodies are ennobled by union with souls.
\q1 Arise, O lovers; this sweet draught is yours;
\q1 Ye are they that endure; eternal life is yours.
\q1 Ho! ye that seek, arise and take your fill of love,
\q1 Snuff up that perfume of Yusuf!
\q1 Approach, O Solomon, thou that knowest birds' language,
\q1 Sound the note of every bird that draws near.
\q1 When God sent thee to the birds,
\q1 He taught thee first the notes of all the birds.
\q1 To the predestinarian bird talk predestination,
\q1 To the bird with broken wings preach patience,
\q1 To the patient well-doer preach comfort and pardon,
\q1 To the spiritual 'Anka relate the glories of Mount Qaf,
\q1 To the pigeon preach avoidance of the hawk,
\q1 To the lordly hawk mercy and self-control;
\q1 As for the bat, who lingers helpless in the dark,
\q1 Acquaint him with the society of the light;
\q1 To the fighting partridge teach peace,
\q1 To the cock the signs of dawning day.
\q1 In this way deal with all from the hoopoo to the eagle.
\v 9 \vp 2.6\vp* Ah! Bilqis, bestir thyself now the market is thronged,
\q1 Flee away from them whose traffic is unprofitable!
\q1 Arise, Bilqis, now that thou hast the choice,
\q1 Before that death lays his heavy hand upon thee.
\q1 Soon will death pull thy ears, as if thou wert
\q1 A thief dragged before the officer in deadly fear.
\q1 How long wilt thou steal shoes from asses of the world?
\q1 If thou must steal, steal pearls of the world above.
\q1 Thy sisters have found the kingdom that lasts forever,
\q1 Thou cleavest to the kingdom of darkness.
\q1 Happy is he who quits this earthly kingdom,
\q1 Which sooner or later death will destroy.
\q1 Arise! O Bilqis, at least behold
\q1 The kingdom of the royal kings of the faith!
\q1 In reality they are seated in the garden of the spirit,
\q1 Though to outward view they are guiding their friends.
\q1 That spiritual garden accompanies them everywhere,
\q1 Yet it is never revealed to the eyes of the people,—
\q1 Its fruits ever asking to be gathered,
\q1 Its fount of life welling up to be drunk!
\q1 Go round about the heavens without aid of wings,
\q1 Like sun or full moon or new moon!
\q1 Thou wilt move as a spirit without aid of feet,
\q1 Thou wilt eat sweet viands without mouth or palate.
\q1 No crocodile of sorrow will attack thy bark,
\q1 Nor will sad thoughts of death assail thee.
\q1 Thou wilt be at once queen, army, and throne,
\q1 Endued with good fortune and fortune itself.
\q1 Thou sayest thou art a great queen of good fortune;
\q1 But thy fortune is apart from thee and will soon fade,
\q1 Thou wilt be left like a beggar without sustenance;
\q1 Therefore, O chosen one, become thy own fortune.
\q1 When, O spiritual one, thou hast become thy own fortune,
\q1 Then, being thyself thy fortune, thou wilt never lose it.
\q1 How. O fortunate one, canst thou ever lose thyself,
\q1 When thy real self is thy treasure and thy kingdom?
\v 10 \vp 2.7\vp* When Solomon laid the foundations of the temple,
\q1 Men and Jinns came and lent their aid to the work,
\q1 Some of them with good-will, and others on compulsion,
\q1 Even as worshippers follow the road of devotion.
\q1 Men are as demons, and lust of wealth their chain,
\q1 Which drags them forth to toil in shop and field.
\q1 This chain is made of their fears and anxieties.
\q1 Deem not that these men have no chain upon them.
\q1 It causes them to engage in labour and the chase,
\q1 It forces them to toil in mines and on the sea,
\q1 It urges them towards good and towards evil.
\q1 God saith, "On their necks is a rope of palm fibre,"
\q1 And "Verily on their necks have we placed ropes,''
\q1 "We make this rope out of their own dispositions;
\q1 There is none either impure or intelligent,
\q1 But we have fastened his work about his neck."
\q1 Thy lust is even as fire burning in thy evil deeds;
\q1 The black coal of these deeds is lighted by the fire;
\q1 The blackness of the coal is first hidden by the fire,
\q1 But, when it is burnt, the blackness is made visible.
\q1 The building of the prophets was without lust,
\q1 And accordingly its splendour ever increased.
\q1 Yea, many are the noble temples they have raised,
\q1 Though all were not named "The Most Remote Temple."
\q1 The Ka'ba, whose renown waxes greater every moment,
\q1 Owed its foundation to the piety of Abraham.
\q1 Its glory is not derived from stones and mortar,
\q1 But from being built without lust or strife.
\q1 Neither are the prophets' writings like other writings;
\q1 Nor their temples, nor their works, nor their families;
\q1 Nor their manners, nor their wrath, nor their chastisements;
\q1 Nor their dreams, nor their reason, nor their words.
\q1 Each one of them is endued with a different glory,
\q1 Each soul's bird winged with different feathers.
\q1 Ho! pious ones, build the lively temple of the heart,
\q1 That the Divine Solomon may be seen, and peace be upon you!
\q1 And if your demons and fairies be recalcitrant,
\q1 Your good angels must place collars on their necks.
\q1 If your demons go astray through guile and fraud,
\q1 Chastisement must overtake them swift as lightning.
\q1 Be like Solomon, so that your demons
\q1 May dig stones for your spiritual edifice.
\q1 Be like Solomon, free from evil thoughts and guile,
\q1 So that carnal demons and Jinns may be submissive to you.
\q1 Your heart is as Solomon's signet; take good care
\q1 That it falls not a prey to demons,
\q1 For then demons will rule over you as over Solomon.
\q1 Guard then your signet from the demons, and be at peace.
\v 11 \vp 2.8\vp* Yea, all the fish in the seas,
\q1 And all feathered fowl in the air above,
\q1 All elephants, wolves, and lions of the forest,
\q1 All dragons and snakes, and even little ants,
\q1 Yea, even air, water, earth, and fire,
\q1 Draw their sustenance from Him, both winter and summer.
\q1 Every moment this heaven cries to Him, saying,
\q1 "O Lord, quit not Thy hold of me for a moment!
\q1 The pillar of my being is Thy aid and protection;
\q1 The whole is folded up in that right hand of Thine."
\q1 And earth cries, "O keep me fixed and steadfast,
\q1 Thou who hast placed me on the top of waters!"
\q1 All of them are waiting and expecting His aid,
\q1 All have learned of Him to represent their needs.
\q1 Every prophet extols this prescription,
\q1 "Seek ye help with patience and with prayer."
\q1 Ho! seek aid of Him, not of another than Him
\q1 Seek water in the ocean, not in a dried-up channel.
\v 12 \vp 2.9\vp* When the body bows in worship, the heart is a temple,
\q1 And where there is a temple, there bad friends are weeds.
\q1 When a liking for bad friends grows up in you,
\q1 Flee from them, and avoid converse with them.
\q1 Root up those weeds, for, if they attain full growth,
\q1 They will subvert you and your temple together.
\q1 O beloved, this weed is deviation from the "right way,"
\q1 You crawl crookedly, like infants unable to walk.
\q1 Fear not to acknowledge your ignorance and guilt,
\q1 That the Heavenly Master may not withhold instruction.
\q1 When you say, "I am ignorant; O teach me,"
\q1 Such open confession is better than false pride.
\q1 O ingenuous one, learn of our father Adam,
\q1 Who said of yore, "O Lord, we have dealt unjustly."
\q1 He made no vain excuses and prevaricated not,
\q1 Nor did he raise the standard of guile and craft.
\q1 On the other hand, Iblis raised arguments, saying,
\q1 "I used to be honorable; Thou hast disgraced me.
\q1 My stain is owing to Thee; Thou art my dyer;
\q1 Thou hast caused my sin and transgression."
\q1 Read the text, "Lord, Thou hast caused me to err,"
\q1 That you plead not compulsion, and so err (like Iblis).
\q1 How long will you climb into that tree of compulsion?
\q1 How long cast out of sight your own freewill?
\q1 How long, like Iblis and his evil crew,
\q1 Throw the blame of your own sins upon God?
\q1 How were you compelled to sin when you took such pleasure
\q1 And pride in engaging in those sins?
\q1 Does a man feel such pleasure in acting on compulsion
\q1 As he exhibits when committing wrong actions?
\q1 You battle like twenty men against those
\q1 Who give you good advice not to do that act;
\q1 Saying to them, "This is right and quite proper;
\q1 Who dissuades me from it but men of no account?"
\q1 Does a man acting on compulsion talk like this?
\q1 Or rather one who is erring of his own freewill?
\q1 Whatever your lust wills you deem freewill,
\q1 What reason demands you deem compulsion.
\q1 Whoso is wise and prudent knows this,
\q1 That cleverness comes from Iblis, but love from Adam.
\q1 Cleverness is like Canaan's swimming in the ocean;
\q1 'Tis no river or small stream; 'tis the mighty ocean.
\q1 Away with this attempt to swim; quit self-conceit.
\q1 'Twill not save you; Canaan was drowned at last.
\q1 Love is as the ark appointed for the righteous,
\q1 Which annuls the danger and provides a way of escape.
\q1 Sell your cleverness and buy bewilderment;
\q1 Cleverness is mere opinion, bewilderment intuition.
\q1 Make sacrifice of your reason at the feet of Mustafa,
\q1 Say, "God Sufficeth me, for He is sufficient for me."
\q1 Do not, like Canaan, hang back from entering the ark,
\q1 Being puffed up with vain conceit of cleverness.
\q1 He said, "I will escape to the top of high mountains,
\q1 Why need I put myself under obligation to Noah?"
\q1 Ah! better for him had he never learnt swimming!
\q1 Then he would have based his hopes on Noah's ark.
\q1 Would he had been ignorant of craft as a babe!
\q1 Then like a babe he would have clung to his mother.
\q1 Would he had been less full of borrowed knowledge!
\q1 Then he would have accepted inspired knowledge from his father.
\q1 When, with inspiration at hand, you seek book-learning,
\q1 Your heart, as if inspired, loads you with reproach.
\q1 Traditional knowledge, when inspiration is available,
\q1 Is like making ablutions with sand when water is near.
\q1 Make yourself ignorant, be submissive, and then
\q1 You will obtain release from your ignorance.
\q1 For this cause, O son, the Prince of men declared,
\q1 "The majority of those in Paradise are the foolish."
\q1 Cleverness is as a wind raising storms of pride;
\q1 Be foolish, so that your heart may be at peace;
\q1 Not with the folly that doubles itself by vain babble,
\q1 But with that arising from bewilderment at "The Truth."
\q1 Those Egyptian women who cut their hands were fools
\q1 Fools as to their hands, being amazed at Yusuf's face.
\q1 Make sacrifice of reason to love of "The Friend,"
\q1 True reason is to be found where He is.
\q1 Men of wisdom direct their reason heavenwards,
\q1 Vain babblers halt on earth where no "Friend" is.
\q1 If through bewilderment your reason quits your head,
\q1 Every hair of your head becomes true reason and a head.
\v 13 \vp 3.1\vp* Moses said, "Their sorcery confuses them;
\q1 What can I do? These people have no discernment."
\q1 God said, "I will generate in them discernment;
\q1 I will make their undiscerning reason to see clearly.
\q1 Although like a sea their waves cast up foam,
\q1 O Moses, thou shalt prevail over them; fear not!"
\q1 The magicians gloried in their own achievements,
\q1 But when Moses' rod became a snake, they were confounded.
\q1 Whoso boasts of his beauty and wit,
\q1 The stone of death is a touchstone of his boasts.
\q1 Sorcery fades away, but the miracles of Moses advance.
\q1 Both resemble a dish falling from a roof:
\q1 The noise of the dish of sorcery leaves only cursing;
\q1 The noise of the dish of faith leaves edification.
\q1 When the touchstone is hidden from the sight of all,
\q1 Then come forth to battle and boast, O base coin!
\q1 Your time for boasting is when the touchstone is away;
\q1 The hand of power will soon crush your exaltation.
\q1 The base coin says to me with pride every moment,
\q1 "O pure gold, how am I inferior to you?"
\q1 The gold replies, "Even so, O comrade;
\q1 But the touchstone is at hand; be ready to meet it!"
\q1 Death of the body is a benefaction to the spiritual;
\q1 What damage has pure gold to dread from the shears?
\q1 If the base coin were of itself far-sighted,
\q1 It would reveal at first the blackness it shows at last.
\q1 If it had showed its blackness at first on its face,
\q1 'Twould have avoided hypocrisy now and misery at last.
\q1 'Twould have sought the alchemy of grace in due time;
\q1 Its reason would have prevailed over its hypocrisy.
\q1 If it became broken-hearted through its own bad state,
\q1 'Twould look onward to Him that heals the broken;
\q1 'Twould look to the result and be broken-hearted,
\q1 And be made whole at once by the Healer of broken hearts.
\q1 Divine grace places base copper in the alembic,
\q1 Adulterated gold is excluded from that favor.
\q1 O adulterated gold, boast not, but see clearly
\q1 That thy Purchaser is not blind to thy defects.
\q1 The light of the judgment-day will enlighten his eyes
\q1 And destroy the glamour of thy fascinations.
\q1 Behold them that have regard to the ultimate result,
\q1 And also the regrets of foolish souls and their envy.
\q1 Behold them that regard only the present,
\q1 And cast away thoughts of evil to come from their minds.
\v 14 \vp 4.1\vp* Once that famous saint Bayazid came to his disciples,
\q1 Saying, "Lo, I myself am God Almighty."
\q1 That man of spiritual gifts being visibly beside himself;
\q1 Said, "There is no God beside me; worship me!"
\q1 Next morning, when his ecstatic state had passed,
\q1 They said, "You said so and so, which was impious."
\q1 He answered, "If I do so again,
\q1 Straightway slay me with your knives!
\q1 God is independent of me; I am in the body.
\q1 If I say that again you must kill me!"
\q1 When that holy person had given this injunction,
\q1 Each of his disciples made ready his knife.
\q1 Again that overflowing cup became beside himself,
\q1 And his recent injunctions passed from his mind.
\q1 Alienation came upon him, reason went astray,
\q1 The dawn shone forth and his lamp paled at its light.
\q1 Reason is like an officer when the king appears;
\q1 The officer then loses his power and hides himself.
\q1 Reason is God's shadow; God is the sun.
\q1 What power has the shadow before the sun?
\q1 When a man is possessed by an evil spirit
\q1 The qualities of humanity are lost in him.
\q1 Whatever he says is really said by that spirit,
\q1 Though it seems to proceed from the man's mouth.
\q1 When the spirit has this rule and dominance over him,
\q1 The agent is the property of the spirit, and not himself;
\q1 His self is departed, and he has become the spirit.
\q1 The Turk without instruction speaks Arabic;
\q1 When he returns to himself he knows not a word of it.
\q1 Seeing God is lord of spirits and of man,
\q1 How can He be inferior in power to a spirit?
\q1 When the eagle of alienation from self took wing,
\q1 Bayazid began to utter similar speeches;
\q1 The torrent of madness bore away his reason,
\q1 And he spoke more impiously than before.
\q1 "Within my vesture is naught but God,
\q1 Whether you seek Him on earth or in heaven."
\q1 His disciples all became mad with horror,
\q1 And struck with their knives at his holy body.
\q1 Each one, like the assassins of Kardkoh,
\q1 Without fear aimed at the body of his chief.
\q1 Each who aimed at the body of the Shaikh,—
\q1 His stroke was reversed and wounded the striker.
\q1 No stroke took effect on that man of spiritual gifts,
\q1 But the disciples were wounded and drowned in blood.
\q1 Each who had aimed a blow at his neck,
\q1 Saw his own throat cut, and gave up the ghost;
\q1 He who had struck at his breast
\q1 Had cleft his own breast and killed himself.
\q1 They who knew better that lord of felicity,
\q1 Who had not courage enough to strike a deadly blow,
\q1 Their half-knowledge held their hands back;
\q1 They saved their lives but slightly wounded themselves.
\q1 On the morrow those disciples, diminished in number,
\q1 Raised lamentations in their houses.
\q1 They went to Bayazid, thousands of men and women,
\q1 Saying, "The two worlds are hidden in thy vesture;
\q1 If this body of thine were that of a man,
\q1 It would have perished of sword-wounds, like a man's."
\q1 The man in his senses fought with him 'beside himself,
\q1 And thrust the thorn into his own eyes."
\q1 Ah! you who smite with your sword him beside himself,
\q1 You smite yourself therewith; Beware!
\q1 For he that is beside himself is annihilated and safe;
\q1 Yea, he dwells in security forever.
\q1 His form is vanished, he is a mere mirror;
\q1 Nothing is seen in him but the reflection of another.
\q1 If you spit at it, you spit at your own face,
\q1 And if you hit that mirror, you hit yourself;
\q1 And if you see an ugly face in it, 'tis your own,
\q1 And if you see an 'Isa there, you are its mother Mary.
\q1 He is neither this nor that— he is void of form;
\q1 'Tis your own form which is reflected back to you.
\q1 But when the discourse reaches this point, lip is closed;
\q1 When pen reaches this point, it is split in twain.
\q1 Close then your lips, though eloquence be possible.
\q1 Keep silence; God knows the right way!
\v 15 \vp 4.2\vp* The Prophet said, "O ye who regard only the outside,
\q1 Regard him not as a youth void of talents.
\q1 Many are they whose beards are black yet are old,
\q1 Many too have white beards and hearts like pitch.
\q1 I have made trial of his wisdom often and often,
\q1 And that youth has shown himself old in his actions.
\q1 Age consists in maturity of wisdom, O son,
\q1 Not in whiteness of the beard and hair.
\q1 How can any one be older than Iblis?
\q1 Yet, if he has no wisdom, he is naught.
\q1 Suppose him an infant, if he has 'Isa's soul,
\q1 He is pure from pride and from carnal lust.
\q1 That whiteness of the hair is a sign of maturity
\q1 Only to purblind eyes whose vision is limited.
\q1 Since that shortsighted one judges by outward signs,
\q1 He seeks the right course by outward tokens.
\q1 For his sake I said that if ye desire counsel
\q1 Ye ought to make choice of an old man.
\q1 He who has emerged from the veil of blind belief
\q1 Beholds by the light of God all things that exist.
\q1 His pure light, without signs or tokens,
\q1 Cleaves for him the rind and brings him to the kernel.
\q1 To the regarder of externals, genuine and base coin are alike.
\q1 How can he know what is inside the basket?
\q1 Many are the gold coins made black with smoke,
\q1 So that they elude the clutches of greedy thieves;
\q1 Many are the copper coins gilded with gold,
\q1 And sold as gold to men of slender wits.
\q1 We who regard the inside of the world,
\q1 We look at the heart and disregard the outside.
\q1 The judges who confine their view to externals
\q1 And base their decisions on outward appearances,
\q1 As they testify and make outward show of faith,
\q1 Are straightway dubbed faithful by men of externals.
\q1 Therefore these heretics, who regard only externals,
\q1 Have secretly shed the blood of many true believers.
\q1 Strive then to be old in wisdom and in faith,
\q1 That, like Universal Reason, you may see within."
\v 16 \vp 5.1\vp* The wise man is he who possesses a torch of his own;
\q1 He is the guide and leader of the caravan.
\q1 That leader is his own director and light;
\q1 That illuminated one follows his own lead.
\q1 He is his own protector; do ye also seek protection
\q1 From that light whereon his soul is nurtured.
\q1 The second, he, namely, who is half wise,
\q1 Knows the wise man to be the light of his eyes.
\q1 He clings to the wise man like a blind man to his guide,
\q1 So as to become possessed of the wise man's sight.
\q1 But the fool, who has no particle of wisdom,
\q1 Has no wisdom of his own, and quits the wise man.
\q1 He knows nothing of the way, great or small,
\q1 And is ashamed to follow the footsteps of the guide.
\q1 He wanders into the boundless desert,
\q1 Sometimes halting and despairing, sometimes running.
\q1 He has no lamp wherewith to light himself on his way,
\q1 Nor half a lamp which might recognise and seek light.
\q1 He lacks wisdom, so as to boast of being alive,
\q1 And also half wisdom, so as to assume to be dead?
\q1 That half wise one became as one utterly dead
\q1 In order to rise up out of his degradation.
\q1 If you lack perfect wisdom, make yourself as dead
\q1 Under the shadow of the wise, whose words give life.
\q1 The fool is neither alive so as to companion with 'Isa,
\q1 Nor yet dead so as to feel the power of 'Isa's breath.
\q1 His blind soul wanders in every direction,
\q1 And at last makes a spring, but springs not upwards.
\v 17 \vp 5.2\vp* A man captured a bird by wiles and snares;
\q1 The bird said to him, "O noble sir,
\q1 In your time you have eaten many oxen and sheep,
\q1 And likewise sacrificed many camels;
\q1 You have never become satisfied with their meat,
\q1 So you will not be satisfied with my flesh.
\q1 Let me go, that I may give you three counsels,
\q1 Whence you will see whether I am wise or foolish.
\q1 The first of my counsels shall be given on your wrist,
\q1 The second on your well-plastered roof,
\q1 And the third I will give you from the top of a tree.
\q1 On hearing all three you will deem yourself happy.
\q1 As regards the counsel on your wrist, 'tis this,—
\q1 'Believe not foolish assertions of any one!'"
\q1 When he had spoken this counsel on his wrist, he flew
\q1 Up to the top of the roof, entirely free.
\q1 Then he said, "Do not grieve for what is past;
\q1 When a thing is done, vex not yourself about it."
\q1 He continued, "Hidden inside this body of mine
\q1 Is a precious pearl, ten drachms in weight.
\q1 That jewel of right belonged to you,
\q1 Wealth for yourself and prosperity for your children.
\q1 You have lost it, as it was not fated you should get it,
\q1 That pearl whose like can nowhere be found."
\q1 Thereupon the man, like a woman in her travail,
\q1 Gave vent to lamentations and weeping.
\q1 The bird said to him, "Did I not counsel you, saying,
\q1 'Beware of grieving over what is past and gone?'
\q1 When 'tis past and gone, why sorrow for it?
\q1 Either you understood not my counsel or are deaf.
\q1 The second counsel I gave you was this, namely,
\q1 'Be not misguided enough to believe foolish assertions.'
\q1 O fool, altogether I do not weigh three drachms,
\q1 How can a pearl of ten drachms be within me?"
\q1 The man recovered himself and said, "Well then,
\q1 Tell me now your third good counsel!"
\q1 The bird replied, "You have made a fine use of the others,
\q1 That I should waste my third counsel upon you!
\q1 To give counsel to a sleepy ignoramus
\q1 Is to sow seeds upon salt land.
\q1 Torn garments of folly and ignorance cannot be patched.
\q1 O counselors, waste not the seed of counsel on them!"
\v 18 \vp 6.1\vp* Destroy your house, and with the treasure hidden in it
\q1 You will be able to build thousands of houses.
\q1 The treasure lies under it; there is no help for it;
\q1 Hesitate not to pull it down; do not tarry!
\q1 For with the coin of that treasure
\q1 A thousand houses can be built without labour.
\q1 At last of a surety that house will be destroyed,
\q1 And the divine treasure will be seen beneath it.
\q1 But 'twill not belong to you, because in truth
\q1 That prize is the wages for destroying the house.
\q1 When one has not done the work he gets no wages;
\q1 "Man gets nothing he has not worked for."
\q1 Then you will bite your finger, saying, "Alas!
\q1 That bright moon was hidden under a cloud.
\q1 I did not do what they told me for my good;
\q1 Now house and treasure are lost and my hand is empty."
\q1 You have taken your house on lease or hired it;
\q1 'Tis not your own property to buy and sell.
\q1 As to the term of the lease, it is till your death;
\q1 In that term you have to turn it to use.
\q1 If before the end of the term of the lease
\q1 You omit to derive profit from the house,
\q1 Then the owner puts you out of it,
\q1 And pulls it down himself to find the gold-mine.
\q1 While you are now smiting your head in deep regret,
\q1 And now tearing your beard to think of your folly,
\q1 Saying, "Alas! that house belonged to me;
\q1 I was blind and did not derive profit from it.
\q1 Alas! the wind has carried off my dwelling
\q1 Forever! 'O misery that rests on slaves!'
\q1 In that house of mine I saw but forms and pictures;
\q1 I was enchanted with that house so fleeting!
\q1 I was ignorant of the treasure hidden beneath it,
\q1 Otherwise I would have grasped an axe as a perfume.
\q1 Ah! if I had administered the justice of the axe,
\q1 I should now have been quit of sorrow.
\q1 But I fixed my gaze on outward forms,
\q1 Like an infant I sported with playthings.
\q1 Well said the famous Hakim Sanai,
\q1 'Thou art a child; thy house is full of pictures.'
\q1 In his divine poem he gives this advice,
\q1 'Sweep away the dust from thy house!'"
\v 19 \vp 6.2\vp* "O Pharaoh, if you are wise, I show you mercy;
\q1 But if you are an ass, I give you the stick as an ass.
\q1 So I will drive you out of your stable,
\q1 Even as I make your head and ears bleed with my stick.
\q1 In this stable asses and men alike
\q1 Are deprived of peace by your oppressions.
\q1 See! I have brought a staff for the purpose of correcting
\q1 Every ass who does not prove tractable.
\q1 It turns into a serpent in vengeance against you,
\q1 Because you have become a serpent in deed and character.
\q1 You are an evil serpent, swelled to the size of a hill.
\q1 Yet look at the Serpent (constellation) in heaven.
\q1 This staff is a foretaste to you of hell,
\q1 Saying, 'Ho, take refuge in the light!
\q1 Otherwise you will fall into my jaws,
\q1 And will find no escape from my clutches!'
\q1 This staff even now became a serpent,
\q1 So that you need not ask, 'Where is God's hell?'
\q1 God makes a hell wheresover He wills;
\q1 He makes the very sky a snare and trap for birds.
\q1 He produces pains and aches in your teeth,
\q1 So that you say, "Tis a hell and serpent's bite.'
\q1 Or again He makes your spittle as honey,
\q1 So that you say, ''Tis heaven and wine of Paradise.'
\q1 He makes sugar to grow in your mouth,
\q1 That you may know the might of the divine decrees.
\q1 Therefore, bite not the innocent with your teeth;
\q1 Bear in mind the divine stroke that tarries not."
\q1 God made the Nile blood to the Egyptians,
\q1 He preserved the Israelites from the peril,
\q1 That you might know how God discerns
\q1 Between the wise and the foolish wayfarers.
\q1 The Nile learned of God discernment
\q1 When it let the ones through and engulphed the others.
\q1 God's mercy made the Nile wise,
\q1 His wrath made Cain foolish.
\q1 Of His mercy He created wisdom in inanimate things,
\q1 And of His wrath He deprived the wise of wisdom.
\q1 Of His mercy wisdom accrued to inanimate things,
\q1 As a chastisement He took wisdom from the wise.
\q1 Here at His command wisdom was shed down like rain,
\q1 Whilst there wisdom saw His wrath and fled away.
\q1 Clouds and sun, and moon and lofty stars,
\q1 All come and go in obedience to His ordinance;
\q1 No one of them comes save at His appointed time;
\q1 It lingers not behind nor anticipates that time.
\q1 Whereas you understood not this secret, the prophets
\q1 Have instilled this knowledge into stone and staff;
\q1 So that you may infer that other inanimate things
\q1 Without doubt resemble in this stones and staves.
\q1 The obedience of stone and staff is shown to you,
\q1 And informs you of that of other inanimate things.
\q1 They cry, "We are all aware of God and obey Him;
\q1 We are not destructive by mere fortuitous chance."
\q1 Thus you know the water of the Nile when in flood
\q1 Made distinction between the Egyptians and the Israelites.
\q1 You know the others are wise as earth, who, when cleft,
\q1 Knew Qarun and swallowed him up in vengeance.
\q1 Or like the moon, who heard the command and hasted
\q1 To sever itself into two halves in the sky.
\q1 Or like the trees and stones, which in all places
\q1 Were seen to bow down at the feet of Mustafa.
\v 20 \vp 6.3\vp* Last night a Sunni said, "The world is transitory;
\q1 The heavens will pass away; 'God will be the heir.'"
\q1 A philosopher replied, "How know you they are transitory?
\q1 How knows the rain the transitory nature of the cloud?
\q1 Are you not a mere mote floating in the sunbeams?
\q1 How know you that the sun is transitory?
\q1 A mere worm buried in a dung-heap,
\q1 How can it know the origin and end of the earth?
\q1 In blind belief you have accepted this from your father,
\q1 And through folly have clung to it ever since.
\q1 Tell me what is the proof of its transitoriness,
\q1 Or else be silent and indulge not in idle talk."
\q1 The Sunni said, "One day I saw two persons
\q1 Engaged in argument on this deep question,—
\q1 Yea, in dispute and controversy and argument.
\q1 At last a crowd was gathered round them.
\q1 I proceeded towards that company
\q1 To inform myself of the subject of their discourse.
\q1 One said, 'This sky will pass away;
\q1 Doubtless this building had a builder.'
\q1 The other said, 'It is eternal and without period;
\q1 It had no builder, or it was its own builder.'
\q1 The first said, 'Do you then deny the Creator,
\q1 The Bringer of day and night, the Sustainer of men?'
\q1 He answered, 'Without proof I will not listen
\q1 To what you say; 'tis only based on blind belief.
\q1 Go! bring proof and evidence, for never
\q1 Will I accept this statement without proof.'
\q1 He answered, 'The proof is within my heart,
\q1 Yea, my proofs are hidden in my heart.
\q1 From weakness of vision you see not the new moon;
\q1 If I see it, be not angry with me!'
\q1 Much talk followed, and the people were perplexed
\q1 About the origin and end of the revolving heavens.
\q1 Then the first said, 'O friend, within me is a proof
\q1 Which assures me of the transitoriness of the heavens.
\q1 I hold it for certain, and the sign of certainty
\q1 In him who possesses it is entering into fire.
\q1 Know this proof is not to be expressed in speech,
\q1 Any more than the feeling of love felt by lovers.
\q1 The secret I labour to express is not revealed
\q1 Save by the pallor and emaciation of my face.
\q1 When the tears course down my cheeks,
\q1 They are a proof of the beauty and grace of my beloved.'
\q1 The other said, "I take not these for a proof,
\q1 Though they may be a proof to common people."
\q1 The Sunni said, "When genuine and base coin boast,
\q1 Saying, 'Thou art false, I am good and genuine,'
\q1 Fire is the test ultimately,
\q1 When the two rivals are cast into the furnace."
\q1 Accordingly both of them entered the furnace,
\q1 Both leapt into the fiery flame;
\q1 And the philosopher was burnt to ashes,
\q1 But the God-fearing Sunni was made fairer than before.
\v 21 \vp 7.1\vp* Moses said, "O Lord of the day of account,
\q1 Thou makest forms; wherefore, then, destroyest Thou them?
\q1 Thou makest charming forms, both men and women;
\q1 Wherefore, then, dost Thou lay them waste?"
\q1 God answered, "I know that this query of thine
\q1 Proceeds not from negation or vain curiosity.
\q1 Otherwise I should chastise and punish thee;
\q1 Yea, I should rebuke thee for this question.
\q1 But thou seekest to discover in my actions
\q1 The ruling principle and the eternal mystery,
\q1 In order to inform the people thereof,
\q1 And to make 'ripe' every 'raw' person.
\q1 Yea, O messenger, thou questionest me that I may reveal
\q1 My ways to the people, though thou knowest them.
\q1 O Moses, go and sow seed in the ground
\q1 In order to do justice to this question."
\q1 When Moses had sowed and his seed had grown up,
\q1 He took a sickle and reaped the corn,
\q1 And then a divine voice reached his ears:—
\q1 "Why hast thou sown and nurtured the corn,
\q1 And then cut it down directly it was ripe?"
\q1 Moses replied, "Lord, I cut it and lay it low
\q1 Because here I have grain and straw.
\q1 Grain is out of place in the straw-yard,
\q1 And straw is useless in the wheat-barn.
\q1 'Tis wrong to mix these two,
\q1 It is needful to sift them one from the other."
\q1 God said, "From whom learnest thou this knowledge
\q1 Whereby thou hast constructed a threshing-floor?"
\q1 Moses said, "O Lord, Thou hast given me discernment."
\q1 God said, "Then have not I also discernment?
\q1 Amongst my creatures there are pure spirits,
\q1 And also dark and befouled spirits.
\q1 The oyster-shells are not all of the same value;
\q1 Some contain pearls, and others black stones.
\q1 It is needful to discern the bad from the good,
\q1 Just as much as to sift wheat from straw.
\q1 The people of this world exist in order to manifest
\q1 And to disclose the 'hidden treasure.'
\q1 Read, 'I was a hidden treasure, and desired to be known;
\q1 Hide not the hidden treasure, but disclose it.
\q1 Your true treasure is hidden under a false one,
\q1 Just as butter is hidden within the substance of milk.
\q1 The false one is this transitory body of yours,
\q1 The true one your divine soul.
\q1 Long time this milk is exposed to view,
\q1 And the soul's butter is hidden and of no account.
\q1 Stir up your milk assiduously with knowledge,
\q1 So that what is hidden in it may be disclosed;
\q1 Because this mortal is the guide to immortality,
\q1 As the cries of revellers indicate the cup-bearer."
\v 22 \vp 8.1\vp* The whole world is the outward form of Universal Reason,
\q1 For it is the father of all creatures of reason.
\q1 When a man acts basely towards Universal Reason,
\q1 Its form, the world, shows its teeth at him.
\q1 Be loyal to this father and renounce disobedience,
\q1 That this earthy house may furnish you golden carpets.
\q1 Then the judgment-day will be the "cash of your state,"
\q1 Earth and heavens will be transfigured before you.
\q1 I am ever in concord with this father of ours,
\q1 And earth ever appears to me as a Paradise.
\q1 Each moment a fresh form, a new beauty,
\q1 So that weariness vanishes at these ever-fresh sights.
\q1 I see the world filled with blessings,
\q1 Fresh waters ever welling up from new fountains.
\q1 The sound of those waters reaches my ears,
\q1 My brain and senses are intoxicated therewith.
\q1 Branches of trees dancing like fair damsels,
\q1 Leaves clapping hands like singers.
\q1 These glories are a mirror shining through a veil;
\q1 If the mirror were unveiled, how would it be?
\q1 I tell not one in a thousand of them,
\q1 Because every ear is stopped with doubt.
\q1 To men of illusions these tales are mere good tidings,
\q1 But men of knowledge deem them not tidings, but ready cash.
\v 23 \vp 9.1\vp* First he appeared in the class of inorganic things,
\q1 Next he passed therefrom into that of plants.
\q1 For years he lived as one of the plants,
\q1 Remembering naught of his inorganic state so different;
\q1 And when he passed from the vegetive to the animal state
\q1 He had no remembrance of his state as a plant,
\q1 Except the inclination he felt to the world of plants,
\q1 Especially at the time of spring and sweet flowers.
\q1 Like the inclination of infants towards their mothers,
\q1 Which know not the cause of their inclination to the breast,
\q1 Or the excessive inclination of young disciples
\q1 Towards their noble and illustrious teachers.
\q1 The disciple's partial reason comes from that Reason,
\q1 The disciple's shadow is from that bough.
\q1 When the shadows in the disciples cease,
\q1 They know the reason of their attachment to the teachers.
\q1 For, O fortunate one, how can the shadow move,
\q1 Unless the tree that casts the shadow move as well?
\q1 Again, the great Creator, as you know,
\q1 Drew man out of the animal into the human state.
\q1 Thus man passed from one order of nature to another,
\q1 Till he became wise and knowing and strong as he is now.
\q1 Of his first souls he has now no remembrance,
\q1 And he will be again changed from his present soul.
\q1 In order to escape from his present soul full of lusts
\q1 He must behold thousands of reasonable souls.
\q1 Though man fell asleep and forgot his previous states,
\q1 Yet God will not leave him in this self-forgetfulness;
\q1 And then he will laugh at his own former state
\q1 Saying, " What mattered my experiences when asleep?
\q1 When I had forgotten my prosperous condition,
\q1 And knew not that the grief and ills I experienced
\q1 Were the effect of sleep and illusion and fancy?
\q1 In like manner this world, which is only a dream.
\q1 Seems to the sleeper as a thing enduring for ever
\q1 But when the morn of the last day shall dawn,
\q1 The sleeper will escape from the cloud of illusion;
\q1 Laughter will overpower him at his own fancied griefs
\q1 When he beholds his abiding home and place.
\q1 Whatever you see in this sleep, both good and evil,
\q1 Will all be exposed to view on the resurrection day.
\q1 Whatever you have done during your sleep in the world
\q1 Will be displayed to you clearly when you awake.
\q1 Imagine not that these ill deeds of yours exist not
\q1 In this sleep of yours, and will not be revealed to you.
\q1 But your present laughter will turn to weeping and woe
\q1 On the day of revealing, O you who oppress captives!
\q1 Your present wailing and sorrow and griefs,
\q1 On the other hand, will be joy when you awake,
\q1 O you, who have rent the garments of many Josephs,
\q1 You will rise from your heavy sleep as a wolf.
\q1 Your bad qualities will rise in the shape of wolves
\q1 And rend you limb from limb in vengeance.
\q1 By the law of retaliation blood sleeps not after death;
\q1 Say not, "I shall die and obtain pardon."
\q1 The retaliation of this world is illusive,
\q1 It is mere sport compared to the retaliation to come.
\q1 Therefore God calls the world "a pastime and a sport,"
\q1 For punishment in this world is sport compared to that.
\q1 Here punishment is as the repression of quarrels,
\q1 There it is as castration or circumcision.
\q1 But this discourse is endless, O Moses,
\q1 Go and leave these asses to their grazing!
\q1 Let them fatten themselves with the food they love,
\q1 For they are very wolves and objects of my wrath.
\v 24 \vp 9.2\vp* Zu'l Qarnain journeyed to Mount Qaf;
\q1 He saw it was formed of a bright emerald,
\q1 Forming as it were a ring round the world,
\q1 Whereat all people are filled with wonder.
\q1 He said, "Thou mighty hill, what are other hills?
\q1 Before thee they are mere playthings."
\q1 The Mount replied, "Those hills are my veins,
\q1 But they are not like me in beauty and importance.
\q1 A hidden vein from me runs to every city,
\q1 The quarters of the world are bound to my veins.
\q1 When God desires an earthquake under any city,
\q1 He bids me shake one of my veins.
\q1 Then in anger I shake that vein
\q1 Which is connected with that particular city.
\q1 When He says, 'Enough,' my vein remains still,
\q1 I remain still, and then haste to perform my work.
\q1 Now still like a plaster, and now operating;
\q1 Now still like thought, and then speaking my thought.
\q1 But they who are void of reason imagine
\q1 That these earthquakes proceed from earth's vapours."
\q1 Just so an ant, who saw a pen writing on paper,
\q1 Delivered himself to another ant in this way:—
\q1 'That pen is making very wonderful figures,
\q1 Like hyacinths and lilies and roses.'
\q1 The other said, 'The finger is the real worker,
\q1 The pen is only the instrument of its working.'
\q1 A third ant said, ' No; the action proceeds from the arm,
\q1 The weak finger writes with the arm's might.'
\q1 So it went on upwards, till at last
\q1 A prince of the ants, who had some wit,
\q1 Said, 'Ye regard only the outward form of this marvel,
\q1 Which form becomes senseless in sleep and death.
\q1 Form is only as a dress or a staff in the hand,
\q1 It is only from reason and mind these figures proceed.'
\q1 But he knew not that this reason and mind
\q1 Would be but lifeless things without God's impulsion.
\v 25 \vp 9.3\vp* Mustafa said to the angel Gabriel,
\q1 "O friend, show me thy form as it really is;
\q1 Show it to me openly and perceptibly,
\q1 That I may behold thee with my eyes."
\q1 Gabriel said, "Thou canst not do so, thou art too weak,
\q1 Thy senses are exceeding weak and frail."
\q1 Muhammad said, "Show it, that this body of mine may see
\q1 To what extent its senses are frail and impotent.
\q1 True, man's bodily senses are frail,
\q1 But he possesses within him a mighty property.
\q1 This body resembles flint and steel,
\q1 But like them it has the power of kindling fire.
\q1 Flint and steel are able to generate fire,
\q1 From them springs fire which can destroy its parents."
\q1 As he continued importuning him, Gabriel displayed
\q1 His awful form, whereat the mountains were rent asunder.
\q1 It occupied the sky from east to west.
\q1 And Mustafa swooned with fear.
\q1 When Gabriel beheld him swooning with fear,
\q1 He came and clasped him in his arms.
\v 26 \vp 9.4\vp* O light of God, Husamu-'d-Din, admit
\q1 This ass's head into that melon-garden!
\q1 For when this ass is killed in the slaughter-house
\q1 That kitchen will bestow upon him a new existence.
\q1 From me proceeds the form, from thee the spirit;
\q1 Nay, form and spirit both proceed from thee!
\q1 Thou art as Muhammad in heaven, O brilliant Sun!
\q1 Be also as Muhammad on earth forever and ever!
\q1 So that earth and heaven on high may be united
\q1 With one heart, one worship, one aspiration!
\q1 And schism and polytheism and duality disappear,
\q1 And Unity abide in the Real Spiritual Being!
\q1 When my spirit recognises thy spirit,
\q1 We remember our essential union and origin.
\c 5
\cp 5
\p
\v 1 \vp 1.1\vp* Prayer and fasting and pilgrimage and holy war
\q1 Bear witness of the faith of the heart.
\q1 Giving alms and offerings and quitting avarice
\q1 Also bear witness of the secret thoughts.
\q1 So, a table spread for guests serves as a plain sign,
\q1 Saying, "O guest, I am your sincere well-wisher."
\q1 So, offerings and presents and oblations
\q1 Bear witness, saying, "I am well pleased with you."
\q1 Each of these men lavishes his wealth or pains,—
\q1 What means it but to say, "I have a virtue within me,
\q1 Yea, a virtue of piety or liberality,
\q1 Whereof my oblations and fasting bear witness"?
\q1 Fasting proclaims that he abstains from lawful food,
\q1 And that therefore he doubtless avoids unlawful food.
\q1 And his alms say, "He gives away his own goods;
\q1 It is therefore plain that he does not rob others."
\q1 If he acts thus from fraud, his two witnesses
\q1 (Fasting and alms) are rejected in God's court;—
\q1 If the hunter scatters grain
\q1 Not out of mercy, but to catch game;—
\q1 If the cat keeps fast, and remains still
\q1 In fasting only to entrap unwary birds;—
\q1 Making hundreds of people suspicious,
\q1 And giving a bad name to men who fast and are liberal;—
\q1 Yet the grace of God, despite this fraud,
\q1 May ultimately purge him from all this hypocrisy.
\q1 Mercy may prevail over vengeance, and give the hypocrite
\q1 Such light as is not possessed by the full moon.
\q1 God may purge his dealings from that hypocrisy,
\q1 And in mercy wash him clean of that defilement.
\q1 In order that the pardoning grace of God may be seen,
\q1 God pardons all sins that need pardon.
\q1 Wherefore God rains down water from the sign Pisces,
\q1 To purify the impure from their impurities.
\q1 Thus acts and words are witnesses of the mind within,
\q1 From these two deduce inferences as to the thoughts.
\q1 When your vision cannot penetrate within,
\q1 Inspect the water voided by the sick man.
\q1 Acts and words resemble the sick man's water,
\q1 Which serves as evidence to the physician of the body.
\q1 But the physician of the spirit penetrates the soul,
\q1 And thence ascertains the man's faith.
\q1 Such an one needs not the evidence of fair acts and words;
\q1 "Beware of such, they spy out the heart."
\q1 Require this evidence of act and word only from one
\q1 Who is not joined to the divine Ocean like a stream.
\q1 But the light of the traveller arrived at the goal,
\q1 Verily that light fills deserts and wastes.
\q1 That witness of his is exempt from bearing witness,
\q1 And from all trouble and risk and good works.
\q1 Since the brilliance of that jewel beams forth,
\q1 It is exempted from these obligations.
\q1 Wherefore require not from him act and word evidence,
\q1 Because both worlds through him bloom like roses.
\q1 What is this evidence but manifestation of hidden things,
\q1 Whether it be evidence in word, or deed, or otherwise?
\q1 Accidents serve only to manifest the secret essence;
\q1 The essential quality abides, and accidents pass away.
\q1 This mark of gold endures not the touchstone,
\q1 But only the gold itself, genuine and undoubted.
\q1 These prayers and holy war and fasting
\q1 Will not endure, only the noble soul endures.
\q1 The soul exhibits acts and words of this sort,
\q1 Then it rubs its substance on the touchstone of God's command,
\q1 Saying, "My faith is true, behold my witnesses!"
\q1 But witnesses are open to suspicion.
\q1 Know that witnesses must be purified,
\q1 And their purification is sincerity, on that you may depend.
\q1 The witness of word consists in speaking the truth,
\q1 The witness of acts in keeping one's promises.
\q1 If the witness of word lie, its evidence is rejected,
\q1 And if the witness of act play false, it is rejected.
\q1 Your words and acts must be without self-contradiction
\q1 In order to be accepted without question.
\q1 "Your aims are different," and you contradict yourselves,
\q1 You sew by day, and tear to pieces by night.
\q1 How can God listen to such contradictory witness,
\q1 Unless He be pleased to decide on it in mercy?
\q1 Act and word manifest the secret thoughts and mind,
\q1 Both of them expose to view the veiled secret.
\q1 When your witnesses are purified they are accepted,
\q1 Otherwise they are arrested and kept in durance.
\q1 They enter into conflict with you, O stiff-necked one;
\q1 "Stand aloof and wait for them, for they too wait."
\v 2 \vp 1.2\vp* O God, who hast no peer, bestow Thy favour upon me;
\q1 Since Thou hast with this discourse put a ring in my ear,
\q1 Take me by the ear, and draw me into that holy assembly
\q1 Where Thy saints in ecstasy drink of Thy pure wine!
\q1 Now that Thou hast caused me to smell its perfume,
\q1 Withhold not from me that musky wine, O Lord of faith!
\q1 Of Thy bounty all partake, both men and women,
\q1 Thou art ungrudging in bounties, O Hearer of prayer.
\q1 Prayers are granted by Thee before they are uttered,
\q1 Thou openest the door to admit hearts every moment!
\q1 How many letters Thou writest with Thy Almighty pen!
\q1 Through marvelling thereat stones become as wax.
\q1 Thou writest the Nun of the brow, the Sad of the eye,
\q1 And the Jim of the ear, to amaze reason and sense.
\q1 These letters exercise and perplex reason;
\q1 Write on, O skilful Fair-writer!
\q1 Imprinting every moment on Not-being the fair forms
\q1 Of the world of ideals, to confound all thought!
\q1 Yea, copying thereon the fair letters of the page of ideals,
\q1 To wit, eye and brow and moustache and mole!
\q1 For me, I will be a lover of Not-being, not of existence,
\q1 Because the beloved of Not-being is more blessed.
\q1 God made reason a reader of all these letters,
\q1 To suggest to it reflections on that outpouring of grace.
\q1 Reason, like Gabriel, learns day by day
\q1 Its daily portion from the "Indelible Tablet."
\q1 Behold the letters written without hands on Not-being!
\q1 Behold the perplexity of mankind at those letters!
\q1 Every one is bewildered by these thoughts,
\q1 And digs for hidden treasure in hope to find it.
\v 3 \vp 3.1\vp* Tear not thy plumage off, it cannot be replaced;
\q1 Disfigure not thy face in wantonness, O fair one!
\q1 That face which is bright as the forenoon sun,—
\q1 To disfigure it were a grievous sin.
\q1 'Twere paganism to mar such a face as thine!
\q1 The moon itself would weep to lose sight of it!
\q1 Knowest thou not the beauty of thine own face?
\q1 Quit this temper that leads thee to war with thyself!
\q1 It is the claws of thine own foolish thoughts
\q1 That in spite wound the face of thy quiet soul.
\q1 Know such thoughts to be claws fraught with poison,
\q1 Which score deep wounds on the face of thy soul.
\q1 Rend not thy plumage off, but avert thy heart from it,
\q1 For hostility between them is the law of this holy war.
\q1 Were there no hostility, that war would be impossible.
\q1 Hadst thou no lust, obedience to the law could not be.
\q1 Hadst thou no concupiscence there could be no abstinence.
\q1 Where no antagonist, exists what need is there of armies?
\q1 Ah! make not thyself an eunuch, become not a monk,
\q1 Because chastity is mortgaged to lust.
\q1 Without lust denial of lust is impossible;
\q1 No man can display bravery against the dead.
\q1 God says, "Expend;" wherefore earn money.
\q1 Since expenditure is impossible without previous gain?
\q1 Although the passage contains only the word "Expend,"
\q1 Read "Acquire first, and then expend."
\q1 In like manner, when the King of kings says "Abstain,"
\q1 It implies an object of desire wherefrom to abstain.
\q1 Again, "Eat ye," is said recognising the snares of lust,
\q1 And afterwards, " Exceed not," to enjoin temperance.
\q1 When there is no subject,
\q1 The existence of a predicate is not possible.
\q1 When thou endurest not the pains of abstinence,
\q1 And fulfillest not the terms, thou gainest no reward.
\q1 How easy those terms! how abundant that reward!—
\q1 A reward that enchants the heart and charms the soul!
\v 4 \vp 3.2\vp* O Thou that changest earth into gold,
\q1 And out of other earth madest the father of mankind,
\q1 Thy business is changing things and bestowing favours,
\q1 My business is mistakes and forgetfulness and error.
\q1 Change my mistakes and forgetfulness to knowledge;
\q1 I am altogether vile, make me temperate and meek.
\q1 O Thou that convertest salt earth into bread,
\q1 And bread again into the life of men;
\q1 Thou who madest the erring soul a guide to men,
\q1 And him that erred from the way a prophet;
\q1 Thou makest some earth-born men as heaven,
\q1 And multipliest heaven-born saints on earth!
\q1 But whoso seeks his water of life in worldly joys,
\q1 To him comes death quicker than to the rest.
\q1 The eyes of the heart which behold the heavens
\q1 See that the Almighty Alchemist is ever working here.
\q1 Mankind are ever being changed, and God's elixir
\q1 Joins the body's garment without aid of needle.
\q1 On the day that you entered upon existence,
\q1 You were first fire, or earth, or air.
\q1 If you had continued in that, your original state,
\q1 How could you have arrived at this dignity of humanity?
\q1 But through change your first existence remained not;
\q1 In lieu thereof God gave you a better existence
\q1 In like manner He will give you thousands of existences,
\q1 One after another, the succeeding ones better than the former.
\q1 Regard your original state, not the mean states,
\q1 For these mean states remove you from your origin.
\q1 As these mean states increase, union recedes;
\q1 As they decrease, the unction of union increases.
\q1 From knowing means and causes holy bewilderment fails;
\q1 Yea, the bewilderment that leads you to God's presence.
\q1 You have obtained these existences after annihilations;
\q1 Wherefore, then, do you shrink from annihilation?
\q1 What harm have these annihilations done you
\q1 That you cling so to present existence, O simpleton?
\q1 Since the latter of your states were better than the former,
\q1 Seek annihilation and adore change of state.
\q1 You have already seen hundreds of resurrections
\q1 Occur every moment from your origin till now;
\q1 One from the inorganic state to the vegetive state,
\q1 From the vegetive state to the animal state of trial;
\q1 Thence again to rationality and good discernment;
\q1 Again you will rise from this world of sense and form.
\q1 Ah! O crow, give up this life and live anew!
\q1 In view of God's changes cast away your life!
\q1 Choose the new, give up the old,
\q1 For each single present year is better than three past.
\v 5 \vp 4.1\vp* Thus spake cursed Iblis to the Almighty,
\q1 "I want a mighty trap to catch human game withal."
\q1 God gave him gold and silver and troops of horses,
\q1 Saying, "You can catch my creatures with these."
\q1 Iblis said, "Bravo!" but at the same time hung his lip,
\q1 And frowned sourly like a bitter orange.
\q1 Then God offered gold and jewels from precious mines
\q1 To that laggard in the faith,
\q1 Saying, "Take these other traps, O cursed one."
\q1 But Iblis said, "Give me more, O blessed Defender."
\q1 God gave him succulent and sweet and costly wines,
\q1 And also store of silken garments.
\q1 But Iblis said, " O Lord, I want more aids than these,
\q1 In order to bind men in my twisted rope
\q1 So firmly that Thy adorers, who are valiant men,
\q1 May not, man-like, break my bonds asunder."
\q1 When at last God showed him the beauty of women,
\q1 Which bereaves men of reason and self-control,
\q1 Then Iblis clapped his hands and began to dance,
\q1 Saying, "Give me these; I shall quickly prevail with these!"
\v 6 \vp 4.2\vp* "If thou goest the road, they will show thee the road;
\q1 If thou becomest naught, they will turn thee to being."
\v 7 \vp 5.1\vp* O Thou that givest aliment and power and stability,
\q1 Set free the people from their instability.
\q1 To the soul that is bent double by envy
\q1 Give uprightness in the path of duty,
\q1 Give them self-control, "weigh down their scales,"
\q1 Release them from the arts of deceivers.
\q1 Redeem them from envying, O gracious One,
\q1 That through envy they be not stoned like Iblis.
\q1 Even in their fleeting prosperity, see how the people
\q1 Burn up wealth and men through envy!
\q1 See the kings who lead forth their armies
\q1 To slay their own people from envy!
\q1 Lovers of sweethearts have conceived jealousy,
\q1 And attempted one another's lives,
\q1 Read " Wais and Ramin" and "Khosrau and Shirin",
\q1 To see what these fools have done to one another.
\q1 Lovers and beloved have both perished;
\q1 And not themselves only, but their love as well.
\q1 'Tis God alone who agitates these nonentities,
\q1 Making one nonentity fall in love with another.
\q1 In the heart that is no heart envy comes to a head,
\q1 Thus Being troubles nonentity.
\v 8 \vp 6.1\vp* When a friend tells a joke to his friend,
\q1 The deaf man who listens laughs twice over;—
\q1 The first time from imitation and foolishness,
\q1 Because he sees all the party laughing;—
\q1 Yet, though he laughs like the others,
\q1 He is then ignorant of the subject of their laughter;—
\q1 Then he inquires what the laughter was about,
\q1 And, on hearing it, proceeds to laugh a second time.
\q1 Wherefore the blind imitator is like a deaf man,
\q1 In regard to the joy he feigns to feel.
\q1 The light is the Shaikh's, the fountain the Shaikh's,
\q1 And the outpouring of joy is also the Shaikh's, not his.
\q1 'Tis like water in a vessel, or light through a glass;
\q1 If they think they come from themselves, they are wrong.
\q1 When the vessel leaves the fountain, it sees its error;
\q1 It sees the water in it comes from the fountain.
\q1 The glass also learns, when the moon sets,
\q1 That its light proceeded from the shining of the moon.
\q1 When his eyes are opened by the command, "Arise!"
\q1 Then that disciple smiles a second time, like the dawn.
\q1 He laughs also at his own previous laughter,
\q1 Which overtook him out of mere blind imitation.
\q1 When he returns from his long and distant wanderings
\q1 He says, "Lo! this was the truth, this the secret!
\q1 With what blindness and misconception did I pretend
\q1 To experience joy in that distant valley?
\q1 What a delusion I was under! what a mistake!
\q1 My feeble wit conjured up vain imaginations."
\q1 How can an infant on the road know the thoughts of men?
\q1 How far its fancies are removed from true knowledge!
\q1 The thoughts of infants run on the nurse and milk,
\q1 Or on raisins or nuts, or on crying and wailing.
\q1 The blind imitator is like a feeble infant,
\q1 Even though he possesses fine arguments and proofs.
\q1 His preoccupation with obscure arguments and proofs
\q1 Drags him away from insight into truth.
\q1 His stock of lore, which is the salve of his eyes,
\q1 Bears him off and plunges him in difficult questions.
\q1 Ah! man of imitation, come out of Bokhara!
\q1 And humble yourself in order to be exalted.
\q1 Then you will behold another Bokhara within you,
\q1 Whereof the heroes ignore these questions of law.
\q1 Though a footman may be swift of foot on land,
\q1 Yet on the sea he is as one with ruptured tendons.
\q1 That footman is only "carried by land,"
\q1 But he who is "carried by sea" is the truly learned one.
\q1 The King of kings showers special favours upon him;
\q1 Know this, O man pledged to vain illusions!
\v 9 \vp 6.2\vp* When the day dawns from heaven night flees away;
\q1 What, then, can its darkness know of the nature of light?
\q1 The gnat scuds away before the blast of the winds;
\q1 What, then, knows the gnat of the savour of the winds?
\q1 When the Eternal appears the transitory is annulled;
\q1 What, then, knows the transitory of the Eternal?
\q1 When He sets foot on the transitory He bewilders it;
\q1 When it is become naught He sheds his light upon it.
\q1 If you wish, you can adduce hundreds of precedents,
\q1 But I take no heed of them, O man poor in spirit!
\q1 The letters Lam, Mim, and Ha, Mim prefixed to some Suras
\q1 Resemble the staff of Moses, when fully understood.
\q1 Ordinary letters resemble these 'to outward view,
\q1 But are far beneath them in signification.
\q1 If an ordinary man 'take a staff and try it,
\q1 Will it prove like the staff of Moses in the test?
\q1 This breath of 'Isa is not like every ordinary breath,
\q1 Which proceeds from mere human joy or sorrow.
\q1 These Alif, Mim, Ha and Mim, O father,
\q1 Proceed from the Lord of mankind.
\q1 If you have sense, regard not in the same way as these
\q1 Every ordinary Alif and Lam which resembles these;
\q1 Although these sacred letters consist of common ones,
\q1 And resemble common ones in their composition.
\q1 Muhammad himself was formed of flesh and skin,
\q1 Although no man is of the same genus as he.
\q1 He had flesh and skin and bones,
\q1 Although no man resembles him in composition;
\q1 Because in his composition were contained divine powers,
\q1 Whereby all human flesh was confounded.
\q1 In like manner the composition of the letters Ha, Mim
\q1 Is far exalted above ordinary compounds of letters;
\q1 Because from these mysterious compositions comes life,
\q1 Even as utter confusion follows the last trump.
\q1 That staff becomes a serpent and divides the Nile,
\q1 Like the staff of Ha, Mim, by the grace of God.
\q1 Its outward form resembles the outward forms of others,
\q1 Yet the disk of a cake differs much from the moon's disk.
\q1 The saint's weeping and laughter and speech
\q1 Are not his own, but proceed from God.
\q1 Whereas fools look only to outward appearances,
\q1 These mysteries are totally hidden from them;
\q1 Of necessity the real meaning is veiled from them,
\q1 For the mystery is lost in the intervening medium.
\v 10 \vp 7.1\vp* Do not, like fools, crave mercy from the spear,
\q1 But from the King in whose hand the spear is held.
\q1 Wherefore do you cry to spear and sword,
\q1 Seeing they are captives in the hand of that Noble One?
\q1 He is as Azar, maker of idols; I am only the idol;
\q1 Whatever instrument He makes me, that I am.
\q1 If He makes of me a cup, a cup am I;
\q1 If He makes of me a dagger, a dagger am I.
\q1 If He makes me a fountain, I pour forth water;
\q1 If He makes me fire, I give forth heat.
\q1 If He makes me rain, I produce rich crops;
\q1 If He makes me a dart, I pierce bodies.
\q1 If He makes me a snake, I dart forth poison;
\q1 If He makes me a friend, I serve my friends.
\q1 I am as the pen in the fingers of the writer,
\q1 I am not in a position to obey or not at will.
\v 11 \vp 7.2\vp* One said, "The world would be a pleasant place
\q1 If death never set foot within it."
\q1 Another answered, "If there were no death,
\q1 The complicated world would be worth not a jot.
\q1 It would be a crop raised in a desert,
\q1 Left neglected and never threshed out.
\q1 Thou fanciest that to be death, which is life,
\q1 Thou sowest thy seed in salt ground.
\q1 Carnal reason deceives us; do thou contradict it,
\q1 For that fool takes what is really death to be life.
\q1 O God, show us all things in this house of deception,—
\q1 Show them all as they really are!"
\q1 It is said in the Hadis that on the last day
\q1 The command, "Arise," will come to every single body.
\q1 The blast of the last trump will be God's command
\q1 To every atom to lift its head from the earth.
\q1 The souls, also, of each will return to their bodies,
\q1 Even as sense returns to bodies awaking from sleep.
\q1 On that morn each soul will recognise its own body,
\q1 And return to its own ruin like hidden treasure.
\q1 It will recognise its own body and enter it.
\q1 The soul of the goldsmith will not enter the tailor;
\q1 The soul of the wise will enter the body of the wise,
\q1 The soul of the unjust the body of the unjust.
\q1 In like manner as the souls will fly into their clay,
\q1 So will the books fly into their right hands and left.
\q1 God will place in their hands their books of greed and liberality,
\q1 Of sin and piety, and whatever they have practised.
\q1 When they shall awake from sleep on that morning,
\q1 All the evil and good they have done will recur to them.
\q1 Every thought which has dwelt in them during life
\q1 Will appear as a form visible to all,
\q1 Like the thought of an architect realised in a house,
\q1 Or the perfect plant issuing from the seed in the ground.
\q1 From onion and saffron and poppy
\q1 The hand of spring will unfold the secret of winter.
\q1 This one will be verdant and flourish, saying, "We are the pious;"
\q1 That other will hang his head like the violet,—
\q1 With tears starting from his eyes through deadly fear;
\q1 Yea, tens of founts of tears through terrible dread;
\q1 With eyes wide opened in deadly apprehension
\q1 Lest his book may be placed in his left hand.
\q1 Then will the evildoer be sent to the fiery prison,
\q1 For thorn can in no wise escape the flame.
\q1 When his guardian angels behind and before,
\q1 Who before were unseen, shall appear like patrols,
\q1 They will hurry him off, pricking him with their spears,
\q1 And saying, "O dog, begone to thy kennel!"
\q1 Then the prisoner will cry, "O Lord, I am a hundred,
\q1 Yea, a hundred times as wicked as Thou sayest.
\q1 But in mercy Thou veilest my sins,
\q1 Otherwise my vileness were known to Thy all-seeing eye.
\q1 But, independently of my own works and warfare,—
\q1 Independently of my faith or unfaith, good or evil,—
\q1 Independently of my poor devotion to Thee,
\q1 And of my thoughts and the thoughts of hundreds like me,—
\q1 I fix my hopes on Thy mercy alone.
\q1 Whether Thou adjudge me upright or rebellious,
\q1 I sue for free pardon from Thy unbought justice.
\q1 O Lord, who art gracious without thought of consequence,
\q1 I set my face towards that free grace of Thine;
\q1 I have no regard to my own acts.
\q1 I set my face towards this hope,
\q1 Seeing that Thou gavest me my being first of all;
\q1 Thou gayest me the garment of being unasked,
\q1 Wherefore I firmly trust in Thy free grace.
\q1 When he thus enumerates his sins and faults,
\q1 God at last will grant him pardon as a free gift,
\q1 Saying, "O angels, bring him back to me,
\q1 Since the eyes of his heart were set on hope,
\q1 Without care for consequences I set him free,
\q1 And draw the pen through the record of his sins!"
\v 12 \vp 8.1\vp* A loved one said to her lover to try him,
\q1 Early one morning, "O such an one, son of such an one,
\q1 I marvel whether you hold me more dear,
\q1 Or yourself; tell me truly, O ardent suitor!"
\q1 He answered, "I am so entirely absorbed in you,
\q1 That I am full of you from head to foot.
\q1 Of my own existence nothing but the name remains,
\q1 In my being is nothing besides you, O Object of desire!
\q1 Therefore am I thus lost in you,
\q1 Just as vinegar is absorbed in honey;
\q1 Or as a stone, which is changed into a pure ruby,
\q1 Is filled with the bright light of the sun.
\q1 In that stone its own properties abide not
\q1 It is filled with the sun's properties altogether;
\q1 So that, if afterwards it holds itself dear,
\q1 'Tis the same as holding the sun dear, O beloved!
\q1 And if it hold the sun dear in its heart,
\q1 'Tis clearly the same as holding itself dear.
\q1 Whether that pure ruby hold itself dear,
\q1 Or hold the sun dear,
\q1 There is no difference between the two preferences;
\q1 On either hand is naught but the light of dawn.
\q1 But till that stone becomes a ruby it hates itself
\q1 For till it becomes one 'I,' it is two separate 'I's,'
\q1 For 'tis then darkened and purblind,
\q1 And darkness is the essential enemy of light.
\q1 If it then hold itself dear, it is an infidel;
\q1 Because that self is an opponent of the mighty Sun.
\q1 Wherefore 'tis unlawful for the stone then to say 'I,
\q1 Because it is entirely in darkness and nothingness."
\q1 Pharaoh said, "I am the Truth," and was laid low.
\q1 Mansur Hallaj said, "I am the Truth," and escaped free.'
\q1 Pharaoh's "I" was followed by the curse of God;
\q1 Mansur's "I" was followed by the mercy of God, O beloved!
\q1 Because Pharaoh was a stone, Mansur a ruby;
\q1 Pharaoh an enemy of light, Mansur a friend.
\q1 O prattler, Mansur's "I am He" was a deep mystic saying,
\q1 Expressing union with the light, not mere incarnation.
\v 13 \vp 9.1\vp* On the resurrection day all secrets will be disclosed;
\q1 Yea, every guilty one will be convicted by himself.
\q1 Hand and foot will bear testimony openly
\q1 Before the Almighty concerning their owner's sins.
\q1 Hand will say, "I stole such and such things;"
\q1 Lip will say, "I asked for such and such things."
\q1 Foot will say, "I went after my own desires;"
\q1 Arm will say, "I embraced the harlot."
\q1 Eye will say, "I looked after forbidden things;"
\q1 Ear will say, "I listened to evil talk."
\q1 Thus the man will be shown to be a liar from head to foot,
\q1 Since his own members will prove him to be a liar.
\v 14 \vp 10.1\vp* A man asked a camel, saying, "Ho! whence comest thou,
\q1 Thou beast of auspicious footstep?"
\q1 He replied, " From the hot bath of thy street."
\q1 The man said, " That is proved false by thy dirty legs!"
\q1 So, when stubborn Pharaoh saw Moses' staff a serpent,
\q1 And begged for a delay (to fetch magicians) and relented,
\q1 Wise men said, "He ought to have become harsher,
\q1 If He really be, as He says, the Lord Supreme.
\q1 What could miracles such as these of serpents,
\q1 Or even dragons, matter to the majesty of His divinity?
\q1 If He be really Lord Supreme, seated on His throne,
\q1 What need has He to wheedle a worm like Moses?"
\q1 O babbler, while thy soul is drunk with mere date wine,
\q1 Thy spirit hath not tasted the genuine grapes.
\q1 For the token of thy having seen that divine light
\q1 Is this, to withdraw thyself from the house of pride.
\q1 When a fowl flies to the salt water,
\q1 It has never beheld the blessing of sweet water;
\q1 But its faith is mere imitation of other fowl,
\q1 And its soul has never seen the face of real faith.
\q1 Wherefore the blind imitator encounters great perils,—
\q1 Perils of the road, of robbers, of cursed Satans.
\q1 But when he has seen the light of God, he is safe
\q1 From the agitation of doubt, and is firm in the faith.
\q1 Till the foam has landed on the shore and dry land,
\q1 Which is its home, it is ever tossed to and fro.
\q1 'Tis at home on the land, but a stranger on the water.
\q1 While it remains a stranger, it must be tossed about.
\q1 When its eyes are opened, and it sees the vision of land,
\q1 Satan has no longer any domination over it.
\q1 Although the ass repeated verities to the fox,
\q1 He spoke them idly and in the way of cant.
\q1 He praised the water, but was not eager to drink;
\q1 He rent his garments and his hair, but was no real lover.
\q1 The excuse of a hypocrite is rejected, not approved,
\q1 Because it comes only from the lips, not from the heart.
\q1 He has the scent of the apple, but not a piece of it,
\q1 And the scent only for the purpose of misleading others.
\q1 Thus a woman's onset in the midst of a battle array,—
\q1 She keeps in line, and forms part of the battle array,
\q1 Yet, though she looks a very lion as she stands in line,
\q1 Her hand begins to tremble as soon as she takes a sword.
\q1 Woe to him whose reason is like a woman
\q1 While his lust is like a resolute man!
\q1 Of a certainty his reason will be worsted in the fight,
\q1 And his imitation of a man will only lead him to ruin.
\q1 Happy is he whose reason is masculine,
\q1 And his ugly lust feminine and under subjection!
\q1 Though the mere imitator quotes a hundred proofs,
\q1 They are all based on opinion, not on conviction.
\q1 He is only scented with musk, he is not himself musk;
\q1 He smells of musk, but is really naught but dung.
\q1 For his dung to become musk, O disciple,
\q1 He must graze year after year in the divine pasture.
\q1 For he who, like the musk-deer, feeds on saffron of Khoten
\q1 Must not eat grass and oats like asses.
\q1 That man of cant has at his tongue's end
\q1 A hundred proofs and precepts, but there is no life in him.
\q1 When the preacher has himself no light or life,
\q1 How can his words yield leaves and fruit?
\q1 He impudently preaches to others to walk aright,
\q1 While himself he is unsteady as a reed shaken by wind.
\q1 Thus, though his preaching is very eloquent,
\q1 It hides within it unsteadiness in the faith.
\v 15 \vp 10.2\vp* The fox said, "In my pure wine there are no dregs;
\q1 These vain suspicions are not becoming.
\q1 All this is only baseless suspicion, O simple one,
\q1 Else you would know I am not plotting against you.
\q1 You repudiate me on account of your own bad fancies;
\q1 Why do you thus suspect your true friends?
\q1 Think well of the 'Brothers of purity,'
\q1 Even though they show harshness toward you;
\q1 For when evil suspicion takes hold of you,
\q1 It severs you from hundreds of friends.
\q1 If a tender friend treats you roughly to try you,
\q1 'Tis contrary to reason to distrust him.
\q1 Though I bear a bad name, my nature is not malevolent;
\q1 What you saw was not dangerous, it was only a talisman.
\q1 But even if there were danger in that object of suspicion,
\q1 Friends always pardon an offence."
\q1 This world of illusions, fancies, desires, and fears,
\q1 Is a mighty obstacle in the traveler's path.
\q1 Thus, when these forms of delusive imaginations
\q1 Misled Abraham, who was a very mountain of wisdom,
\q1 He said of the star, " This is my Lord,"
\q1 Having fallen into the midst of the world of illusion.
\q1 He thus interpreted the meaning of sun and stars,—
\q1 Yea, he, that great man who threaded jewels of interpretation,
\q1 Seeing then that this world of eye-fascinating illusion
\q1 Seduced from the right path such a mountain as Abraham,
\q1 So that he said of the star, "This is my Lord,—"
\q1 What will not its illusions effect on a stupid ass?
\q1 Human reason is drowned, like the high mountains,
\q1 In the flood of illusion and vain imaginations.
\q1 The very mountains are overwhelmed by this flood,
\q1 Where is safety to be found save in Noah's ark?
\q1 By illusions that plunder the road of faith
\q1 The faithful have been split into seventy-two sects.
\q1 But the man of conviction escapes illusion;
\q1 He does not mistake his eyelash for the new moon.
\q1 He who is divorced from 'Omar's light
\q1 Is deceived by his own crooked eyelash.
\q1 Thousands of ships, in all their majesty and pomp,
\q1 Have gone to pieces in this sea of illusion.
\v 16 \vp 10.3\vp* The monk said, "I am searching everywhere for a man
\q1 Who lives by the life of the breath of God."
\q1 The other said, "Here are men; the bazaar is full;
\q1 These are surely men, O enlightened sage!"
\q1 The monk said, "I seek a man who walks straight
\q1 As well in the road of anger as in that of lust.
\q1 Where is one who shows himself a man in anger and lust?
\q1 In search of such a one I run from street to street.
\q1 If there be one who is a true man in these two states,
\q1 I will yield up my life for him this day!"
\q1 The other, who was a fatalist, said, "What you seek is rare.
\q1 But you are ignorant of the force of the divine decree;
\q1 You see the branches, but ignore the root.
\q1 We men are but branches, God's eternal decree the root.
\q1 That decree turns from its course the revolving sky,
\q1 And makes foolish hundreds of planets like Mercury.
\q1 It reduces to helplessness the world of devices;
\q1 It turns steel and stone to water.
\q1 O you who attribute stability to these steps on the road,
\q1 You are one of the raw ones; yea, raw, raw!
\q1 When you have seen the millstone turning round,
\q1 Then, prithee, go and see the stream that turns it.
\q1 When you have seen the dust rising up into the air,
\q1 Go and mark the air in the midst of the dust.
\q1 You see the kettles of thought boiling over,
\q1 Look with intelligence at the fire beneath them.
\q1 God said to Job, 'Out of my clemency
\q1 I have given a grain of patience to every hair of thine.'
\q1 Look not, then, so much at your own patience;
\q1 After seeing patience, look to the Giver of patience.
\q1 How long will you confine your view to the waterwheel?
\q1 Lift up your head and view also the water."
\v 17 \vp 11.1\vp* Love is a perfect muzzle of evil suggestions;
\q1 Without love who ever succeeded in stopping them?
\q1 Be a lover, and seek that fair Beauty,
\q1 Hunt for that Waterfowl in every stream!
\q1 How can you get water from that which cuts it off?
\q1 How gain understanding from what destroys understanding?
\q1 Apart from principles of reason are other principles
\q1 Of light and great price to be gained by love of God.
\q1 Besides this reason of yours God has other reasons
\q1 Which will procure for you heavenly nourishment.
\q1 By your carnal reason you may procure earthly food,
\q1 By God-given reason you may mount the heavens.
\q1 When, to win enduring love of God, you sacrifice reason,
\q1 God gives you "a tenfold recompense;" yea, seven hundred fold.
\q1 When those Egyptian women sacrificed their reason,
\q1 They penetrated the mansion of Joseph's love;
\q1 The Cup-bearer of life bore away their reason,
\q1 They were filled with wisdom of the world without end.
\q1 Joseph's beauty was only an offshoot of God's beauty;
\q1 Be lost, then, in God's beauty more than those women.
\q1 Love of God cuts short reasonings, O beloved,
\q1 For it is a present refuge from perplexities.
\q1 Through love bewilderment befalls the power of speech,
\q1 It no longer dares to utter what passes;
\q1 For if it sets forth an answer, it fears greatly
\q1 That its secret treasure may escape its lips.
\q1 Therefore it closes lips from saying good or bad,
\q1 So that its treasure may not escape it.
\q1 In like manner the Prophet's companions tell us,
\q1 "When the Prophet used to tell us deep sayings,
\q1 That chosen one, while scattering pearls of speech,
\q1 Would bid us preserve perfect quiet and silence."
\q1 So, when the mighty phoenix hovers over your head,
\q1 Causing your soul to tremble at the motion of its wings,
\q1 You venture not to stir from your place,
\q1 Lest that bird of good fortune should take wing.
\q1 You hold your breath and repress your coughs,
\q1 So as not to scare that phoenix into flying away.
\q1 And if one say a word to you, whether good or bad,
\q1 You place finger on lip, as much as to say, "Be silent."
\q1 That phoenix is bewilderment, it makes you silent;
\q1 The kettle is silent, though it is boiling all the while.
\v 18 \vp 12.1\vp* His brain is dried up; and as for his reason,
\q1 It is now less than that of a child.
\q1 Age and abstinence have added infirmity to infirmity,
\q1 And his abstinence has yielded him no rejoicing.
\q1 He has endured toils, but gained no reward from his Friend;
\q1 He has done the work, but has not been paid.
\q1 Either his work has lacked value,
\q1 Or the time of recompense is not decreed as yet.
\q1 Either his works are as the works of the Jews,
\q1 Or his reward is held back till the appointed time.
\q1 This grief and sorrow are enough for him,
\q1 That in this valley of pain he is utterly friendless.
\q1 With sad eyes he sits in his corner,
\q1 With frowning face and downcast looks.
\q1 There is no oculist who cares to open his eyes,
\q1 Nor has he reason enough to discover the eye-salve.
\q1 He strives earnestly with firm resolve and in hope,
\q1 His work is done on the chance of being right.
\q1 The vision of "The Friend" is far from his course,
\q1 For he loses the kernel in his love for the shell.
\v 19 \vp 8.2\vp* O Ayaz, what is this love of yours for your old shoes,
\q1 Which resembles the love of a lover for his mistress?
\q1 You have made these old shoes your object of devotion,
\q1 Just as Majnun made an idol of his Laila!
\q1 You have bound the affection of your soul to them,
\q1 And hung them up in your secret chamber.
\q1 How long will you say orisons to this old pair of shoes?
\q1 And breathe your oft-told secrets into inanimate ears?
\q1 Like the Arab lover to the house of his dead mistress,
\q1 You address to them long invocations of love.
\q1 Of what great Asaf were your shoes the house?
\q1 Is your old garment, think you, the coat of Yusuf?
\q1 Like a Christian who confesses to a priest
\q1 His past year's sins of fornication, fraud, and deceit;
\q1 In order that the priest may absolve him of those sins;
\q1 He thinks the priest's absolution the same as God's!
\q1 That priest is unable to condemn or to absolve;
\q1 But faith and love are a mighty enchantment!
\v 20 \vp 8.3\vp* The wine is from that world, the vessels from this;
\q1 The vessels are seen, but the wine is hidden!
\q1 Hidden indeed from the sight of the carnal,
\q1 But open and manifest to the spiritual!
\q1 O God, our eyes are blinded!
\q1 O pardon us, our sins are a heavy burden!
\q1 Thou art hidden from us, though the heavens are filled
\q1 With Thy light, which is brighter than sun and moon!
\q1 Thou art hidden, yet revealest our hidden secrets!
\q1 Thou art the source that causes our rivers to flow.
\q1 Thou art hidden in Thy essence, but seen by Thy bounties.
\q1 Thou art like the water, and we like the millstone.
\q1 Thou art like the wind, and we like the dust;
\q1 The wind is unseen, but the dust is seen by all.
\q1 Thou art the spring, and we the sweet green garden;
\q1 Spring is not seen, though its gifts are seen.
\q1 Thou art as the soul, we as hand and foot;
\q1 Soul instructs hand and foot to hold and take.
\q1 Thou art as reason, we like the tongue;
\q1 'Tis reason that teaches the tongue to speak.
\q1 Thou art as joy, and we are laughing;
\q1 The laughter is the consequence of the joy.
\q1 Our every motion every moment testifies,
\q1 For it proves the presence of the Everlasting God.
\q1 So the revolution of the millstone, so violent,
\q1 Testifies to the existence of a stream of water.
\q1 O Thou who art above our conceptions and descriptions,
\q1 Dust be on our heads, and upon our similitudes of Thee!
\q1 Yet Thy slaves never cease devising images of Thee;
\q1 They cry to Thee always, "My life is Thy footstool!"
\q1 Like that shepherd who cried," O Lord!
\q1 Come nigh to thy faithful shepherd,
\q1 That he may cleanse thy garment of vermin,
\q1 And mend thy shoes, and kiss the hem of thy robe!"
\q1 No one equaled that shepherd in love and devotion,
\q1 Though his manner of expressing it was most faulty.
\q1 His love pitched its tent on the heavens,
\q1 He himself was as the dog at the tent-door.
\q1 When the sea of love to God boiled up,
\q1 It touched his heart, but it touches your ears only.
\v 21 \vp 8.4\vp* I said, "O foul and faithless passion,
\q1 Whence have you derived this inclination to war?
\q1 Tell me truly, O passion, is this your trickery?
\q1 Or else is it stubbornness shunning obedience to God?
\q1 If you say not truly I will attack you,
\q1 And will afflict you more severely with discipline."
\q1 Passion then heaved a cry from its breast,
\q1 And without mouth vented the following complaints:
\q1 "In this cell you slay me every day;
\q1 You slay my life like the life of a Gueber.
\q1 Not a soul is aware of my condition;
\q1 You drag me along without food or sleep.
\q1 In the fight with one wound I shall quit the body,
\q1 And the people will admire my valour and self-devotion."
\q1 I said, "O bad passion, you live as an infidel,
\q1 And as an infidel you will die; shame be upon you!
\q1 In both worlds you are naught but a hypocrite;
\q1 In the two worlds only an unprofitable servant.
\q1 I have vowed to God never to quit this cell
\q1 While life remains in this body;
\q1 Because whatever the body does in this privacy
\q1 Is not done to make a fair show before men.
\q1 Its movements and its rest in the privacy of this cell
\q1 Are not intended for the sight of any besides God.
\q1 This is the 'greater warfare,' that the 'lesser;'
\q1 Both these warfares have their Rustams and Haidars.
\q1 They are not to be fought by one whose reason and sense
\q1 Flee away as soon as a mouse wags its tail.
\q1 Such persons must shun the array of battle,
\q1 And keep aloof from it even as women do."
\v 22 \vp 8.5\vp* A person put this question to a philosopher,
\q1 "O sage, what is true and what is false?"
\q1 The sage touched his ear and said, "This is false,
\q1 But the eye is true and its report is certain."
\q1 The ear is false in relation to the eye,
\q1 And most assertions are related to the ear.
\q1 If a bat turn away its eyes from the sun,
\q1 Still it is not veiled from some idea of the sun;
\q1 Its very dread of the sun frames an idea of the sun,
\q1 And that idea scares it away to the darkness.
\q1 That idea of light terrifies it,
\q1 And makes it cling to the murky night.
\q1 Just so 'tis your idea of your terrible foe
\q1 Which makes you cling to your friends and allies.
\q1 O Moses, thy revelations shed glory on the mount,
\q1 But that frightened one endured not thy realities.
\q1 Be not too proud, but know that you must first endure
\q1 The idea of the Truth, and thence come to the reality.
\q1 No one is frightened by the mere idea of fighting,
\q1 For "no courage is needed before fighting begins."
\q1 In the mere idea of fighting a coward can imagine
\q1 Himself as attacking and retreating like a Rustam.
\q1 The pictures of Rustam on the wall of a bath
\q1 Are similar to a coward's ideas of fighting.
\q1 But when these ideas are tested by actual sight,
\q1 What of the coward then? His bravery is gone!
\q1 Strive, then, from mere hearing to press on to seeing;
\q1 What ear has told you falsely eye will tell truly.
\q1 Then ear too will acquire the properties of an eye;
\q1 Your ears, now worthless as wool, will become gems;
\q1 Yea, your whole body will become a mirror,
\q1 It will be as an eye or a bright gem in your bosom.
\q1 First the hearing of the ear enables you to form ideas,
\q1 Then these ideas guide you to the Beloved.
\q1 Strive, then, to increase the number of these ideas,
\q1 That they may guide you, like Majnun, to the Beloved.
\v 23 \vp 8.6\vp* To return; that prince played the fool,
\q1 And took delight in the society of the damsel.
\q1 O prince, suppose your dominion extend from east to west,
\q1 Yet, as it endures not, esteem it transitory as lightning;
\q1 Yea, O sleeping heart, know the kingdom that endures not
\q1 Forever and ever is only a mere dream.
\q1 I marvel how long you will indulge in vain illusion,
\q1 Which has seized you by the throat like a headsman.
\q1 Know that even in this world there is a place of refuge;
\q1 Hearken not to the unbeliever who denies it.
\q1 His argument is this: he says again and again,
\q1 "If there were aught beyond this life we should see it."
\q1 But if the child sees not the state of reason,
\q1 Does the man of reason therefore forsake reason?
\q1 And if the man of reason sees not the state of love,
\q1 Is the blessed moon of love thereby eclipsed?
\q1 The beauty of Joseph was not visible to his brethren;
\q1 Was it therefore hidden from the eyes of Jacob?
\q1 The eyes of Moses regarded his staff as a stick,
\q1 But the divine eye saw it to be a deadly serpent.
\q1 The eye of the head was at issue with the divine eye,
\q1 But the latter prevailed and gave convincing proof.
\q1 To the eyes of Moses his hand looked a mere hand,
\q1 But to the divine eye it appeared a flashing light.
\q1 This subject in its entirety is endless,
\q1 But to the unbeliever it is a mere fanciful idea.
\q1 The only realities to him are lust and gluttony;
\q1 Speak not then to him of the mysteries of the Beloved.
\q1 To us believers lust and gluttony are only ideas,
\q1 Therefore we behold alway the beauty of the Beloved.
\q1 To all men whose creed is lust and gluttony
\q1 Applies the text, "To you be your creed, to me mine."
\q1 In the face of negations like these cut short speech,
\q1 "O Ahmad, say little to an old Fire-worshipper!"
\v 24 \vp 8.7\vp* If the prince lacked the animal manliness of asses,
\q1 Yet he possessed the true manliness of the prophets.
\q1 He renounced lust and anger and concupiscence,
\q1 And showed himself a man of the lineage of the prophets.
\q1 Grant that he lacked the virility of asses,
\q1 Yet God esteemed him a lord of lords.
\q1 Let me be dead, so long as God regards me with favour!
\q1 I am better off than the living who are rejected of God;
\q1 The former is the kernel of manliness, the latter only the rind;
\q1 The former is borne to Paradise, the latter to hell.
\q1 The Prophet says, "Paradise is annexed to tribulation,
\q1 But hell-fire follows indulgence in lust."
\q1 O Ayaz, who slayest demons like a male lion,
\q1 Manliness of asses is naught, manliness of mind much.
\q1 What sort of man dost thou think him who sports as a boy,
\q1 But who has no comprehension of these chief matters?
\q1 o thou who hast seen the delight of my commandments,
\q1 And risked thy life to perform them faithfully,
\q1 Hear a tale of the sweetness of my commandments,
\q1 That the meaning of this sweetness may be made plain.
\c 6
\cp 6
\p
\v 1 \vp prologue.1\vp* O LIFE of the heart, Husamu-'d-Din,
\q1 My zeal burnt within me to write this sixth part!
\q1 The Masnavi became a standard through thy influence,
\q1 Thy sword (Husam) has made it an exemplar to the world
\q1 O spiritual one, I now offer it to thee,—
\q1 This sixth part of the entire Masnavi.
\q1 Enlighten the world's six sides with its six parts,
\q1 That it may illuminate him who is not illuminated!
\q1 Love has naught to do with five senses or six sides,
\q1 Its only aim is to be attracted to the Beloved!
\q1 But haply leave may be given me hereafter
\q1 To tell those mysteries so far as they can be told,
\q1 In a discourse more closely approximating to the facts
\q1 Than these faint indications of those abstruse matters.
\q1 Mysteries are not communicable, save to those who know;
\q1 Mystery in the ear of infidels is no mystery.
\q1 Nevertheless, this is a call to you from God;
\q1 It matters not to Him whether ye accept or reject it.
\q1 Noah repeated His call for nine hundred years,
\q1 But his people only increased in rebellion.
\q1 Never did he draw back from admonishing them,
\q1 Never did he retire into the cave of silence.
\q1 He said, "At the barking and howling of the dogs
\q1 No caravan ever turned back in its road.
\q1 Nor does the full moon on a bright night cease shining
\q1 Because of the howling of dogs on earth.
\q1 The moon sheds her light, and the dogs howl;
\q1 Every one acts according to his nature.
\q1 To each one his office is allotted by the divine decree,
\q1 And he acts agreeably to his nature."
\q1 Art thou thirsting for the Ocean of spirituality?
\q1 Disport thyself on this island of the Masnavi!
\q1 Disport thyself, so long as thou seest every moment
\q1 Spiritual verities revealed in this Masnavi.
\q1 When the wind blows the grass off the water,
\q1 The water then shows forth its own purity.
\q1 Behold the bright and fresh sprays of coral,
\q1 And the princely fruits growing in the water of life!
\q1 So, when the Masnavi is purged of letters and words,
\q1 It drops all these, and appears as the sea of Unity.
\q1 Then speaker and hearer and spoken words
\q1 All three give up the ghost in that consummation.
\q1 Bread-giver and bread-eater and bread itself
\q1 Are purified of their forms and turn to dust.
\q1 But their essences in each of these three grades
\q1 Are distinguished, as in those states, so eternally.
\q1 Their form turns to dust, but their essence not;
\q1 If one says it does, tell him it does not.
\q1 In the world of spirits all three await judgment,
\q1 Sometimes wearing their earthly forms, sometimes not.
\v 2 \vp prologue.2\vp* One day a student asked a preacher,
\q1 Saying, "O most orthodox ornament of the pulpit,
\q1 I have a question to ask, O lord of learning;
\q1 Tell me the answer to it in this congregation.
\q1 A bird sat on the top of a wall;
\q1 Which was best, its head or its tail?"
\q1 He replied, "If its face was towards the town,
\q1 And its tail to the villages, then its face was best.
\q1 But if its tail was towards the town, and its face
\q1 Towards the villages, then prefer its tail to its face."
\q1 A bird flies with its wings towards its nest,
\q1 The wings of a man are his aspiration and aim.
\q1 If a lover be befouled with good and evil,
\q1 Yet regard not these; regard rather his aspiration.
\q1 Though a falcon be all white and unmatched in form,
\q1 If he hunts mice he is contemptible and worthless.
\q1 And if an owl fixes his affection on the king,
\q1 He is a falcon in reality; regard not his outward form.
\q1 Adam's clay was kneaded in the limits of a trough,
\q1 Yet was he exalted above heaven and stars.
\q1 "We have honoured Adam" was not addressed to the sky,
\q1 But to Adam himself, full of defects as he was.
\q1 Did one ever propose to earth or heaven to receive
\q1 Beauty, reason, and speech and aspiration?
\q1 Would you ever offer to the heavens
\q1 Beauty of face and acuteness of thought?
\q1 O son, did you ever present your silver body
\q1 As an offering to the damsels pictured on bath walls?
\q1 Nay, you pass by those pictures though fair as Huris,
\q1 And offer yourself sooner to half-blind old women.
\q1 What is there in the old women which the pictures lack,
\q1 Which draws you from the pictures to the old women?
\q1 Say not, for I will say it in plain words,
\q1 'Tis reason, sense, perception, thought, and life.
\q1 In the old woman life is infused,
\q1 While the pictures of the bath have no life.
\q1 If the pictures of the bath should stir with life (soul),
\q1 They would uproot your love to all the old women.
\q1 What is soul? 'Tis acquainted with good and evil,—
\q1 Rejoicing at pleasant things, grieving at ills.
\q1 Since, then, the principle of soul is knowledge,
\q1 He who knows most is most full of soul.
\q1 Knowledge is the effect flowing from soul;
\q1 He who has most of it is most godlike.
\q1 Seeing then, beloved, that knowledge is the mark of soul,
\q1 He who knows most has the strongest soul.
\q1 The world of souls is itself entirely knowledge,
\q1 And he who is void of knowledge is void of soul.
\q1 When knowledge is lacking in a man's nature,
\q1 His soul is like a stone on the plain.
\q1 Primal Soul is the theatre of God's court,
\q1 Soul of souls the exhibition of God Himself.
\q1 All the angels were pure reason and soul,
\q1 Yet when the new soul of Adam came, they were as its body.
\q1 When in joy they crowded round that new soul,
\q1 They bowed before it as body does before soul.
\v 3 \vp prologue.3\vp* O Husamu-'d-Din, I might tell some of thy many virtues,
\q1 Were it not for the fear of the evil eyes.
\q1 From evil eyes and malice-empoisoned breaths
\q1 Already have I suffered fatal wounds.
\q1 Therefore I cannot relate thy ecstatic states,
\q1 Save by hints of the ecstatic states of others.
\q1 This manoeuvre is one of the devices of the heart,
\q1 Whereby the heart's feet wend their way to the truth.
\q1 Many hearts and souls would become lovers of God
\q1 Did not evil eyes or evil ears hold them back.
\q1 Of these Abu Talib, the Prophet's uncle, was one;
\q1 The malice of the Arabs scared him from the faith.
\q1 He said, "What will the Arabs say of me?
\q1 That my own nephew has perverted me from my religion!"
\q1 Muhammad said, "O uncle, confess the faith to me,
\q1 That I may strive with God for thee!"
\q1 He said, "Nay; it will be published by them that hear;
\q1 'A secret known to more than two is known to every one.'
\q1 As I live in the midst of these Arabs,
\q1 It will cause me to lose caste with them.
\q1 Yet, had the mighty grace of God led the way,
\q1 How could this fear have vied with God's attraction?
\q1 O Granter of aid, lend us aid
\q1 In this dilemma of the feeble will.
\v 4 \vp prologue.4\vp* This flux and reflux of resolves came to me from Thee,
\q1 Else these tides of will had rested still, O God!
\q1 By the same fiat whereby Thou madest me thus irresolute,
\q1 Of Thy mercy deliver me from this irresolution!
\q1 Thou triest me; O give me aid!
\q1 For men are as women through this trial.
\q1 How long, O Lord, is this trial to last?
\q1 Give me one ruling principle, not ten principles!
\q1 The whole world flees away from its own will and being
\q1 Towards self-abandonment and intoxication.
\q1 In order to escape a while from self-consciousness,
\q1 Men incur the reproach of wine and strong drink;
\q1 For all know well this existence is a snare,
\q1 This thought and memory and will only a hell.
\q1 Therefore they flee from self to being beside themselves,
\q1 Call it intoxication or call it preoccupation, O guided one.
\q1 Ere it is annihilated, no single soul
\q1 Finds admittance to the divine hall of audience.
\q1 What is "ascension" to heaven? Annihilation of self;
\q1 Self-abandonment is the creed and religion of lovers.
\v 5 \vp 2.1\vp* When he had eaten the grain he was caught in the trap,
\q1 And began to recite the chapters "Yasin" and "An'am."
\q1 Then he began to wail and cry loudly,
\q1 So that the very fowler and his trap shook with grief.
\q1 He said, "My back is broken by the conflict of my thoughts;
\q1 O Beloved One, come and stroke my head in mercy!
\q1 The palm of Thy hand on my head gives me rest,
\q1 Thy hand is a sign of Thy bounteous providence.
\q1 Remove not Thy shadow from my head,
\q1 I am afflicted, afflicted, afflicted!
\q1 Sleep has deserted my eyes
\q1 Through my longing for Thee, O Envy of cypresses!
\q1 Though I be unworthy of Thy favour, how were it
\q1 If thou shouldst regard the griefs of unworthy me?
\q1 What claim of right can a non-existent thing make
\q1 To have the doors of Thy bounty opened to it?
\q1 Yet Thy bounty had regard to my senseless dust
\q1 And endued it with the ten jewels of the senses;—
\q1 Five external senses and five internal senses,
\q1 Whereby inanimate seed became a living man.
\q1 O Light on high! what is repentance without Thy grace
\q1 But a mere mockery of the beard of repentance;
\q1 Thou rootest up the hairs of such repentance,
\q1 Repentance is the shadow, Thou the shining Moon.
\q1 Alas! Thou hast ruined my house and home;
\q1 How can I cease wailing while Thou oppressest me?
\q1 How can I flee away when there is no living away?
\q1 Without Thy sustaining lordship there is no slave.
\q1 O take my life, Thou that art the source of life!
\q1 For apart from Thee I am wearied of my life.
\q1 I am a lover well versed in lovers' madness,
\q1 I am weary of learning and sense.
\q1 Since my bashfulness is destroyed, I will publish secrets;
\q1 How long must I bear this trepidation and anxiety?
\q1 Formerly I was covered by modesty as by a veil,
\q1 Now I will leap from it under Thy coverlet!
\q1 O comrades, our Beloved has closed up all paths;
\q1 We are as lame deer, and He as a raging lion.
\q1 Say what remedy is there but resignation
\q1 When one is fallen into the hands of the raging lion?"
\v 6 \vp 3.1\vp* "I know not if thou art a moon or an idol,
\q1 I know not what thou requirest of me.
\q1 I know not what service to pay thee,
\q1 Whether to keep silence or to speak.
\q1 Thou art not apart from me, yet, strange to say,
\q1 I know not where I am, or where thou art.
\q1 I know not wherefore thou art dragging me,
\q1 Now embracing me, and now wounding me!"
\v 7 \vp 3.2\vp* That wine of God is gained from that minstrel,
\q1 This bodily wine from this minstrel.
\q1 Both of these have one and the same name in speech,
\q1 But the difference between their worth is great.
\q1 Men's bodies are like pitchers with closed mouths;
\q1 Beware, till you see what is inside them.
\q1 The pitcher of this body holds the water of life,
\q1 Whilst that one holds deadly poison.
\q1 If you look at the contents you are wise;
\q1 If you look only at the vessel you are misguided.
\q1 Know words resemble these bodies,
\q1 And the meaning resembles the soul.
\q1 The body's eyes are ever intent on bodies,
\q1 The soul's eyes on the reasonable soul;
\q1 Wherefore, in the figures of the words of the Masnavi,
\q1 The form misleads, but the inner meaning guides.
\q1 In the Koran it is declared that its parables
\q1 "Mislead some and guide some."
\q1 O God! when a spiritual man talks of wine,
\q1 How can a fellow spiritual man mistake his meaning?
\q1 Thus that minstrel began his intoxicating song,
\q1 "O give me Thy cup, Thou whom I see not!
\q1 Thou art my face; what wonder if I see it not?
\q1 Extreme nearness acts as an obscuring veil.
\q1 Thou art my reason; what wonder if I see Thee not
\q1 Through the multitude of intervening obstacles?
\q1 Thou art 'nearer to me than my neck vein,'
\q1 How can I call to Thee, 'Ho,' as if thou wert far off?
\q1 Nay, but I will mislead some by calling in the desert,
\q1 To hide my Beloved from those of whom I am jealous!"
\v 8 \vp 3.3\vp* You have said your say; now hear my answer,
\q1 So as not to remain in astonishment and bewilderment.
\q1 Though to you this time seems midnight,
\q1 To me the dawn of joyful morn seems nigh.
\q1 To the vulgar all parts of the world seem dead,
\q1 But to God they are instinct with sense and love.
\q1 And as to your saying that "this house is empty,
\q1 Why then should I beat a drum before it?"
\q1 Know that the people of God expend money,
\q1 And build many mosques and holy places,
\q1 And spend health and wealth in distant pilgrimages,
\q1 In ecstatic delight, like intoxicated lovers;
\q1 And none of them ever say, "The Ka'ba is empty;"
\q1 How can one who knows the truth say that?
\q1 These people are ranged in battle array,
\q1 And risk their lives to gain God's favour;
\q1 One plunged in calamities like Job himself,
\q1 Another exhibiting patience like Jacob.
\q1 Thousands of them are thirsty and afflicted,
\q1 Striving in earnest desire to do God's will.
\q1 I likewise, in order to please the merciful God,
\q1 Beat my drum at every door in hope of dawn.
\q1 Seek ye a purchaser who will pay you gold;
\q1 Where will you find one more liberal than God?
\q1 He buys the worthless rubbish which is your wealth,
\q1 He pays you the light that illumines your heart.
\q1 He accepts these frozen and lifeless bodies of yours,
\q1 And gives you a kingdom beyond what you dream of.
\q1 He takes a few drops of your tears,
\q1 And gives you the divine fount sweeter than sugar.
\q1 He takes your sighs fraught with grief and sadness,
\q1 And for each sigh gives rank in heaven as interest.
\q1 In return for the sigh-wind that raised tear-clouds,
\q1 God gave Abraham the title of "Father of the faithful."
\q1 Come! in this incomparable and crowded mart
\q1 Sell your old goods and take a kingdom in payment!
\v 9 \vp 4.1\vp* Since you have told the story of Hilal (the new moon)
\q1 Now set forth the story of Badr (the full moon).
\q1 That new moon and that full moon are now united,
\q1 Removed from duality and defect and shortcomings.
\q1 That Hilal is now exalted above inward defect;
\q1 His outward defects served as degrees of ascension.
\q1 Night after night that mentor taught him grades of ascent
\q1 And through his patient waiting gave him happiness.
\q1 The mentor says, "O raw hastener, through patient waiting,
\q1 You must climb to the summit step by step.
\q1 Boil your pot by degrees and in a masterly way;
\q1 Food boiled in mad haste is spoiled.
\q1 Doubtless God could have created the universe
\q1 By the fiat 'Be!' in one moment of time;
\q1 Why, then, did He protract His work over six days,
\q1 Each of which equalled a thousand years, O disciple?
\q1 Why does the formation of an infant take nine months?
\q1 Because God's method is to work by slow degrees,
\q1 Why did the formation of Adam take forty days?
\q1 Because his clay was kneaded by slow degrees.
\q1 Not hurrying on like you, O raw one,
\q1 Who claim to be a Shaikh whilst yet only a child!
\q1 You run up like a gourd higher than all plants,
\q1 But where is your power of resistance or combat?
\q1 You have leant on trees or on walls,
\q1 And so mounted up like a gourd, O little dog rose;
\q1 Even though your prop may be a lofty cypress,
\q1 At last you are seen to be dry and hollow.
\q1 O gourd, your bright green hue soon turns yellow,
\q1 For it is not a natural but an artificial colour."
\v 10 \vp 5.1\vp* Well said that Leader of mankind,
\q1 That whosoever passes away from the world
\q1 Does not grieve and lament over his death,
\q1 But grieves ever over lost opportunities.
\q1 He says, "Why did I not keep death always in view,
\q1 Which is the treasury of wealth and sustenance?
\q1 Why did I blindly all my life set my affections
\q1 On vain shadows which perish at death?
\q1 My regret is not that I have died,
\q1 But that I rested on these vain shadows in life.
\q1 I saw not that my body was a mere shadow or foam,
\q1 Which foam rises out of and lives on the Ocean (God).
\q1 When the Ocean casts its foam-drops to land,
\q1 Go to the graveyard and behold them,
\q1 And ask them, "Where is your motion and activity?
\q1 The Ocean has cast you into a mortal sickness!"
\q1 They will answer by their condition, if not with words,
\q1 "Put this question to the Ocean, not to us!"
\q1 How can mere foam move unless moved by the waves?
\q1 How can dust mount on high unless raised by wind?
\q1 When you see the dust-cloud, see the wind too!
\q1 When you see the foam, see the ocean that heaves it!
\q1 Ah! look till you see your own real final cause,
\q1 The rest of you is only fat and flesh, warp and woof.
\q1 Your fat kindles no light or flame in a lamp;
\q1 Your kneaded flesh is not good for roasting.
\q1 Burn up, then, all this body of yours with discernment;
\q1 Rise to sight, to sight, to sight!
\v 11 \vp 5.2\vp* The Sufi said, "The Great Helper is able
\q1 To procure for us profit without loss.
\q1 He who casts into the fire roses and trees
\q1 Can accomplish good without injury to any.
\q1 He who extracts the rose from the thorn
\q1 Can also turn this winter into spring.
\q1 He who exalts the heads of the cypresses
\q1 Is able also out of sadness to bring joy.
\q1 He by whose fiat all non-existent things exist,
\q1 What harm to Him were it if He made them eternal?
\q1 He who gave to the body a soul and made it live,
\q1 What loss to Him were it if He never caused it to die?
\q1 How would it be if That Liberal One were to give
\q1 Their hearts' desire to his slaves without toil,
\q1 And keep away from these feeble ones
\q1 The ambushed snares of lust and temptations of Iblis?"
\q1 The Qazi said, "If there were no bitter things,
\q1 And no opposition of fair and foul, stone and pearl,
\q1 And no lust or Satan or concupiscence,
\q1 And no wounds or war or fraud,
\q1 Pray, O destroyer of virtue, by what name and title
\q1 Could the King of kings address His slaves?
\q1 How could He say, 'O temperate or O meek one!'
\q1 Or, 'O courageous one, or O wise one?'
\q1 How could there be temperate, gentle, or liberal men
\q1 If there were no cursed Satan to tempt them astray?
\q1 Rustam and Hamza would be all the same as cowards;
\q1 Wisdom and knowledge would be useless and vain.
\q1 Wisdom and knowledge serve to guide the wanderers;
\q1 Were there but one road wisdom would be needless.
\q1 To pamper the house of your body fleeting as water,
\q1 Do you think it right to ruin both worlds?
\q1 I know you are pure of guile and ripe,
\q1 And ask this only to edify the ignorant.
\q1 The ills of fortune and all troubles soever
\q1 Are better than exile from God and neglect of Him;
\q1 For the former pass away, but the latter abide;
\q1 He is happy who carries a wary heart before God."
\v 12 \vp 5.3\vp* Inquire now, I pray, of each one of your members;
\q1 These dumb members have a thousand tongues.
\q1 Inquire the detail of the bounties of the All-sustainer,
\q1 Which are recorded in the volume of the universe.
\q1 Day and night you are eagerly asking for news,
\q1 Whilst every member of your body is telling you news.
\q1 Since each member of your body issued from Not-being,
\q1 How much pleasure has it seen, and how much pain?
\q1 For no member grows and flourishes without pleasure,
\q1 And each member is weakened by every pain.
\q1 The member endures, but that pleasure is forgotten,
\q1 Yet not all forgotten, but hidden from the senses.
\q1 Like summer wherein cotton is produced,—
\q1 The cotton remains, but the summer is forgotten.
\q1 Or like ice which is formed in great frost,—
\q1 The frost departs, but the ice is still before us.
\q1 The ice is mindful of that extreme cold,
\q1 And even in winter that crop is mindful of the summer.
\q1 In like manner, O son, every member of your body
\q1 Tells you tales of God's bounties to your body.
\q1 Even as a woman who has borne twenty children,—
\q1 Each child tells a tale of pleasure felt by her.
\q1 She became not pregnant save after sexual pleasure,
\q1 Can a garden bloom without the spring?
\q1 Pregnant women and their teeming wombs
\q1 Tell tales of love frolics in the spring.
\q1 So every tree which nurtures its fruits
\q1 Has been, like Mary, impregnated by the Unseen King.
\q1 Though fire's heat be hidden in the midst of water,
\q1 Yet a thousand boiling bubbles prove it present.
\q1 Though the heat of the fire be working unseen,
\q1 Yet its bubbles signify its presence plainly.
\q1 In like manner, the members of those enjoying "union"
\q1 Become big with child, viz., with forms of "states" and "words."
\q1 Gazing on the beauty of these forms they stand agape,
\q1 And the forms of the world vanish from their sight.
\q1 These spiritual progenies are not born of the elements,
\q1 And are perforce invisible to the sensual eye.
\q1 These progenies are born of divine apparitions,
\q1 And are therefore bidden by veils without colour.
\q1 I said "born," but in reality they are not born;
\q1 I used this expression only by way of indication.
\q1 But keep silence till the King bids you speak,
\q1 Offer not your nightingale songs to these roses;
\q1 For they themselves are saying to you in loud tones,
\q1 "O nightingale, hold your peace, and listen to us!"
\q1 Those two kinds of fair forms (ecstatic states and words)
\q1 Are undeniable proofs of a previous "union;"
\q1 Yea, those two kinds of exalted manifestations
\q1 Are the evident fruits of a preceding wedlock.
\q1 The ecstasy is past, but your members recall it;
\q1 Ask them about it, or call it to mind yourself.
\q1 When sorrow seizes you, if you are wise,
\q1 You will question that sorrow-fraught moment,
\q1 Saying to it, "O sorrow, who now deniest
\q1 Thy portion of bounty given thee by the Perfect One,
\q1 Even if each moment be not to thee a glad spring,
\q1 Yet of what is thy body, like a rose-heap, a storehouse?
\q1 Thy body is a heap of roses, thy thought rosewater;
\q1 'Twere strange if rosewater ignored the rose-heap!"
\v 13 \vp 6.1\vp* This sad Faqir too put up his cries for aid,
\q1 And bore off the ball of acceptance from the field.
\q1 But at times he distrusted the efficacy of his prayers,
\q1 On account of the delay in answering them.
\q1 Again, hope of the mercy of the Lord
\q1 Arose in his heart as an earnest of rejoicing.
\q1 When he was hopeless and ceasing to pray in weariness
\q1 He heard from God the word "Ascend!"
\q1 God is an Abaser and an Exalter;
\q1 Without these two processes nothing comes into being.
\q1 Behold the abasement of earth and uplifting of heaven;
\q1 Without these two heaven would not revolve, O man!
\q1 The abasement and exaltation of earth is otherwise,
\q1 Half the year is barren, half green and verdant.
\q1 The abasement and exaltation of weary time
\q1 Is otherwise again,— half day and half night.
\q1 The abasement and exaltation of this compound body
\q1 Is now health and now grievous sickness.
\q1 Know all the conditions of the world are in this wise,—
\q1 Drought, famine, peace, war, and trials.
\q1 This world flies, as it were, with these two wings;
\q1 Through these all souls are homes of hope and fear;
\q1 So that the world keeps trembling like leaves,
\q1 In the cold and hot winds of death and resurrection.
\q1 Till the jar of pure wine of our 'Isa (Unity)
\q1 Shall supersede the jar of many-coloured wine (plurality),
\q1 For that world (of unity) is as a saltpan;
\q1 Whatever enters it loses its varied hues.
\v 14 \vp 6.2\vp* Whereas the aim and will of the Merciful God
\q1 Inclined to the revelation and manifestation of Himself,
\q1 And one opposite cannot be shown but by its opposite,
\q1 And that Unique King of kings has no opposite or peer,
\q1 Therefore that Lord of the heart set up a Khalifa,
\q1 To serve as a mirror to reflect His own sovereignty.
\q1 Therefore He gave him unlimited purity and light,
\q1 And on the other side He set darkness opposing the light.
\q1 God set up two standards, a white and a black one,
\q1 The one Adam and the other Iblis;
\q1 And between these two mighty armies
\q1 Ensued war and battle and all we have witnessed.
\q1 Thus, too, in the second generation lived pure Abel;
\q1 Cain was the opposite of his pure light.
\q1 In like manner these two standards of right and wrong
\q1 Were borne aloft till the age of Nimrod.
\q1 Nimrod was the opponent and adversary of Abraham,
\q1 And their opposing camps warred and fought one another.
\q1 When God grew weary of the length of this war,
\q1 His fire was appointed to arbitrate between them.
\q1 He commanded fire and its flaming torment
\q1 To settle the matter in dispute between them.
\q1 Age after age these two parties contended,
\q1 Even till the time of Pharaoh and gentle Moses.
\q1 Between these two the war was waged for years,
\q1 And when it passed all bounds and affliction increased
\q1 God made the water of the Nile a judge between them,
\q1 That the one who deserved preeminence should endure.
\q1 In like manner it went on till the time of Mustafa
\q1 And Abu Jahl, that prince of iniquity.
\q1 Likewise did God ordain a punishment for the Thamud,
\q1 Namely, an earthquake which destroyed their lives.
\q1 Likewise a punishment for the Adites,
\q1 Namely, a swiftly rising and violent wind.
\q1 Likewise God ordained acute punishment for Qarun;
\q1 For the earth concealed wrath under its mildness,
\q1 Till all its mildness was converted into wrath,
\q1 And it swallowed up Qarun and his wealth in its depth.
\q1 So with the mouthful which nourishes your body
\q1 And wards off the darts of hunger like a cuirass,
\q1 When God instils wrath into this mouthful of bread,
\q1 That same bread will choke you like a halter.
\q1 This same garment which protects you from the cold,
\q1 God may give it the quality of intense cold,
\q1 So that this warm vest may become to your body
\q1 Cold as ice and biting as frost;
\q1 So that you will cast off these furs and silks,
\q1 And seek for a refuge from cold with cold itself.
\q1 You have only one eye, not two (for these two possibilities).
\q1 You have forgotten the story of the "shadowing cloud."
\q1 God's command came to city and village,
\q1 And to house and wall, saying, "Afford no shade!
\q1 Ward not off the pouring rain and the sun's heat;"
\q1 Till those men hasted to listen to the prophet Shu'aib,
\q1 Saying, 'O king, have pity; most of us are dead!'
\q1 But read the rest of the tale in the commentaries.
\q1 When that Omnipotent hand made the staff a serpent,
\q1 If you have reason, that portent should suffice.
\q1 You have sight indeed, but fail to mark carefully;
\q1 Your eyes are dimmed and closed with fat.
\v 15 \vp 6.3\vp* The Faqir was in this state when a divine voice came,
\q1 And God thus solved his difficulties,
\q1 Saying, "The voice told you to place an arrow on the bow,—
\q1 It did not bid you draw the bowstring to the utmost;
\q1 It did not bid you draw the bow with all your might;
\q1 It said, 'Adjust an arrow,' not 'Draw the bow fully.'
\q1 You elevated the bow to excess,
\q1 You magnified unduly the bowman's art,
\q1 Go! abandon this strong bowmanship,
\q1 Fix an arrow on the string, but make it not fly far.
\q1 When it falls, dig in that spot and search,
\q1 Abandon force and seek the treasure with humility."
\q1 God is "what is nearer to you than your neck-vein,"
\q1 You have cast the arrow of speculation afar off.
\q1 O you, who have made ready your bow and arrows,
\q1 The game is close to you, and you shoot too far off.
\q1 The further a man shoots, the further off he is,
\q1 And the more removed from the treasure he seeks.
\q1 The philosopher kills himself with thinking,
\q1 Tell him that his back is turned to that treasure;
\q1 Tell him that the more he runs to and fro,
\q1 The Almighty says, "Make efforts in our ways,"
\q1 Not "Make efforts away from us," O restless one.
\q1 Like Canaan, who went away, from shame to follow Noah,
\q1 Up to the top of that lofty mountain,
\q1 The more he sought safety on that mountain,
\q1 The further was he removed from the safe asylum.
\q1 So this Faqir, in search of that hidden treasure,
\q1 Day after day drew his bow stronger and stronger;
\q1 And the harder he drew his bow,
\q1 The further was he from the seat of that treasure.
\q1 This parable applies to all times,
\q1 For the soul of the ignorant is pledged to misfortune.
\q1 Because the ignorant man is ashamed of a master,
\q1 Perforce he goes and opens a new school for himself.
\q1 That school is higher than your true master, O beloved,
\q1 And hard of access, and full of scorpions and snakes.
\q1 Straightway overthrow it, and turn back again
\q1 To the green garden and sweet watered meadows.
\q1 Not like Canaan, who, through pride and ignorance,
\q1 Sought his ark of safety on a protecting mountain.
\q1 His far-shooting learning veiled his eyes,
\q1 While his heart's desire was all the while in his grasp.
\q1 Ah! oftentimes have learning and genius and wit
\q1 Proved to the traveller to be Ghouls and highwaymen!
\q1 "The majority of those in Paradise are the simple,"
\q1 Who have escaped the snares of philosophy.
\q1 Strip yourself bare of overweening intellect,
\q1 That grace may ever be shed upon you from above.
\q1 Cleverness is the opposite of humility and submission,
\q1 Quit cleverness, and consort with simple-mindedness!
\v 16 \vp 7.1\vp* The Musulman said, "O my friends,
\q1 My lord, the Prophet Muhammad, appeared to me
\q1 And said, 'The Jew has hurried to the top of Sinai,
\q1 And plays a game of love with God's interlocutor;
\q1 The Christian has been carried by 'Isa, Lord of bliss
\q1 Up to the summit of the fourth heaven;
\q1 Thou who art left behind and hast endured anguish,
\q1 Arise quickly and eat the sweetmeats and confections!
\q1 Those two clever and learned men have ascended,
\q1 And read their titles of dignity and exaltation;
\q1 Those two exalted ones have found exalted science,
\q1 And rivalled the very angels in intellect;
\q1 O humble and simple and despised one,
\q1 Arise and eat of the banquet of the divine sweets!"
\q1 They said to him, "Then you have been gluttonous;
\q1 Well indeed! you have eaten all the sweets!"
\q1 He answered, "When my sovereign lord commanded me,
\q1 Who am I that I should abstain from obeying?
\q1 Would you, O Jew, resist the commands of Moses
\q1 If he bade you do something, either pleasant or not?
\q1 Would you, O Christian, rebel against 'Isa's commands,
\q1 Whether those commands were agreeable or the reverse?
\q1 How could I rebel against the 'Glory of the prophets'?
\q1 Nay, I ate the sweets, and am now happy."
\q1 They replied, "By Allah, you have seen a true vision;
\q1 Your vision is better than a hundred like ours.
\q1 Your dream was seen by you when awake, O happy one,
\q1 For it was seen to be real by your being awake."
\q1 Quit excessive speculation and inordinate science,
\q1 'Tis service of God and good conduct that gains its end.
\q1 'Tis for this that God created us,
\q1 "We created not mankind save to worship us"
\q1 What profit did his science bring to Samiri?
\q1 His science excluded him from God's portals.
\q1 Consider what Qarun gained by his alchemy;
\q1 He was swallowed up in the depths of the earth.
\q1 Abu-l Jahl, again, what gained he from his wit
\q1 Save to be hurled head-foremost into hell for infidelity?
\q1 Know real science is seeing the fire directly,
\q1 Not mere talk, inferring the fire from the smoke.
\q1 Your scientific proofs are more offensive to the wise
\q1 Than the urine and breath whence a physician infers.
\q1 If these be your only proofs, O son,
\q1 Smell foul breath and inspect urine like physicians.
\q1 Such proofs are as the staff of a blind man,
\q1 Which prove only the blindness of the holder.
\q1 All your outcry and pompous claims and bustle
\q1 Only say, "I cannot see, hold me excused!"
\v 17 \vp 7.2\vp* He said, "Alms of mercy repel calamity,
\q1 Alms cure thy sickness, O son!
\q1 'Tis not charitable to burn up the poor,
\q1 Or to put out the eyes of the meek."
\q1 The prince replied, "Kindness is good in its place,
\q1 Provided you do kindness in its proper place.
\q1 If at chess you put the king in the rook's place
\q1 That is wrong; and so if you put the knight in the king's,
\q1 The law prescribes both rewards and chastisements.
\q1 The king's place is the throne, the horse's the gate.
\q1 What is justice but putting each in his place?
\q1 What injustice but putting each in what is not his place?
\q1 Nothing is vain of all that God has created,
\q1 Whether vengeance or mercy, or plain dealing or snares.
\q1 Not one of all these is good absolutely,
\q1 Nor is any one of them absolutely bad.
\q1 Each is harmful or beneficial according to its place,
\q1 Wherefore knowledge of these points is proper and useful.
\q1 Ah! many are the chastisements sent to the poor
\q1 Which are more beneficial to him than bread and sweets;
\q1 Because sweets out of season excite biliousness,
\q1 While blows make him pure from impurity.
\q1 Strike the poor man timely blows,
\q1 Which may save him from being beheaded later."
\v 18 \vp 7.3\vp* The two friends discussed the matter long,
\q1 And after discussion this plan was settled,
\q1 That they should fetch a long string,
\q1 By means of which to communicate with one another.
\q1 The mouse said, "One end must be tied to your leg,
\q1 And the other end to the leg of me, your double,
\q1 That by this contrivance we two may be united,
\q1 And be mingled together like soul and body."
\q1 Body is like a string tied to soul's foot,
\q1 That string drags soul down to earth.
\q1 The soul is the frog in the water of ecstatic bliss;
\q1 Escaping from the mouse of the body, it is in bliss.
\q1 The mouse of the body drags it back with that string;
\q1 Ah! what sorrow it tastes through being dragged back!
\q1 If it were not dragged down by that insolent mouse,
\q1 The frog would remain at peace in its water.
\q1 On the last day, when you shall awake from sleep,
\q1 You will learn the rest of this from the Sun of truth!
\v 19 \vp 7.4\vp* He who, when he had once seen a person at night,
\q1 Recognized him without fail when he saw him by day,
\q1 Saw the king upon the throne, and straightway cried,
\q1 "This was he who accompanied us on our nightly walk;
\q1 This is he whose beard possessed such rare talent;
\q1 Our arrest is due to his sagacity."
\q1 He added, "'Yea, he was with you,' this great king;
\q1 He beheld our actions and heard our secrets.
\q1 My eyes guided me to recognise that king at night,
\q1 And dwelt lovingly on his face, like the moon at night.
\q1 Now, therefore, I will implore his grace for myself,
\q1 For he will never avert his face from him that knew him."
\q1 Know the eye of the ' Knower is a safeguard in both worlds,
\q1 For therein ye will find a very Bahram to aid you.
\q1 For this cause Muhammad was the intercessor for faults,
\q1 Because his eye 'did not wander' from the King of kings.
\q1 In the night of this world, when the sun is hidden,
\q1 He beheld God, and placed his hopes on Him.
\q1 His eyes were anointed with the words, ' We opened thy heart,'
\q1 He beheld what Gabriel himself had not power to see."
\v 20 \vp 8.1\vp* When he recovered himself he said, "O God,
\q1 I have sinned in looking for aid to a creature!
\q1 Although the Prefect showed great liberality,
\q1 It was in no wise equal to Thy bounty.
\q1 He gave me a cap, but Thou my head full of sense;
\q1 He gave me a garment, but Thou my tall form.
\q1 He gave me gold, but Thou my hand which counts it;
\q1 He gave me a horse, but Thou my reason to guide it;
\q1 He gave me a lamp, but Thou my lucid eyes;
\q1 He gave me sweetmeats, but Thou my appetite for them;
\q1 He gave me a pension, but Thou my life and being;
\q1 His gift was gold, but Thine true blessings;
\q1 He gave me a house, but Thou heaven and earth;
\q1 In Thy house he and a hundred like him are nourished.
\q1 The gold was of Thy providing, he did not create it;
\q1 The bread of Thy providing, and furnished to him by Thee.
\q1 Thou also didst give him his liberality,
\q1 For thereby Thou didst augment his happiness.
\q1 I made him my Qibla, and directed my prayers to him;
\q1 I turned away my eyes from Thee, the Qibla-maker!
\q1 Where was he when the Supreme Dispenser of faith
\q1 Sowed reason in the water and clay of man,
\q1 And drew forth from Not-being this heavenly dome,
\q1 And spread out the carpet of the earth?
\q1 Of the stars He made torches to illumine the sky,
\q1 And of the four elements locks with keys (of reason).
\q1 Ah! many are the buildings visible and invisible
\q1 Which God has made between heaven's dome and earth.
\q1 Man is the astrolabe of those exalted attributes,
\q1 The attribute of man is to manifest God's signs.
\q1 Whatever is seen in man is the reflection of God,
\q1 Even as the reflection of the moon in water."
\q1 Say not two, know not two, call not on two!
\q1 Know the slave is obliterated in his lord!
\q1 So the lord is obliterated in God that created him;
\q1 Yea, lost and dead and buried in his Creator!
\q1 When you regard this lord as separate from God,
\q1 You annihilate at once text and paraphrase.
\q1 With eyes and heart look beyond mere water and clay,
\q1 God alone is the Qibla; regard not two Qiblas!
\q1 If you regard two you lose the benefit of both;
\q1 A spark falls on the tinder and the tinder vanishes!
\v 21 \vp 8.2\vp* In like manner Joseph, in the prison,
\q1 With humble and earnest supplications
\q1 Begged aid, saying, "When you are released,
\q1 And are occupied with your ministrations to the king,
\q1 Remember me, and entreat the king
\q1 To release me too from this prison."
\q1 How can one prisoner fettered in the snare
\q1 Procure release for a fellow prisoner?
\q1 The people of the world are all prisoners,
\q1 Awaiting death on the stake of annihilation;
\q1 Except one or two rare exceptions,
\q1 Whose bodies are in prison but their souls in heaven.
\q1 Afterwards, because Joseph had looked to man for aid,
\q1 He remained in prison for many years.
\q1 The Devil caused the man to forget Joseph,
\q1 And blotted Joseph's words from his remembrance;
\q1 And on account of this fault of that holy man
\q1 God left him in the prison for many years.
\v 22 \vp 9.1\vp* Be not intoxicated with these goblets of forms,
\q1 Lest you become a maker and worshipper of idols.
\q1 Pass by these cups full of forms, linger not;
\q1 There is wine in the cups, but it proceeds not from them.
\q1 Look to the Giver of the wine with open mouth;
\q1 When His wine comes, is not cup too small to hold it?
\q1 O Adam, seek the reality of my love,
\q1 Quit the mere husk and form of the wheat.
\q1 When sand was made meal for "The Friend of God,"
\q1 Know, O master, the form of wheat was dispensed with.
\q1 Form proceeds from the world that is without form,
\q1 Even as smoke arises from fire.
\q1 The Divine art without form designs forms (ideals),
\q1 Those forms fashion bodies with senses and instruments.
\q1 Whatever the form, it fashions in its own likeness
\q1 Those bodies either to good or to evil.
\q1 If the form be blessing, the man is thankful;
\q1 If it be suffering, he is patient;
\q1 If it be cherishing, he is cheerful;
\q1 If it be bruising, he is full of lamentation!
\q1 Since all these forms are slaves of Him without form,
\q1 Why do they deny their Lord and Master?
\q1 They exist only through Him that is without form;
\q1 What, then, means their disavowal of their Sustainer?
\q1 This very denial of Him proceeds from Him,
\q1 This act is naught but a reflection from Himself!
\q1 The forms of the walls and roofs of houses
\q1 Know to be shadows of the architect's thought;
\q1 Although stones and planks and bricks
\q1 Find no entrance into the sanctuary of thought,
\q1 Verily the Absolute Agent is without form,
\q1 Form is only a tool in His hands.
\q1 Sometimes that Formless One of His mercy
\q1 Shows His face to His forms from behind the veil of Not-being,
\q1 That every form may derive aid therefrom,—
\q1 From its perfect beauty and power.
\q1 Again, when that Formless One hides His face,
\q1 Those forms set forth their needs.
\q1 If one form sought perfection from another form,
\q1 That would be the height of error.
\q1 Why then, O simpleton, do you set forth your needs
\q1 To one who is as needy as yourself?
\q1 Since forms are slaves, apply them not to God,
\q1 Seek not to use a form as a similitude of God.
\q1 Seek Him with humbleness and self-abasement,
\q1 For thought yields naught but forms of thought.
\q1 Still, if you are unable to dispense with forms,
\q1 Those occurring independently of your thought are best.
\v 23 \vp 9.2\vp* "Now have we seen what the king saw at the first,
\q1 When that Incomparable One adjured us."
\q1 The prophets have many claims to our gratitude,
\q1 Because they forewarn us of our ultimate lot,
\q1 Saying, "What ye sow will yield only thorns;
\q1 If ye fly that way, ye will fly astray.
\q1 Take seed of us to yield you a good harvest,
\q1 Fly with our wings to hit the mark with your arrow.
\q1 Now ye know not the truth and nature of the 'Truth,'
\q1 But at the last ye will cry, 'That was the "Truth."'
\q1 The Truth is yourself, but not your mere bodily self,
\q1 Your real self is higher than 'you' and 'me.'
\q1 This visible 'you' which you fancy to be yourself
\q1 Is limited in place, the real 'you' is not limited.
\q1 Why, O pearl, linger you trembling in your shell?
\q1 Esteem not yourself mere sugar-cane, but real sugar.
\q1 This outward 'you' is foreign to your real ' you;'
\q1 Cling to your real self, quit this dual self.
\q1 Your last self attains to your first (real) self
\q1 Only through your attending earnestly to that union.
\q1 Your real self lies hid beneath your outward self,
\q1 For 'I am the servant of him who looks into himself.' "
\q1 "What a youth sees only when reflected in a glass,
\q1 Our wise old fathers saw long ago though hid in stones.
\q1 But we disobeyed the advice of our father,
\q1 And rebelled against his affectionate counsels.
\q1 We made light of the king's exhortations,
\q1 And slighted his matchless intimations.
\q1 Now we have all fallen into the ditch,
\q1 Wounded and crushed in this fatal struggle.
\q1 We relied on our own reason and discernment,
\q1 And for that cause have fallen into this calamity.
\q1 We fancied ourselves free from defects of sight,
\q1 Even as those affected by colour-blindness.
\q1 Now at last our hidden disease has been revealed,
\q1 After we have been involved in these calamities."
\q1 "The shadow of a guide is better than directions to God,
\q1 To be satisfied is better than a hundred nice dishes.
\q1 A seeing eye is better than a hundred walking-sticks,
\q1 Eye discerns jewels from mere pebbles."
\v 24 \vp 9.3\vp* They told their secrets to one another in dark sayings,
\q1 Speaking beneath their breath in fear and trembling.
\q1 None but God was privy to their secrets,
\q1 None but Heaven was partner in their sighs.
\q1 Yea, they used technical expressions one to another,
\q1 And possessed intelligence to extract the sense.
\q1 The vulgar learn the words of this "language of birds,"
\q1 And make boast of their mastery thereof;
\q1 But these words are only the outward form of the language,
\q1 The "raw" man is ignorant of the birds' meaning.
\q1 He is the true Solomon who knows the birds' language,
\q1 A demon, though he usurp his kingdom, is quite another.
\q1 The demon has taken upon him the form of Solomon,
\q1 His knowledge is fraud, not "what we have been taught."
\q1 When Solomon was blessed with inspiration from God,
\q1 He learned birds' language from "what we were taught."
\q1 But thou art only a bird of the air; understand then
\q1 That thou hast never seen the true spiritual birds!
\q1 The nest of the Simurgh is beyond Mount Qaf,
\q1 Not every thought can attain thereto;
\q1 Save thoughts which catch a glimpse thereof,
\q1 And after the vision are again shut off.
\q1 Yet not all shut off, rather intermitted for a wise end,
\q1 For the blessing abides, though shut off and hidden!
\q1 In order to preserve that body which is as a soul,
\q1 The Sun is veiled for a while behind a cloud;
\q1 In order not to melt that soul-like body,
\q1 The Sun withdraws itself as from ice.
\q1 For thy soul's sake seek counsel of these inspired ones.
\q1 Ah! rob not their words of their technical meanings!
\q1 Zulaikha applied to Yusuf the names of all things,
\q1 Beginning with wild rue and ending with frankincense.
\q1 She veiled his name under all other names,
\q1 And imparted her secret meaning to her confidants.
\q1 When she said, "The wax is melted by the fire,"
\q1 She meant, "My lover is wroth with me."
\q1 So when she said, "See, the moon is risen!"
\q1 Or, "Lo! the willow-bough is putting forth leaves;"
\q1 Or if she said, "The leaves quiver in the wind,"
\q1 Or, "The wild rue yields perfume as it burns;"
\q1 Or if she said, "The rose tells her tale to the Bulbul,"
\q1 Or, "The king sings his love-strain;"
\q1 Or if she said, "Ah! what a blessed lot!"
\q1 Or, "Who hath disturbed my heart's repose?"
\q1 Or if she said, "The water-carrier hath brought water,"
\q1 Or, "Lo! the sun emerges from the clouds;"
\q1 Or if she said, "Last night the victuals were boiled,"
\q1 Or, "The food was perfectly cooked;"
\q1 Or if she said, "My bread is without savour"
\q1 Or, "The heavens are revolving the wrong way;
\q1 Or if she said, "My head aches with pain,"
\q1 Or, "My headache is now relieved;—"
\q1 If she gave thanks, 'twas for being united to Yusuf;
\q1 If she wailed, 'twas that she was separated from him.
\q1 Though she gave vent to thousands of names,
\q1 Her meaning and purport was only Yusuf;
\q1 Was she an hungred, when she pronounced his name,
\q1 She became filled and cheered by his nourishment.
\q1 Her thirst was quenched by Yusuf's name,
\q1 His name was spiritual water to her soul.
\q1 Was she in pain, by pronouncing his mighty name
\q1 At once her pain was turned into joy.
\q1 In the cold it was a warm garment;
\q1 Her lover's name accomplished all this through love.
\q1 Strangers may pronounce the "pure name" of God,
\q1 Yet it effects no such marvels, for they lack love.
\q1 All that 'Isa accomplished by the name of Jehovah,
\q1 Zulaikha attained through the name "Yusuf."
\q1 When the soul is intimately united with God,
\q1 To name the one is the same as naming the other.
\q1 Zulaikha was empty of self and filled with love of Yusuf,
\q1 And there flowed out of her jar what it contained.
\q1 The scent of the saffron of union made her smile,
\q1 The stench of the onion of separation made her weep.
\q1 Each to have in his heart a hundred meanings,—
\q1 Such is not the creed of true love and devotion.
\q1 "The Friend" is to the lover as day to the sun,
\q1 The material sun is a veil over the face of the real day.
\q1 Whoso distinguishes not the veil from "The Friend's" face
\q1 Is a worshipper of the sun; of such an one beware!
\q1 "The Friend" is the real day, and daily food of lovers,
\q1 The heart and the heart's torment of His lovers.
\v 25 \vp 9.4\vp* Ah! many earnest suppliants wail forth prayers,
\q1 Till the smoke of their wailing rises to heaven;
\q1 Yea, the perfume of the incense of sinners' groans
\q1 Mounts up above the lofty roof of heaven.
\q1 Then the angels supplicate God, saying,
\q1 "O Thou that hearest prayer and relievest pain,
\q1 Thy faithful slave is bowing down before Thee.
\q1 He knows of none on whom to rely save Thee;
\q1 Thou bestowest favours on the helpless.
\q1 Every suppliant obtains his desire from Thee."
\q1 God makes answer, "The delay in granting his prayer
\q1 Is intended to benefit him, not to harm him.
\q1 His pressing need draws him from his negligence to me;
\q1 Yea, drags him by the hair into my courts.
\q1 If I at once remove his need he will go away,
\q1 And will be destroyed in his idle sports.
\q1 Though he is wailing with heartfelt cry of 'O Aider!'
\q1 Bid him wail on with broken heart and contrite breast.
\q1 His voice sounds sweet in my ears,
\q1 And his wailing and cries of' O God!'
\q1 In this way by supplication and lamentation
\q1 He prevails with me altogether."
\q1 It is on account of their sweet voices
\q1 That choice parrots and nightingales are prisoned in cages.
\q1 Ugly owls and crows are never prisoned in cages;
\q1 Such a thing was never heard of in history.
\q1 The disappointments of the pious, be sure,
\q1 Are appointed for this wise purpose.
\v 26 \vp 9.5\vp* In short, the King cherished him lovingly,
\q1 And he like a moon waned in that sun.
\q1 That waning of lovers makes them wax stronger,
\q1 Just as the moon waxes brighter after waning.
\q1 Ordinary sick persons crave a remedy for sickness,
\q1 But the lovesick one cries, "Increase my waning!
\q1 I have never tasted wine sweeter than this poison,
\q1 No health can be sweeter than this sickness!
\q1 No devotion is better than this sin (of love),
\q1 Years are as a moment compared to this moment!"
\q1 Long time he dwelt with the King in this manner,
\q1 With burning heart, as a lively sacrifice.
\q1 Thus his life passed, yet he gained not the union he wished.
\q1 Patient waiting consumed him, his soul could not bear it;
\q1 He dragged on life with pain and gnashing of teeth.
\q1 At last life ended before he had attained his desire.
\q1 The form of his earthly Beloved was hidden from him;
\q1 He departed, and found union with his Spiritual Beloved.
\q1 Then he said, "Though she lacks clothes of silk and wool,
\q1 'Tis sweeter to embrace her without those veils.
\q1 I have become naked of the body and its illusions,
\q1 I am admitted into the most intimate union."
\q1 The story admits of being told up to this point,
\q1 Bat what follows is hidden and inexpressible in words.
\q1 If you should speak and try a hundred ways to express it,
\q1 'Tis useless; the mystery becomes no clearer.
\q1 You can ride on saddle and horse up to the sea-coast,
\q1 But then you must use a horse of wood (i.e., a boat).
\q1 A horse of wood is useless on dry land,
\q1 It is the special conveyance of voyagers by sea.
\q1 Silence is this horse of wood,
\q1 Silence is the guide and support of men at sea.
\q1 This Silence which causes you annoyance
\q1 Is uttering cries of love audible to the spiritual.
\q1 You say, "How strange the spiritual man is silent!"
\q1 He answers, "How strange you have no ears!
\q1 Though I utter cries, you hear them not;
\q1 Sensual ears, however sharp, are deaf to my cries."
\q1 The spiritual man, as it were, cries in his sleep,
\q1 Uttering thousands of words of comfort;
\q1 While the carnal man at his side hears nothing at all,
\q1 For he is asleep, and deaf to the other's voice.
\q1 But the perfect spiritualist who has broken his boat
\q1 Plunges into the sea as a fish of the sea (of Truth).
\q1 He is then neither silent nor speaking, but a mystery.
\q1 No words are available to express his condition.
\q1 That marvellous one is in neither of these states;
\q1 'Twould be irreverent to explain his state more fully.
\q1 These illustrations are weak and inappropriate,
\q1 But no fitter ones are obtainable from sensible objects.
\v 27 \vp 9.6\vp* In short, the vengeance of That Jealous One (God)
\q1 After one year bore him to the grave.
\q1 When the King awoke out of his trance to consciousness,
\q1 His Mars-like eyes shed tears of blood.
\q1 When that incomparable one looked into his quiver,
\q1 He saw that one of his arrow-shafts was missing.
\q1 He cried to God, "What has become of my arrow?"
\q1 God answered, "Thy arrow is fixed in his throat!"
\q1 That King, bountiful as the sea, had pardoned him;
\q1 Nevertheless his arrow had dealt him a mortal wound.
\q1 He was slain, and cried out with his last breath,
\q1 "The King is all in all, my slayer and my saviour.
\q1 If he is not both these, he is not all in all;
\q1 Nay, he is both my slayer and my mourner!"
\q1 That expiring martyr also gave thanks,
\q1 That the King had smitten his body, not his spirit;
\q1 For the visible body must perforce perish,
\q1 Ere the spirit can live in happiness for evermore.
\q1 Though he incurred chastisement, it affected his body only,
\q1 And as a friend he now goes, free of pain, to his Friend.
\q1 Thus at first he clung to the King's stirrup,
\q1 But at last went his way guided by perfect sight.
\v 28 \vp 9.7\vp* Part of the story remains untold; it was retained
\q1 In his mind and was not disclosed.
\q1 The story of the princes remains unfinished,
\q1 The pearl of the third brother remains unstrung.
\q1 Here speech, like a camel, breaks down on its road;
\q1 I will say no more, but guard my tongue from speech.
\q1 The rest is told without aid of tongue
\q1 To the heart of him whose spirit is alive.
